  user_input {
    user_response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PRODUCTION-ORIENTED MASTER SPECIFICATION\n\nYou are helping me build a REAL, functional, production-oriented VPN and privacy platform called **Privacy Gateway**.\n\nThis is NOT a mockup, UI-only project, simulated VPN, fake dashboard, or academic-only prototype.\n\nThe long-term goal is to develop this into a real VPN/privacy service that users can actually connect to and use, comparable in product scope to commercial VPN services.\n\nHowever, development must happen incrementally. Do not pretend something is production-ready if it has not been implemented, tested, audited, or validated.\n\nEvery displayed metric must come from real data.\n\nEvery VPN connection must be a real WireGuard connection.\n\nEvery privacy detection feature must perform a real test.\n\nDo not use fake/random values.\n\n---\n\n# 1. PRODUCT\n\nProduct name:\n\n**Privacy Gateway**\n\nCore idea:\n\nA real WireGuard-based VPN service combined with a privacy-intelligence platform.\n\nThe VPN provides:\n\n* encrypted network transport\n* VPN IP address\n* secure routing\n* DNS protection\n* device authentication\n* VPN server management\n\nThe privacy-intelligence platform provides:\n\n* digital-footprint analysis\n* tracker detection\n* browser fingerprint analysis\n* DNS leak detection\n* WebRTC exposure detection\n* privacy scoring\n* privacy recommendations\n* real-time privacy events\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nNever claim that Privacy Gateway makes users completely anonymous or invisible.\n\nThe product should accurately communicate what is protected, what remains observable, and what privacy risks were detected.\n\n---\n\n# 2. REAL-TIME REQUIREMENT\n\nThe entire system must use real data and real-time state.\n\nExamples:\n\nVPN connects\n\342\206\222 backend detects the connection\n\342\206\222 desktop client updates\n\342\206\222 dashboard updates immediately.\n\nVPN disconnects\n\342\206\222 backend detects the change\n\342\206\222 dashboard updates immediately.\n\nNew WireGuard handshake\n\342\206\222 peer status updates.\n\nTracker detected\n\342\206\222 browser extension sends a real event\n\342\206\222 backend processes it\n\342\206\222 privacy dashboard updates.\n\nDNS leak detected\n\342\206\222 event is generated\n\342\206\222 user receives an alert.\n\nPrivacy score changes\n\342\206\222 dashboard updates without requiring a page refresh.\n\nUse WebSockets or another appropriate real-time mechanism.\n\nDo NOT simulate real-time behavior using fake timers or randomly generated data.\n\n---\n\n# 3. AUTHENTICATION\n\nImplement real authentication from the beginning.\n\nSupport:\n\n### Google Sign-In\n\nUse Google\'s official OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect authentication.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Google login\n* secure callback handling\n* verified identity\n* account creation\n* account linking\n* logout\n* session management\n* token validation\n* secure refresh/session strategy\n\nDo NOT implement Google login by simply trusting data sent from the browser.\n\nThe backend must validate authentication securely.\n\nDesign authentication so additional providers can be added later.\n\nPotential future providers:\n\n* Email/password\n* Apple\n* GitHub\n* Microsoft\n\n---\n\n# 4. USER ACCOUNTS\n\nEach authenticated user should have:\n\n* unique user ID\n* email\n* display name\n* profile information\n* account creation timestamp\n* authentication provider\n* subscription status\n* devices\n* VPN peers\n* privacy settings\n* security settings\n\nDo not collect unnecessary personal information.\n\n---\n\n# 5. DATABASE\n\nUse **PostgreSQL**.\n\nCreate a proper relational schema.\n\nAt minimum consider:\n\nusers\nauthentication_accounts\nsessions\ndevices\nwireguard_peers\nvpn_servers\nsubscriptions\nprivacy_scans\nprivacy_events\ntracker_events\nfingerprint_results\ndns_tests\nwebrtc_tests\nsecurity_events\naudit_logs\n\nDo not store browsing history by default.\n\nDo not store full URLs unless there is a clearly justified feature requiring them.\n\nUse data minimization.\n\nImplement:\n\n* migrations\n* indexes\n* foreign keys\n* constraints\n* timestamps\n* soft deletion where appropriate\n* retention policies\n\nNever store passwords in plaintext.\n\nNever store WireGuard private keys unnecessarily in the database.\n\n---\n\n# 6. VPN SERVER\n\nUse **WireGuard** as the VPN protocol.\n\nDo NOT create a custom VPN protocol.\n\nImplement a real Linux WireGuard server.\n\nRequired functionality:\n\n* server key management\n* peer creation\n* peer revocation\n* peer deletion\n* peer activation/deactivation\n* VPN IP allocation\n* configuration generation\n* peer status\n* latest handshake\n* RX/TX statistics\n* routing\n* IP forwarding\n* NAT\n* firewall configuration\n* DNS configuration\n\nThe backend must never expose private WireGuard keys through normal API responses.\n\nUse a narrowly scoped privileged component for operations that require elevated privileges.\n\nDo NOT give the entire web application unrestricted root access.\n\n---\n\n# 7. VPN CLIENTS\n\nThe long-term product should support:\n\n* Windows\n* macOS\n* Linux\n* Android\n* iOS\n\nFor the initial implementation, choose one desktop platform and build it properly.\n\nUse a cross-platform architecture such as:\n\nTauri + Rust + TypeScript\n\nThe desktop client should provide:\n\n* Google login\n* device registration\n* VPN server selection\n* connect\n* disconnect\n* reconnect\n* connection status\n* VPN IP\n* connection duration\n* server latency\n* data transferred\n* privacy score\n* privacy alerts\n* DNS status\n* WebRTC status\n* tracker statistics\n\nThe desktop application must control a REAL WireGuard tunnel.\n\nDo not simulate connection states.\n\n---\n\n# 8. BROWSER EXTENSION\n\nBuild a real browser extension using:\n\nTypeScript + WebExtension APIs.\n\nInitial target:\n\nChrome/Chromium.\n\nDesign for future Firefox support.\n\nThe extension should provide real:\n\n### Tracker detection\n\nDetect requests associated with:\n\n* advertising trackers\n* analytics\n* social tracking\n* fingerprinting infrastructure\n* known tracking domains\n\nUse maintained privacy/tracker lists rather than only a handful of hardcoded domains.\n\n### Fingerprint analysis\n\nAnalyze browser-exposed fingerprint surfaces where browser APIs permit:\n\n* User-Agent\n* language\n* timezone\n* screen properties\n* Canvas\n* WebGL\n* relevant browser characteristics\n\nReturn an estimated uniqueness/risk result.\n\nDo NOT claim to identify the user.\n\n### WebRTC testing\n\nPerform real tests where supported.\n\nDistinguish:\n\n* public IP\n* VPN IP\n* local/private IP\n* exposed addresses\n\nDo not falsely label normal behavior as a leak.\n\n---\n\n# 9. PRIVACY INTELLIGENCE ENGINE\n\nCreate a dedicated privacy-analysis service.\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n* process privacy signals\n* normalize results\n* calculate risk\n* create events\n* calculate privacy score\n* generate recommendations\n\nKeep this separate from the WireGuard networking code.\n\n---\n\n# 10. PRIVACY SCORE\n\nCreate an explainable scoring system.\n\nExample categories:\n\nNetwork Privacy\nBrowser Privacy\nTracking Exposure\nLeak Exposure\nFingerprint Risk\n\nExample:\n\nPrivacy Score: 82/100\n\nNetwork Privacy: 95\nTracking Exposure: 76\nFingerprint Risk: 70\nLeak Protection: 100\n\nEvery score must have an explanation.\n\nNever generate random scores.\n\nNever present an estimate as a certainty.\n\n---\n\n# 11. TRACKER INTELLIGENCE\n\nCreate a tracker intelligence system using reputable maintained lists.\n\nClassify trackers into categories such as:\n\n* advertising\n* analytics\n* social\n* fingerprinting\n* telemetry\n\nFor every detected tracker, where technically possible record:\n\n* domain\n* category\n* page origin\n* timestamp\n* action taken\n\nAvoid storing unnecessary browsing history.\n\nProvide configurable retention.\n\n---\n\n# 12. DNS LEAK PROTECTION\n\nImplement real DNS leak detection.\n\nThe system should verify:\n\n* DNS configuration\n* VPN DNS path\n* observed DNS resolver\n* whether DNS bypasses the VPN\n\nProvide:\n\nSAFE\nWARNING\nLEAK DETECTED\n\nwith an explanation.\n\nWhere appropriate, configure the VPN to use a controlled DNS resolver.\n\n---\n\n# 13. DESKTOP PRIVACY CENTER\n\nThe desktop application should provide a Privacy Center.\n\nShow real-time:\n\nVPN:\nCONNECTED / DISCONNECTED\n\nVPN IP: <real value>\n\nDNS:\nSAFE / WARNING / LEAK\n\nWebRTC:\nSAFE / EXPOSED\n\nTRACKERS: <real count>\n\nFINGERPRINT:\nLOW / MEDIUM / HIGH\n\nPRIVACY SCORE: <calculated score>\n\nSECURITY ALERTS: <real events>\n\n---\n\n# 14. WEB ADMIN DASHBOARD\n\nCreate a secure web dashboard for account/device/server administration.\n\nUser dashboard:\n\n* account\n* devices\n* VPN status\n* servers\n* privacy score\n* privacy events\n* tracker statistics\n* leak tests\n* fingerprint results\n\nAdmin dashboard:\n\n* users\n* devices\n* VPN servers\n* WireGuard peers\n* server health\n* connection statistics\n* security events\n* audit logs\n\nStrictly separate user permissions from administrator permissions.\n\n---\n\n# 15. BILLING / SUBSCRIPTIONS\n\nDesign the architecture so the service can eventually support paid plans.\n\nCreate a subscription abstraction.\n\nPotential plans:\n\nFREE\nPREMIUM\nBUSINESS\n\nDo not hardcode payment provider logic throughout the application.\n\nCreate a payment-provider interface so Stripe or another provider can be integrated later.\n\nDo not mark a subscription as paid based only on frontend input.\n\nPayment status must be verified server-side.\n\n---\n\n# 16. VPN SERVER INFRASTRUCTURE\n\nThe architecture must support multiple VPN servers.\n\nExample:\n\nVPN Server\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 India\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Singapore\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Germany\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 United States\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 United Kingdom\n\nUsers should eventually be able to select a server.\n\nThe backend should know:\n\n* server location\n* server health\n* latency\n* capacity\n* active peers\n* availability\n\nDo not expose unnecessary server infrastructure details to ordinary users.\n\n---\n\n# 17. DOCKER\n\nThe development and initial deployment environment must be Dockerized.\n\nUse Docker Compose initially.\n\nSeparate services logically:\n\nfrontend\nbackend\ndatabase\nprivacy-engine\nwireguard\nmonitoring\n\nUse:\n\n* non-root containers where possible\n* minimal images\n* pinned dependencies\n* health checks\n* resource limits where appropriate\n* isolated networks\n* secrets management\n\nDo not put sensitive production secrets directly into source code.\n\n---\n\n# 18. OBSERVABILITY\n\nUse appropriate monitoring.\n\nPotential stack:\n\nPrometheus\nGrafana\nstructured application logs\n\nMonitor:\n\n* CPU\n* memory\n* disk\n* network\n* WireGuard peers\n* handshake status\n* RX/TX\n* API health\n* database health\n* privacy engine health\n\nOperational monitoring must not become unnecessary user surveillance.\n\n---\n\n# 19. SECURITY\n\nTreat this as a security-sensitive production system.\n\nImplement:\n\n* TLS\n* secure authentication\n* authorization\n* RBAC\n* rate limiting\n* CSRF protection where applicable\n* CORS restrictions\n* secure headers\n* input validation\n* output validation\n* secret management\n* audit logging\n* dependency scanning\n* container security\n* firewall rules\n* least privilege\n* secure key management\n\nCreate a threat model covering:\n\n* malicious users\n* compromised devices\n* compromised VPN server\n* malicious administrator\n* database compromise\n* API attacks\n* credential theft\n* WireGuard key theft\n* browser extension compromise\n* supply-chain attacks\n\nClearly document what the system can and cannot protect against.\n\n---\n\n# 20. PRIVACY ARCHITECTURE\n\nPrivacy is a core product requirement.\n\nBy default:\n\nDO NOT store:\n\n* browsing history\n* full URLs\n* DNS query history\n* page contents\n* private keys\n* passwords\n\nunless explicitly required and justified.\n\nPrefer aggregate/anonymized metrics.\n\nImplement configurable:\n\n* data retention\n* event deletion\n* account deletion\n* privacy settings\n\nProvide users with a clear explanation of what data is collected.\n\nThe VPN operator should not automatically be able to inspect a user\'s browsing history.\n\n---\n\n# 21. PRODUCTION READINESS\n\nThe long-term goal is to operate this as a real VPN service.\n\nTherefore design for:\n\n* multiple servers\n* high availability\n* backups\n* disaster recovery\n* key rotation\n* server provisioning\n* server decommissioning\n* monitoring\n* alerting\n* abuse handling\n* account management\n* subscription management\n* capacity management\n* security incident response\n\nHowever, do not falsely claim production readiness before these areas are actually implemented and tested.\n\n---\n\n# 22. TESTING\n\nEvery major feature requires real tests.\n\nBackend:\n\n* unit tests\n* integration tests\n* authentication tests\n* authorization tests\n\nVPN:\n\n* real WireGuard connectivity\n* routing\n* NAT\n* DNS\n* peer lifecycle\n\nPrivacy:\n\n* DNS leak tests\n* WebRTC tests\n* tracker detection tests\n* fingerprint analysis tests\n* privacy scoring tests\n\nSecurity:\n\n* authentication attacks\n* authorization bypass tests\n* malformed requests\n* rate limiting\n* privilege boundary tests\n\nEnd-to-end:\n\nDesktop\n\342\206\222 Authentication\n\342\206\222 Backend\n\342\206\222 WireGuard\n\342\206\222 Internet\n\nBrowser Extension\n\342\206\222 Privacy API\n\342\206\222 Privacy Engine\n\342\206\222 Real-time Dashboard\n\n---\n\n# 23. DEVELOPMENT RULE\n\nDO NOT generate the complete application in one response.\n\nWork in phases.\n\nPhase 0:\nArchitecture and threat model\n\nPhase 1:\nRepository + Docker infrastructure + database\n\nPhase 2:\nAuthentication + Google OAuth\n\nPhase 3:\nReal WireGuard server\n\nPhase 4:\nBackend API\n\nPhase 5:\nDesktop client\n\nPhase 6:\nBrowser extension\n\nPhase 7:\nTracker intelligence\n\nPhase 8:\nFingerprint analysis\n\nPhase 9:\nDNS/WebRTC leak detection\n\nPhase 10:\nPrivacy scoring\n\nPhase 11:\nReal-time WebSocket architecture\n\nPhase 12:\nMulti-server architecture\n\nPhase 13:\nSubscription/billing architecture\n\nPhase 14:\nSecurity hardening\n\nPhase 15:\nIntegration and end-to-end testing\n\nPhase 16:\nProduction deployment\n\nAfter each phase report:\n\n1. What was implemented\n2. Files changed\n3. Architecture changes\n4. Database changes\n5. Security implications\n6. How to run\n7. How to test\n8. Known limitations\n9. What is NOT implemented\n10. Next recommended phase\n\n---\n\n# 24. NO FAKE FUNCTIONALITY\n\nThis rule is mandatory.\n\nNever:\n\n* fake VPN connections\n* fake peer status\n* fake tracker counts\n* generate random privacy scores\n* fake DNS leak results\n* fake WebRTC results\n* hardcode \"Connected\"\n* pretend an unimplemented security feature works\n\nIf something is not implemented, explicitly say:\n\nNOT IMPLEMENTED\n\nIf something is only a development approximation, explicitly say:\n\nDEVELOPMENT IMPLEMENTATION \342\200\224 NOT PRODUCTION READY\n\n---\n\n# 25. FIRST RESPONSE\n\nDo NOT generate application code yet.\n\nFirst give me:\n\n1. Complete architecture\n2. System components\n3. Data-flow diagram\n4. Network-flow diagram\n5. Repository structure\n6. Docker architecture\n7. Database schema\n8. Authentication architecture\n9. Google OAuth flow\n10. WireGuard architecture\n11. Desktop architecture\n12. Browser extension architecture\n13. Privacy engine architecture\n14. Real-time architecture\n15. Multi-server architecture\n16. Security architecture\n17. Threat model\n18. Privacy/data-retention model\n19. Production deployment strategy\n20. Development roadmap\n21. Major technical risks\n22. Estimated complexity of each component\n\nDo not start coding until this architecture is reviewed and approved.\n\nThe goal is to build a REAL Privacy Gateway that can eventually become a genuine VPN service, not a fake demonstration.\n"
    items {
      text: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PRODUCTION-ORIENTED MASTER SPECIFICATION\n\nYou are helping me build a REAL, functional, production-oriented VPN and privacy platform called **Privacy Gateway**.\n\nThis is NOT a mockup, UI-only project, simulated VPN, fake dashboard, or academic-only prototype.\n\nThe long-term goal is to develop this into a real VPN/privacy service that users can actually connect to and use, comparable in product scope to commercial VPN services.\n\nHowever, development must happen incrementally. Do not pretend something is production-ready if it has not been implemented, tested, audited, or validated.\n\nEvery displayed metric must come from real data.\n\nEvery VPN connection must be a real WireGuard connection.\n\nEvery privacy detection feature must perform a real test.\n\nDo not use fake/random values.\n\n---\n\n# 1. PRODUCT\n\nProduct name:\n\n**Privacy Gateway**\n\nCore idea:\n\nA real WireGuard-based VPN service combined with a privacy-intelligence platform.\n\nThe VPN provides:\n\n* encrypted network transport\n* VPN IP address\n* secure routing\n* DNS protection\n* device authentication\n* VPN server management\n\nThe privacy-intelligence platform provides:\n\n* digital-footprint analysis\n* tracker detection\n* browser fingerprint analysis\n* DNS leak detection\n* WebRTC exposure detection\n* privacy scoring\n* privacy recommendations\n* real-time privacy events\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nNever claim that Privacy Gateway makes users completely anonymous or invisible.\n\nThe product should accurately communicate what is protected, what remains observable, and what privacy risks were detected.\n\n---\n\n# 2. REAL-TIME REQUIREMENT\n\nThe entire system must use real data and real-time state.\n\nExamples:\n\nVPN connects\n\342\206\222 backend detects the connection\n\342\206\222 desktop client updates\n\342\206\222 dashboard updates immediately.\n\nVPN disconnects\n\342\206\222 backend detects the change\n\342\206\222 dashboard updates immediately.\n\nNew WireGuard handshake\n\342\206\222 peer status updates.\n\nTracker detected\n\342\206\222 browser extension sends a real event\n\342\206\222 backend processes it\n\342\206\222 privacy dashboard updates.\n\nDNS leak detected\n\342\206\222 event is generated\n\342\206\222 user receives an alert.\n\nPrivacy score changes\n\342\206\222 dashboard updates without requiring a page refresh.\n\nUse WebSockets or another appropriate real-time mechanism.\n\nDo NOT simulate real-time behavior using fake timers or randomly generated data.\n\n---\n\n# 3. AUTHENTICATION\n\nImplement real authentication from the beginning.\n\nSupport:\n\n### Google Sign-In\n\nUse Google\'s official OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect authentication.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Google login\n* secure callback handling\n* verified identity\n* account creation\n* account linking\n* logout\n* session management\n* token validation\n* secure refresh/session strategy\n\nDo NOT implement Google login by simply trusting data sent from the browser.\n\nThe backend must validate authentication securely.\n\nDesign authentication so additional providers can be added later.\n\nPotential future providers:\n\n* Email/password\n* Apple\n* GitHub\n* Microsoft\n\n---\n\n# 4. USER ACCOUNTS\n\nEach authenticated user should have:\n\n* unique user ID\n* email\n* display name\n* profile information\n* account creation timestamp\n* authentication provider\n* subscription status\n* devices\n* VPN peers\n* privacy settings\n* security settings\n\nDo not collect unnecessary personal information.\n\n---\n\n# 5. DATABASE\n\nUse **PostgreSQL**.\n\nCreate a proper relational schema.\n\nAt minimum consider:\n\nusers\nauthentication_accounts\nsessions\ndevices\nwireguard_peers\nvpn_servers\nsubscriptions\nprivacy_scans\nprivacy_events\ntracker_events\nfingerprint_results\ndns_tests\nwebrtc_tests\nsecurity_events\naudit_logs\n\nDo not store browsing history by default.\n\nDo not store full URLs unless there is a clearly justified feature requiring them.\n\nUse data minimization.\n\nImplement:\n\n* migrations\n* indexes\n* foreign keys\n* constraints\n* timestamps\n* soft deletion where appropriate\n* retention policies\n\nNever store passwords in plaintext.\n\nNever store WireGuard private keys unnecessarily in the database.\n\n---\n\n# 6. VPN SERVER\n\nUse **WireGuard** as the VPN protocol.\n\nDo NOT create a custom VPN protocol.\n\nImplement a real Linux WireGuard server.\n\nRequired functionality:\n\n* server key management\n* peer creation\n* peer revocation\n* peer deletion\n* peer activation/deactivation\n* VPN IP allocation\n* configuration generation\n* peer status\n* latest handshake\n* RX/TX statistics\n* routing\n* IP forwarding\n* NAT\n* firewall configuration\n* DNS configuration\n\nThe backend must never expose private WireGuard keys through normal API responses.\n\nUse a narrowly scoped privileged component for operations that require elevated privileges.\n\nDo NOT give the entire web application unrestricted root access.\n\n---\n\n# 7. VPN CLIENTS\n\nThe long-term product should support:\n\n* Windows\n* macOS\n* Linux\n* Android\n* iOS\n\nFor the initial implementation, choose one desktop platform and build it properly.\n\nUse a cross-platform architecture such as:\n\nTauri + Rust + TypeScript\n\nThe desktop client should provide:\n\n* Google login\n* device registration\n* VPN server selection\n* connect\n* disconnect\n* reconnect\n* connection status\n* VPN IP\n* connection duration\n* server latency\n* data transferred\n* privacy score\n* privacy alerts\n* DNS status\n* WebRTC status\n* tracker statistics\n\nThe desktop application must control a REAL WireGuard tunnel.\n\nDo not simulate connection states.\n\n---\n\n# 8. BROWSER EXTENSION\n\nBuild a real browser extension using:\n\nTypeScript + WebExtension APIs.\n\nInitial target:\n\nChrome/Chromium.\n\nDesign for future Firefox support.\n\nThe extension should provide real:\n\n### Tracker detection\n\nDetect requests associated with:\n\n* advertising trackers\n* analytics\n* social tracking\n* fingerprinting infrastructure\n* known tracking domains\n\nUse maintained privacy/tracker lists rather than only a handful of hardcoded domains.\n\n### Fingerprint analysis\n\nAnalyze browser-exposed fingerprint surfaces where browser APIs permit:\n\n* User-Agent\n* language\n* timezone\n* screen properties\n* Canvas\n* WebGL\n* relevant browser characteristics\n\nReturn an estimated uniqueness/risk result.\n\nDo NOT claim to identify the user.\n\n### WebRTC testing\n\nPerform real tests where supported.\n\nDistinguish:\n\n* public IP\n* VPN IP\n* local/private IP\n* exposed addresses\n\nDo not falsely label normal behavior as a leak.\n\n---\n\n# 9. PRIVACY INTELLIGENCE ENGINE\n\nCreate a dedicated privacy-analysis service.\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n* process privacy signals\n* normalize results\n* calculate risk\n* create events\n* calculate privacy score\n* generate recommendations\n\nKeep this separate from the WireGuard networking code.\n\n---\n\n# 10. PRIVACY SCORE\n\nCreate an explainable scoring system.\n\nExample categories:\n\nNetwork Privacy\nBrowser Privacy\nTracking Exposure\nLeak Exposure\nFingerprint Risk\n\nExample:\n\nPrivacy Score: 82/100\n\nNetwork Privacy: 95\nTracking Exposure: 76\nFingerprint Risk: 70\nLeak Protection: 100\n\nEvery score must have an explanation.\n\nNever generate random scores.\n\nNever present an estimate as a certainty.\n\n---\n\n# 11. TRACKER INTELLIGENCE\n\nCreate a tracker intelligence system using reputable maintained lists.\n\nClassify trackers into categories such as:\n\n* advertising\n* analytics\n* social\n* fingerprinting\n* telemetry\n\nFor every detected tracker, where technically possible record:\n\n* domain\n* category\n* page origin\n* timestamp\n* action taken\n\nAvoid storing unnecessary browsing history.\n\nProvide configurable retention.\n\n---\n\n# 12. DNS LEAK PROTECTION\n\nImplement real DNS leak detection.\n\nThe system should verify:\n\n* DNS configuration\n* VPN DNS path\n* observed DNS resolver\n* whether DNS bypasses the VPN\n\nProvide:\n\nSAFE\nWARNING\nLEAK DETECTED\n\nwith an explanation.\n\nWhere appropriate, configure the VPN to use a controlled DNS resolver.\n\n---\n\n# 13. DESKTOP PRIVACY CENTER\n\nThe desktop application should provide a Privacy Center.\n\nShow real-time:\n\nVPN:\nCONNECTED / DISCONNECTED\n\nVPN IP: <real value>\n\nDNS:\nSAFE / WARNING / LEAK\n\nWebRTC:\nSAFE / EXPOSED\n\nTRACKERS: <real count>\n\nFINGERPRINT:\nLOW / MEDIUM / HIGH\n\nPRIVACY SCORE: <calculated score>\n\nSECURITY ALERTS: <real events>\n\n---\n\n# 14. WEB ADMIN DASHBOARD\n\nCreate a secure web dashboard for account/device/server administration.\n\nUser dashboard:\n\n* account\n* devices\n* VPN status\n* servers\n* privacy score\n* privacy events\n* tracker statistics\n* leak tests\n* fingerprint results\n\nAdmin dashboard:\n\n* users\n* devices\n* VPN servers\n* WireGuard peers\n* server health\n* connection statistics\n* security events\n* audit logs\n\nStrictly separate user permissions from administrator permissions.\n\n---\n\n# 15. BILLING / SUBSCRIPTIONS\n\nDesign the architecture so the service can eventually support paid plans.\n\nCreate a subscription abstraction.\n\nPotential plans:\n\nFREE\nPREMIUM\nBUSINESS\n\nDo not hardcode payment provider logic throughout the application.\n\nCreate a payment-provider interface so Stripe or another provider can be integrated later.\n\nDo not mark a subscription as paid based only on frontend input.\n\nPayment status must be verified server-side.\n\n---\n\n# 16. VPN SERVER INFRASTRUCTURE\n\nThe architecture must support multiple VPN servers.\n\nExample:\n\nVPN Server\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 India\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Singapore\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Germany\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 United States\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 United Kingdom\n\nUsers should eventually be able to select a server.\n\nThe backend should know:\n\n* server location\n* server health\n* latency\n* capacity\n* active peers\n* availability\n\nDo not expose unnecessary server infrastructure details to ordinary users.\n\n---\n\n# 17. DOCKER\n\nThe development and initial deployment environment must be Dockerized.\n\nUse Docker Compose initially.\n\nSeparate services logically:\n\nfrontend\nbackend\ndatabase\nprivacy-engine\nwireguard\nmonitoring\n\nUse:\n\n* non-root containers where possible\n* minimal images\n* pinned dependencies\n* health checks\n* resource limits where appropriate\n* isolated networks\n* secrets management\n\nDo not put sensitive production secrets directly into source code.\n\n---\n\n# 18. OBSERVABILITY\n\nUse appropriate monitoring.\n\nPotential stack:\n\nPrometheus\nGrafana\nstructured application logs\n\nMonitor:\n\n* CPU\n* memory\n* disk\n* network\n* WireGuard peers\n* handshake status\n* RX/TX\n* API health\n* database health\n* privacy engine health\n\nOperational monitoring must not become unnecessary user surveillance.\n\n---\n\n# 19. SECURITY\n\nTreat this as a security-sensitive production system.\n\nImplement:\n\n* TLS\n* secure authentication\n* authorization\n* RBAC\n* rate limiting\n* CSRF protection where applicable\n* CORS restrictions\n* secure headers\n* input validation\n* output validation\n* secret management\n* audit logging\n* dependency scanning\n* container security\n* firewall rules\n* least privilege\n* secure key management\n\nCreate a threat model covering:\n\n* malicious users\n* compromised devices\n* compromised VPN server\n* malicious administrator\n* database compromise\n* API attacks\n* credential theft\n* WireGuard key theft\n* browser extension compromise\n* supply-chain attacks\n\nClearly document what the system can and cannot protect against.\n\n---\n\n# 20. PRIVACY ARCHITECTURE\n\nPrivacy is a core product requirement.\n\nBy default:\n\nDO NOT store:\n\n* browsing history\n* full URLs\n* DNS query history\n* page contents\n* private keys\n* passwords\n\nunless explicitly required and justified.\n\nPrefer aggregate/anonymized metrics.\n\nImplement configurable:\n\n* data retention\n* event deletion\n* account deletion\n* privacy settings\n\nProvide users with a clear explanation of what data is collected.\n\nThe VPN operator should not automatically be able to inspect a user\'s browsing history.\n\n---\n\n# 21. PRODUCTION READINESS\n\nThe long-term goal is to operate this as a real VPN service.\n\nTherefore design for:\n\n* multiple servers\n* high availability\n* backups\n* disaster recovery\n* key rotation\n* server provisioning\n* server decommissioning\n* monitoring\n* alerting\n* abuse handling\n* account management\n* subscription management\n* capacity management\n* security incident response\n\nHowever, do not falsely claim production readiness before these areas are actually implemented and tested.\n\n---\n\n# 22. TESTING\n\nEvery major feature requires real tests.\n\nBackend:\n\n* unit tests\n* integration tests\n* authentication tests\n* authorization tests\n\nVPN:\n\n* real WireGuard connectivity\n* routing\n* NAT\n* DNS\n* peer lifecycle\n\nPrivacy:\n\n* DNS leak tests\n* WebRTC tests\n* tracker detection tests\n* fingerprint analysis tests\n* privacy scoring tests\n\nSecurity:\n\n* authentication attacks\n* authorization bypass tests\n* malformed requests\n* rate limiting\n* privilege boundary tests\n\nEnd-to-end:\n\nDesktop\n\342\206\222 Authentication\n\342\206\222 Backend\n\342\206\222 WireGuard\n\342\206\222 Internet\n\nBrowser Extension\n\342\206\222 Privacy API\n\342\206\222 Privacy Engine\n\342\206\222 Real-time Dashboard\n\n---\n\n# 23. DEVELOPMENT RULE\n\nDO NOT generate the complete application in one response.\n\nWork in phases.\n\nPhase 0:\nArchitecture and threat model\n\nPhase 1:\nRepository + Docker infrastructure + database\n\nPhase 2:\nAuthentication + Google OAuth\n\nPhase 3:\nReal WireGuard server\n\nPhase 4:\nBackend API\n\nPhase 5:\nDesktop client\n\nPhase 6:\nBrowser extension\n\nPhase 7:\nTracker intelligence\n\nPhase 8:\nFingerprint analysis\n\nPhase 9:\nDNS/WebRTC leak detection\n\nPhase 10:\nPrivacy scoring\n\nPhase 11:\nReal-time WebSocket architecture\n\nPhase 12:\nMulti-server architecture\n\nPhase 13:\nSubscription/billing architecture\n\nPhase 14:\nSecurity hardening\n\nPhase 15:\nIntegration and end-to-end testing\n\nPhase 16:\nProduction deployment\n\nAfter each phase report:\n\n1. What was implemented\n2. Files changed\n3. Architecture changes\n4. Database changes\n5. Security implications\n6. How to run\n7. How to test\n8. Known limitations\n9. What is NOT implemented\n10. Next recommended phase\n\n---\n\n# 24. NO FAKE FUNCTIONALITY\n\nThis rule is mandatory.\n\nNever:\n\n* fake VPN connections\n* fake peer status\n* fake tracker counts\n* generate random privacy scores\n* fake DNS leak results\n* fake WebRTC results\n* hardcode \"Connected\"\n* pretend an unimplemented security feature works\n\nIf something is not implemented, explicitly say:\n\nNOT IMPLEMENTED\n\nIf something is only a development approximation, explicitly say:\n\nDEVELOPMENT IMPLEMENTATION \342\200\224 NOT PRODUCTION READY\n\n---\n\n# 25. FIRST RESPONSE\n\nDo NOT generate application code yet.\n\nFirst give me:\n\n1. Complete architecture\n2. System components\n3. Data-flow diagram\n4. Network-flow diagram\n5. Repository structure\n6. Docker architecture\n7. Database schema\n8. Authentication architecture\n9. Google OAuth flow\n10. WireGuard architecture\n11. Desktop architecture\n12. Browser extension architecture\n13. Privacy engine architecture\n14. Real-time architecture\n15. Multi-server architecture\n16. Security architecture\n17. Threat model\n18. Privacy/data-retention model\n19. Production deployment strategy\n20. Development roadmap\n21. Major technical risks\n22. Estimated complexity of each component\n\nDo not start coding until this architecture is reviewed and approved.\n\nThe goal is to build a REAL Privacy Gateway that can eventually become a genuine VPN service, not a fake demonstration.\n"
    }
  }
}
