  planner_response {
    response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 0: SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & MASTER SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Complete System Architecture\n\nPrivacy Gateway is engineered as a decoupled, dual-plane architecture comprising a **Cryptographic Network Plane (WireGuard)** and an **Asynchronous Privacy Intelligence Plane**.\n\n```\n                           +-------------------------------------------------------------+\n                           |                    CLIENT ECOSYSTEM                         |\n                           |  +---------------------------+  +------------------------+  |\n                           |  |      Desktop Client       |  |   Browser Extension    |  |\n                           |  |  (Tauri/Rust Core + GUI)  |  |  (Manifest V3 / WEXT)  |  |\n                           |  +-------------+-------------+  +-----------+------------+  |\n                           +----------------|----------------------------|---------------+\n                                            |                            |\n                     Encrypted WG Tunnel    | HTTPS / WSS / gRPC         | HTTPS / WSS Events\n                     (UDP / Kernel WireGuard)|                            |\n                                            v                            v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                    GATEWAY & INGRESS LAYER                                         |\n|                                                                                                    |\n|   +------------------------------------+          +--------------------------------------------+   |\n|   |         WireGuard Edge Node        |          |            API Gateway / Reverse Proxy     |   |\n|   |   (Kernel wg0 / nftables / CoreDNS)|          |                 (Nginx / Envoy)            |   |\n|   +-----------------+------------------+          +---------------------+----------------------+   |\n+---------------------|---------------------------------------------------|--------------------------+\n                      |                                                   |\n                      | Metrics & Telemetry                               | Auth & API Traffic\n                      v                                                   v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                      CONTROL PLANE SERVICES                                        |\n|                                                                                                    |\n|   +-----------------------+     +-----------------------+     +--------------------------------+   |\n|   |   Node Control Daemon |     |   Core API Service    |     |    Privacy Intelligence Engine |   |\n|   |  (wgctrl / Netlink)   |     |    (Auth, Sub, API)   |     | (Trackers, Scoring, Leaks, FP) |   |\n|   +-----------+-----------+     +-----------+-----------+     +---------------+----------------+   |\n|               |                             |                                 |                    |\n|               +-----------------------------+---------------------------------+                    |\n|                                             |                                                      |\n|                                             v                                                      |\n|                              +------------------------------+                                      |\n|                              |   Event Broker & Bus (Redis) |                                      |\n|                              |  (Pub/Sub, Stream Queue, WS) |                                      |\n|                              +--------------+---------------+                                      |\n|                                             |                                                      |\n|                                             v                                                      |\n|                              +------------------------------+                                      |\n|                              |   Persistent Storage (PGSQL) |                                      |\n|                              |  (TimescaleDB / PostgreSQL)  |                                      |\n|                              +------------------------------+                                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. System Components\n\n### A. Client Tier\n1. **Desktop Client (`pg-desktop`)**:\n   - **Engine**: Tauri v2 + Rust Core with native kernel interface bindings (`wireguard-control` / `wintun` on Windows, `wireguard-go` or `wg-quick` on macOS, Linux native kernel `netlink`).\n   - **Frontend UI**: React + Tailwind CSS client running within the Tauri webview.\n   - **Responsibilities**: Device authentication, local WireGuard keypair generation (never uploads private key), tunnel lifecycle management, DNS routing protection, killswitch (firewall rules via `pfctl`/`iptables`/`wfp`), and local diagnostic probes.\n2. **Browser Privacy Extension (`pg-extension`)**:\n   - **Engine**: WebExtension Manifest V3 (Chromium + Firefox Gecko).\n   - **Responsibilities**: Declarative request analysis (`declarativeNetRequest` + `webRequest`), heuristic third-party tracker mapping, local Canvas/WebGL/Font fingerprint entropy assessment, and active WebRTC IP binding inspection.\n3. **Web Dashboard & Admin Center (`pg-web`)**:\n   - **Engine**: Single-Page App + Server-Side Dashboard for subscription billing, active device/peer management, privacy diagnostic drill-downs, and infrastructure node health metrics.\n\n### B. Gateway & Edge Tier\n1. **WireGuard VPN Node (`pg-vpn-node`)**:\n   - High-throughput Linux edge instance running WireGuard kernel module (`wireguard.ko`), `nftables` for secure IPv4/IPv6 NAT masquerading and peer isolation, and a custom `CoreDNS` resolver instance that applies sinkholes and anti-leak routing.\n2. **Node Daemon (`pg-node-daemon`)**:\n   - Unprivileged Go/Rust sidecar daemon running on each edge node with scoped root/CAP_NET_ADMIN capabilities to read `wg show` statistics via netlink, add/remove peers dynamically, and publish keepalive metrics to the central bus.\n3. **Synthetic Leak Testing Prober (`pg-leak-probe`)**:\n   - Authoritative DNS name server (`*.leak.privacygateway.net`) capable of generating single-use cryptographically signed subdomains (`<token>.leak.privacygateway.net`) to detect upstream DNS resolver leaks.\n\n### C. Core & Analytics Tier\n1. **Core API Server (`pg-api`)**:\n   - Node.js/TypeScript (or Go) backend service handling OAuth 2.0 / OIDC user lifecycle, device authentication sessions, WireGuard public key registration, server topology distribution, and RBAC admin actions.\n2. **Privacy Intelligence Engine (`pg-privacy-engine`)**:\n   - Streaming analytics pipeline ingesting sanitized client telemetry and test payloads, calculating the 5-pillar explainable Privacy Score, and generating actionable remediations.\n3. **Real-time Event Broker (`pg-redis`)**:\n   - In-memory Redis Pub/Sub cluster + Redis Streams for real-time WebSocket fan-out, peer handshake monitoring, and live telemetry updates.\n4. **Relational Database (`pg-db`)**:\n   - PostgreSQL 16 relational database maintaining strict referential integrity, row-level security (RLS), and zero unhashed credential/key storage.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Data-Flow Diagram\n\n```\n+----------------+      +-------------------+      +------------------+      +-------------------+\n| Desktop Client |      | Browser Extension |      | Core API Service |      | Privacy Engine    |\n+-------+--------+      +---------+---------+      +--------+---------+      +---------+---------+\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        | 1. Generate Keypair     |                         |                          |\n        |    (PubKey, PrivKey)    |                         |                          |\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        | 2. POST /api/v1/peers   |                         |                          |\n        |    { pubkey, device }   |                         |                          |\n        |-------------------------------------------------->|                          |\n        |                         |                         | 3. Allocate VIP & Node   |\n        | 4. Return WG Config     |                         |    Store in PostgreSQL   |\n        |    { vip, endpoint, pk }|                         |                          |\n        |<--------------------------------------------------|                          |\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        | 5. Initialize WG Tunnel |                         |                          |\n        |    Handshake to Node    |                         |                          |\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        |                         | 6. Intercept Request    |                          |\n        |                         |    Match Disconnect List|                          |\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        |                         | 7. POST /api/v1/telemetry                          |\n        |                         |    { tracker_hash, cat }|                          |\n        |                         |--------------------------------------------------->|\n        |                         |                         |                          | 8. Re-evaluate\n        |                         |                         |                          |    Privacy Score\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        | 9. WS Event (Real-time update)                    |                          |\n        |<-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n        |    { score: 87, active_trackers: 14, wg_rx_tx: \"12MB/4MB\" }                  |\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Network-Flow Diagram\n\n```\n               [ User Computer / OS Network Stack ]\n                                |\n               +----------------v----------------+\n               |   OS Routing Table Default GW   |\n               | (Redirect all to wg0 interface) |\n               +----------------+----------------+\n                                |\n             +------------------+------------------+\n             |                                     |\n   [ Encrypted WG Tunnel ]                [ Split Local DNS / Probe ]\n             |                                     |\n             v                                     v\n   +--------------------+               +---------------------+\n   | Edge WireGuard VIP |               | Synthetic Subdomain |\n   |  UDP Port 51820    |               |  Authoritative DNS  |\n   +---------+----------+               +----------+----------+\n             |                                     |\n    [ nftables NAT PostRouting ]                   |\n             |                                     |\n   +---------v----------+                          |\n   | CoreDNS (Local WG) |                          |\n   | (10.66.0.1:53)     |                          |\n   +---------+----------+                          |\n             |                                     |\n             +-----------------+-------------------+\n                               |\n                               v\n                     [ Public Internet ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Repository Structure\n\nA monorepo structure designed with strict module boundaries:\n\n```\nprivacy-gateway/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 .github/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 workflows/              # CI/CD: build, test, lint, container scanning\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docker/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docker-compose.yml      # Local dev environment\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docker-compose.prod.yml # Production multi-service deployment\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api/Dockerfile\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 privacy-engine/Dockerfile\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 wireguard/Dockerfile\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 coredns/Dockerfile\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 packages/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 shared-types/           # TypeScript interfaces & DTOs shared across API & UI\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 crypto-utils/           # WireGuard Curve25519 & token validation utilities\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tracker-db/             # Processed tracker domain definitions & blocklists\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 apps/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api/                    # Core REST + WebSocket Server (Node.js/Express/TypeScript)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/         # Environment & security config\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/    # Route controllers\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/     # Auth, RateLimit, RBAC, SecurityHeaders\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/         # Express router endpoints\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/       # WireGuard, OAuth, Device, Billing services\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 ws/             # WebSocket connection & real-time dispatchers\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 privacy-engine/         # Dedicated scoring, fingerprint & leak analyzer\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 analyzers/      # Fingerprint, DNS, WebRTC, Tracker analyzers\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 scoring/        # Deterministic 5-pillar privacy scoring math\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.ts\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 web-dashboard/          # Production React Dashboard (SPA / SSR)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 components/     # High-contrast, accessibility-first UI components\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 hooks/          # Real-time WebSocket & data hooks\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 pages/          # User Dashboard, Admin Center, Diagnostics\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 desktop-client/         # Tauri v2 Desktop Application\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src-tauri/          # Rust Core (Native WFP / iptables / wg-control)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/                # Client React UI\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 browser-extension/      # Chromium & Firefox Manifest V3 Extension\n\342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202       \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 background/     # DeclarativeNetRequest & event listeners\n\342\224\202       \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 content/        # In-page fingerprint & WebRTC probers\n\342\224\202       \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 popup/          # Extension popup UI\n\342\224\202       \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 manifest.json\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 infrastructure/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 migrations/             # Database SQL schema migrations\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 wireguard/              # Linux edge server startup scripts & nftables rules\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 coredns/                # Corefile & blocklist configuration\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tsconfig.base.json\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Docker Architecture\n\nThe local development and edge node setup orchestrates 6 containerized services with minimal privilege execution:\n\n```yaml\nversion: \'3.9\'\n\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\n    container_name: pg_database\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_DB: privacy_gateway\n      POSTGRES_USER: ${DB_USER:-pgadmin}\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD:-pgsecret}\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n      - ../infrastructure/migrations:/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U ${DB_USER:-pgadmin} -d privacy_gateway\"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7-alpine\n    container_name: pg_redis\n    command: [\"redis-server\", \"--appendonly\", \"yes\", \"--requirepass\", \"${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}\"]\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"redis-cli\", \"-a\", \"${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}\", \"ping\"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  api:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/api/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_api\n    depends_on:\n      postgres:\n        condition: service_healthy\n      redis:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    environment:\n      NODE_ENV: production\n      PORT: 3000\n      DATABASE_URL: postgresql://${DB_USER:-pgadmin}:${DB_PASSWORD:-pgsecret}@postgres:5432/privacy_gateway\n      REDIS_URL: redis://:${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}@redis:6379\n      GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID: ${GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID}\n      GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET: ${GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET}\n      JWT_SECRET: ${JWT_SECRET}\n    ports:\n      - \"3000:3000\"\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n      - pg-public-net\n\n  privacy-engine:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/privacy-engine/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_privacy_engine\n    depends_on:\n      - redis\n      - postgres\n    environment:\n      REDIS_URL: redis://:${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}@redis:6379\n      DATABASE_URL: postgresql://${DB_USER:-pgadmin}:${DB_PASSWORD:-pgsecret}@postgres:5432/privacy_gateway\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n\n  wireguard-node:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/wireguard/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_wireguard\n    cap_add:\n      - NET_ADMIN\n      - SYS_MODULE\n    sysctls:\n      - net.ipv4.ip_forward=1\n      - net.ipv4.conf.all.src_valid_mark=1\n    ports:\n      - \"51820:51820/udp\"\n    volumes:\n      - /lib/modules:/lib/modules:ro\n      - wg-config:/etc/wireguard\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n      - pg-vpn-net\n\n  coredns:\n    image: coredns/coredns:latest\n    container_name: pg_coredns\n    command: [\"-conf\", \"/etc/coredns/Corefile\"]\n    volumes:\n      - ../infrastructure/coredns/Corefile:/etc/coredns/Corefile:ro\n    networks:\n      - pg-vpn-net\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\n  wg-config:\n\nnetworks:\n  pg-internal-net:\n    internal: true\n  pg-public-net:\n  pg-vpn-net:\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Relational Database Schema (PostgreSQL DDL)\n\n```sql\n-- Extensions\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS \"uuid-ossp\";\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS \"pgcrypto\";\n\n-- Enums\nCREATE TYPE user_role AS ENUM (\'user\', \'admin\', \'auditor\');\nCREATE TYPE auth_provider AS ENUM (\'google\', \'email_password\', \'github\', \'apple\');\nCREATE TYPE subscription_tier AS ENUM (\'free\', \'premium\', \'business\');\nCREATE TYPE subscription_status AS ENUM (\'active\', \'past_due\', \'canceled\', \'trialing\');\nCREATE TYPE peer_status AS ENUM (\'active\', \'revoked\', \'dormant\');\nCREATE TYPE leak_status AS ENUM (\'safe\', \'warning\', \'leak_detected\');\nCREATE TYPE risk_level AS ENUM (\'low\', \'medium\', \'high\');\n\n-- 1. Users Table\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    display_name VARCHAR(100),\n    avatar_url TEXT,\n    role user_role DEFAULT \'user\' NOT NULL,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 2. Authentication Accounts (OIDC / OAuth identities)\nCREATE TABLE authentication_accounts (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    provider auth_provider NOT NULL,\n    provider_subject_id VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    email_at_provider VARCHAR(255),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    UNIQUE(provider, provider_subject_id)\n);\n\n-- 3. User Sessions\nCREATE TABLE user_sessions (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    refresh_token_hash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    user_agent TEXT,\n    ip_address INET,\n    expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    revoked_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 4. Devices (Registered desktops/mobiles)\nCREATE TABLE devices (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    device_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL, -- \'windows\', \'macos\', \'linux\', \'android\', \'ios\'\n    client_version VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\n    last_active_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 5. VPN Server Infrastructure Nodes\nCREATE TABLE vpn_servers (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    node_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    country_code VARCHAR(2) NOT NULL, -- \'US\', \'DE\', \'SG\', \'IN\'\n    city VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    public_endpoint VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL, -- \'us-east.privacygateway.net:51820\'\n    wireguard_public_key VARCHAR(44) NOT NULL,\n    ipv4_subnet CIDR NOT NULL, -- e.g. \'10.66.1.0/24\'\n    ipv6_subnet CIDR,\n    capacity_peers INTEGER DEFAULT 250 NOT NULL,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE NOT NULL,\n    is_maintenance BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 6. WireGuard Peers\nCREATE TABLE wireguard_peers (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    vpn_server_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES vpn_servers(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,\n    public_key VARCHAR(44) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    allocated_ipv4 INET UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    allocated_ipv6 INET UNIQUE,\n    preshared_key_encrypted TEXT, -- Optional encrypted PSK for post-quantum resistance\n    status peer_status DEFAULT \'active\' NOT NULL,\n    rx_bytes BIGINT DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    tx_bytes BIGINT DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    last_handshake_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 7. Subscriptions & Billing Abstraction\nCREATE TABLE subscriptions (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    tier subscription_tier DEFAULT \'free\' NOT NULL,\n    status subscription_status DEFAULT \'active\' NOT NULL,\n    payment_provider VARCHAR(50), -- e.g. \'stripe\', \'paddle\'\n    external_subscription_id VARCHAR(255),\n    current_period_start TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    current_period_end TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    canceled_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 8. Privacy Scans & Scores (Aggregated Snapshot)\nCREATE TABLE privacy_scans (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    overall_score INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (overall_score BETWEEN 0 AND 100),\n    network_privacy_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    tracking_exposure_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    fingerprint_risk_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    leak_protection_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    dns_leak_status leak_status NOT NULL,\n    webrtc_leak_status leak_status NOT NULL,\n    trackers_blocked_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 9. Privacy Events (Granular Event Feed)\nCREATE TABLE privacy_events (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    event_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL, -- \'tracker_blocked\', \'dns_leak_detected\', \'new_handshake\'\n    severity VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL, -- \'info\', \'warning\', \'critical\'\n    description TEXT NOT NULL,\n    metadata JSONB DEFAULT \'{}\'::JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 10. Audit Logs (Security & Admin Activity)\nCREATE TABLE audit_logs (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    actor_user_id UUID REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    action VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    target_resource VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    target_id UUID,\n    ip_address INET,\n    metadata JSONB DEFAULT \'{}\'::JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Indexes for performance\nCREATE INDEX idx_user_sessions_lookup ON user_sessions(user_id, expires_at);\nCREATE INDEX idx_wg_peers_server ON wireguard_peers(vpn_server_id, status);\nCREATE INDEX idx_privacy_scans_user ON privacy_scans(user_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_privacy_events_user ON privacy_events(user_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_audit_logs_actor ON audit_logs(actor_user_id, created_at DESC);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Authentication Architecture\n\nThe authentication framework uses an **OpenID Connect (OIDC) Token Exchange Model** combined with short-lived JWT Access Tokens and cryptographically rotated Refresh Tokens.\n\n```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                    AUTHENTICATION MODEL                                        |\n|                                                                                                |\n|  [ Client (Desktop / Web) ]                                                                    |\n|             |                                                                                  |\n|             | 1. Initiates Google OAuth Code Flow with PKCE                                    |\n|             v                                                                                  |\n|  [ Google Identity Server (accounts.google.com) ]                                              |\n|             |                                                                                  |\n|             | 2. Grants Authorization Code & ID Token                                          |\n|             v                                                                                  |\n|  [ Client (Desktop / Web) ]                                                                    |\n|             |                                                                                  |\n|             | 3. POST /api/v1/auth/google/callback { code, code_verifier, redirect_uri }       |\n|             v                                                                                  |\n|  [ Privacy Gateway Core API (Server-Side Verification) ]                                       |\n|             |                                                                                  |\n|             | 4. Exchanging code directly with Google OAuth2 Token Endpoint (HTTPS Backend)    |\n|             | 5. Verifies Google ID Token Signature against Google\'s public certs (JWKS)        |\n|             | 6. Extracts & verifies { sub, email, email_verified }                             |\n|             | 7. Performs Upsert in `users` and `authentication_accounts`                      |\n|             | 8. Issues:                                                                       |\n|             |    - Access Token: RS256 JWT (Expiry: 15 minutes, stateless)                     |\n|             |    - Refresh Token: CSPRNG 256-bit hash (Expiry: 30 days, stored in `user_sessions`)|\n|             v                                                                                  |\n|  [ Client (Desktop / Web) ]                                                                    |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Google OAuth 2.0 Flow (PKCE Hardened)\n\n```\nClient (Desktop App)            Google Identity           Privacy Gateway Backend\n       |                               |                              |\n       | 1. Generate code_verifier     |                              |\n       |    & code_challenge (S256)    |                              |\n       |                               |                              |\n       | 2. Open Auth Browser / Loopback                              |\n       |    https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth              |\n       |------------------------------>|                              |\n       |                               |                              |\n       | 3. User approves sign-in      |                              |\n       |                               |                              |\n       | 4. Redirect with auth ?code=..|                              |\n       |<------------------------------|                              |\n       |                                                              |\n       | 5. POST /api/v1/auth/google/verify                           |\n       |    { code, code_verifier, redirect_uri }                     |\n       |------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                                              | 6. Backend exchanges code\n       |                                                              |    for tokens with Google\n       |                                                              |    via https://oauth2.googleapis.com\n       |                                                              | 7. Validate Google certs\n       |                                                              | 8. Ensure email_verified == true\n       |                                                              | 9. Mint PG Access/Refresh tokens\n       | 10. Return { access_token, refresh_token, user_profile }     |\n       |<-------------------------------------------------------------|\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. WireGuard Network & Server Architecture\n\n### A. Dynamic Peer Provisioning Protocol\n1. **Client-Side Key Generation**: The desktop client executes a secure curve25519 scalar multiplication locally using `x25519-dalek` / libsodium to derive a `PrivateKey` and `PublicKey`. The `PrivateKey` **never leaves the device**.\n2. **Registration API**: Client sends `POST /api/v1/vpn/peers` payload `{ public_key, server_id, device_id }` with its JWT Bearer token.\n3. **Subnet IP Allocation**: The API assigns the next available unique IP within the target node\'s CIDR (e.g. `10.66.1.42/32`).\n4. **Node Synchronization**: The API pushes peer configuration to the edge node daemon over TLS gRPC/UNIX socket:\n   ```bash\n   wg set wg0 peer <CLIENT_PUBLIC_KEY> allowed-ips 10.66.1.42/32\n   ```\n5. **Config Output**: Client receives the connection profile:\n   ```ini\n   [Interface]\n   PrivateKey = <CLIENT_LOCAL_PRIVATE_KEY>\n   Address = 10.66.1.42/32\n   DNS = 10.66.0.1\n\n   [Peer]\n   PublicKey = <VPN_NODE_PUBLIC_KEY>\n   Endpoint = us-east.privacygateway.net:51820\n   AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0, ::/0\n   PersistentKeepalive = 25\n   ```\n\n### B. Linux Edge Node Firewall & NAT (`nftables`)\n```nftables\ntable inet filter {\n    chain input {\n        type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;\n        iif \"lo\" accept\n        ct state established,related accept\n        ip protocol icmp accept\n        ip6 nexthdr icmpv6 accept\n        tcp dport 22 accept # Restricted to Bastion SSH\n        udp dport 51820 accept # WireGuard Ingress\n    }\n    chain forward {\n        type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;\n        iif \"wg0\" oif \"eth0\" accept\n        iif \"eth0\" oif \"wg0\" ct state established,related accept\n    }\n}\ntable ip nat {\n    chain postrouting {\n        type nat hook postrouting priority 100; policy accept;\n        oif \"eth0\" masquerade\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Desktop Architecture (Tauri / Rust)\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         TAURI DESKTOP CLIENT                       |\n|                                                                    |\n|  +--------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  |                 Frontend Webview (React + TS)                |  |\n|  |   - Privacy Dashboard, Server Selector, Realtime Bandwidth    |  |\n|  |   - Diagnostic Leak Prober UI, Alert Toast Notifications     |  |\n|  +------------------------------+-------------------------------+  |\n|                                 | IPC (Tauri Invoke Commands)       |\n|  +------------------------------v-------------------------------+  |\n|  |                     Tauri Rust Core Engine                   |  |\n|  |                                                              |  |\n|  |  +---------------------+  +-------------------------------+  |  |\n|  |  | Auth & Token Store  |  | WireGuard Controller          |  |  |\n|  |  | (OS Keyring / Vault)|  | (WFP / Netlink / Wintun)       |  |  |\n|  |  +---------------------+  +---------------+---------------+  |  |\n|  |                                           |                  |  |\n|  |  +---------------------+  +---------------v---------------+  |  |\n|  |  | System Killswitch   |  | Traffic Diagnostics Worker    |  |  |\n|  |  | (Blocks non-WG out) |  | (Latency, RX/TX, Handshake)   |  |  |\n|  |  +---------------------+  +-------------------------------+  |  |\n|  +--------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Local Secret Isolation**: Refresh tokens and WireGuard private keys reside in the OS Keychain/SecretService via the `keyring` Rust crate.\n- **Privilege Separation**: Tunnel activation is handled by a lightweight privileged helper daemon or `setuid`/`WFP` service on Windows/Linux/macOS, preventing the GUI from needing root permissions.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Browser Extension Architecture (Manifest V3)\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    BROWSER EXTENSION (MANIFEST V3)                 |\n|                                                                    |\n|  +-----------------------+           +--------------------------+  |\n|  |     Popup / Panel     |           |   In-Page Content Script |  |\n|  | (Score, Active Blocks)|           | (Canvas/WebGL/WebRTC)    |  |\n|  +-----------+-----------+           +------------+-------------+  |\n|              |                                    |                |\n|              +-----------------+------------------+                |\n|                                | Message Passing                   |\n|              +-----------------v------------------+                |\n|              |      Service Worker (Background)   |                |\n|              |                                    |                |\n|              |  +------------------------------+  |                |\n|              |  | declarativeNetRequest Rules  |  |                |\n|              |  | (Blocks Advertising/Trackers)|  |                |\n|              |  +------------------------------+  |                |\n|              |  +------------------------------+  |                |\n|              |  | Event Aggregator & Telemetry |  |                |\n|              |  | (Hashes domain, posts to API)|  |                |\n|              |  +------------------------------+  |                |\n|              +------------------------------------+                |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n1. **Tracker Identification**: Uses parsed public blocklists (EasyPrivacy, Peter Lowe, Disconnect) compiled into efficient binary Bloom filters / DNR rulesets.\n2. **Fingerprint Surface Detection**: Measures local entropy of `navigator.userAgent`, screen color depths, Canvas 2D rendered hash deviations, and audio context buffer anomalies.\n3. **WebRTC Prober**: Executes an in-memory `RTCPeerConnection` ice candidate test, checks whether raw local/public IPs leak past the interface, and marks status as `SAFE` or `EXPOSED`.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Privacy Intelligence Engine\n\nThe Privacy Engine evaluates 4 orthogonal privacy vectors to compute an overall 100-point index:\n\n```\n                                  +---------------------------------------+\n                                  |       PRIVACY INTELLIGENCE ENGINE     |\n                                  +-------------------+-------------------+\n                                                      |\n                 +--------------------+---------------+--------------------+---------------------+\n                 |                    |                                    |                     |\n                 v                    v                                    v                     v\n       +------------------+  +--------------------+              +-------------------+  +-------------------+\n       | Network Privacy  |  | Tracking Exposure  |              | Fingerprint Risk  |  |  Leak Protection  |\n       |     (Weight 30%) |  |    (Weight 25%)    |              |   (Weight 20%)    |  |   (Weight 25%)    |\n       +--------+---------+  +---------+----------+              +---------+---------+  +---------+---------+\n                |                      |                                   |                      |\n                v                      v                                   v                      v\n        - WG Tunnel Active?    - Trackers Blocked / Visited        - Canvas Entropy       - DNS Leak Clean?\n        - Encrypted DNS?       - Third-Party Cookie Policy         - User-Agent Uniqueness- WebRTC Binding Safe?\n        - VPN Server Trust?    - Telemetry Requests                - AudioContext Entropy - IPv6 Leak Safe?\n```\n\n### Deterministic Scoring Formula:\n$$\\text{PrivacyScore} = (0.30 \\times S_{\\text{network}}) + (0.25 \\times S_{\\text{tracking}}) + (0.20 \\times S_{\\text{fingerprint}}) + (0.25 \\times S_{\\text{leak}})$$\n\nEvery category is explainable down to exact deduction reasons (e.g. *\"-15 points: WebSockets leaking ISP DNS address 198.51.100.1\"*, *\"-10 points: Canvas fingerprint uniqueness matches >99.4% of web cohort\"*).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Real-Time Architecture (WebSocket & Event Stream)\n\n```\n[ Edge VPN Node ]            [ Browser Extension ]         [ Desktop Client ]\n       |                              |                            |\n       | Handshake & RX/TX metrics    | Tracker Block Event        | Diagnostic Result\n       v                              v                            v\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       API & PRIVACY INGESTION PIPELINE                          |\n+---------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n                                        |\n                                        v\n                       +---------------------------------+\n                       | Redis Pub/Sub (`pg:events:user`) |\n                       +----------------+----------------+\n                                        |\n                                        v\n                       +---------------------------------+\n                       | WebSocket Gateway (Socket.IO/ws)|\n                       +----------------+----------------+\n                                        |\n                +-----------------------+------------------------+\n                |                                                |\n                v                                                v\n    [ Web Dashboard Client ]                         [ Desktop Client GUI ]\n    (Real-time State Mutation)                       (Real-time State Mutation)\n```\n\n- **Protocol**: Standard WebSocket with heartbeat ping/pong every 25 seconds.\n- **Payload Format**: Standardized Envelope `{ event: \"TELEMETRY_UPDATE\" | \"PEER_HANDSHAKE\" | \"LEAK_ALERT\", payload: { ... }, timestamp: 1771720000 }`.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Multi-Server Node Topology\n\n```\n                                +---------------------------+\n                                |  Central API / Dispatcher |\n                                +-------------+-------------+\n                                              |\n                     +------------------------+------------------------+\n                     |                        |                        |\n                     v                        v                        v\n            +-----------------+      +-----------------+      +-----------------+\n            |   Node: US-East |      |   Node: EU-West |      |  Node: AP-South |\n            | (Virginia, USA) |      | (Frankfurt, DE) |      | (Singapore, SG) |\n            +--------+--------+      +--------+--------+      +--------+--------+\n                     |                        |                        |\n                     |                        |                        |\n             Capacity: 250 peers      Capacity: 250 peers      Capacity: 250 peers\n             Subnet: 10.66.1.0/24     Subnet: 10.66.2.0/24     Subnet: 10.66.3.0/24\n```\n\n1. **Topology Distribution**: Client queries `GET /api/v1/vpn/servers` to receive live list of active nodes, geolocations, latency metrics, and real-time load percentages.\n2. **Graceful Failover**: If a node goes to maintenance mode (`is_maintenance = true`), the control plane notifies connected clients via WebSocket to automatically transition to the nearest available server without dropping authentication sessions.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Security Architecture\n\n1. **Defense in Depth**:\n   - Zero open listening ports on backend internal networks; only port 443 (HTTPS) and port 51820 (WireGuard UDP) are publicly exposed.\n2. **Cryptographic Primitives**:\n   - Authentication: JWT signed with RS256 (RSA 4096-bit keypair) or EdDSA.\n   - Tunnel: WireGuard (Curve25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305, BLAKE2s, SipHash24).\n   - Secret Storage: PBKDF2 / Argon2id with random salt for all sensitive tokens.\n3. **Privilege Boundaries**:\n   - The web app runs as unprivileged user `node` / `appuser` (UID 10001).\n   - WireGuard management commands are executed exclusively via a dedicated Go/Rust daemon over a restricted local UNIX socket requiring `CAP_NET_ADMIN`.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Threat Model (STRIDE)\n\n| Threat Category | Potential Attack Vector | System Countermeasure & Mitigation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Spoofing** | Rogue client attempts to claim another user\'s allocated VIP. | WireGuard cryptographic binding: `AllowedIPs` on server matches only the registered client public key. Packets with forged source IPs are dropped by the kernel. |\n| **Tampering** | In-transit tampering of DNS queries to induce leaks. | All DNS traffic within the tunnel is routed strictly to internal `CoreDNS` (10.66.0.1:53) with DNSSEC validation and encrypted DoH upstream. |\n| **Repudiation** | Malicious administrator deletes or modifies audit trails. | Write-only, append-only PostgreSQL `audit_logs` table with non-superuser grants and immutable timestamps. |\n| **Information Disclosure** | Memory dump of API server revealing user private keys. | Zero-Knowledge Key Principle: Private keys are generated on client devices and **never transmitted to or stored on servers**. |\n| **Denial of Service** | Volumetric SYN flood or UDP flood against API / WG node. | Kernel-level `syncookies`, WireGuard built-in Cookie mechanism (BLAKE2s MACs against DoS), and rate-limiting middleware (Redis token bucket). |\n| **Elevation of Privilege** | Container escape from API server to host network stack. | Container isolation: rootless containers, Read-Only root filesystem, `no-new-privileges:true`, and dropped Linux capabilities. |\n\n---\n\n## 18. Privacy & Data-Retention Model\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        ZERO BROWSING LOGS GUARANTEE                               |\n|                                                                                   |\n|  [ WHAT WE NEVER RECORD OR LOG ]                                                  |\n|  x Full URLs or HTTP/HTTPS request bodies                                         |\n|  x DNS query histories or destination IPs                                         |\n|  x Unencrypted user traffic or payload contents                                   |\n|  x WireGuard Private Keys (Never touch server)                                    |\n|                                                                                   |\n|  [ WHAT IS MINIMALLY RETAINED & CONFIGURABLE ]                                    |\n|  \342\234\223 Account metadata (User ID, Email, Role, Subscription)                         |\n|  \342\234\223 WireGuard Public Key & Allocated VIP (Active lifetime only)                    |\n|  \342\234\223 Aggregated Bandwidth (Total RX/TX bytes for capacity planning)                 |\n|  \342\234\223 Diagnostic Test Snapshots (User-controlled retention: 24h / 7d / 30d / Purge)  |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 19. Production Deployment Strategy\n\n1. **CI/CD Pipeline**:\n   - GitHub Actions automated matrix testing: unit tests, linter, static security analysis (Trivy, Semgrep), and Docker container builds.\n2. **Infrastructure as Code (IaC)**:\n   - Terraform / OpenTofu provisioning of edge cloud compute instances with pinned Ubuntu 24.04 LTS kernel across geographical regions.\n3. **Rolling Updates**:\n   - Zero-downtime blue/green deployment for the Central API and WebSocket cluster.\n   - WireGuard servers updated node-by-node with automated drainage of active peers.\n\n---\n\n## 20. Development Roadmap\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 0: Master Architecture Specification & Security Blueprint (COMPLETED)       |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 1: Repository Structure, Docker Orchestration & PostgreSQL Schema Setup     |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 2: OpenID Connect & Google OAuth Authentication Engine (Secure PKCE)        |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 3: Real WireGuard Server Node Controller, IP Allocation & Netlink Daemon    |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 4: Core REST & WebSocket Real-time Telemetry API                            |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 5: Tauri Desktop Client Engine (Cross-Platform Tunnel & Killswitch)         |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 6: Browser Privacy Extension (Declarative Tracker Blocking & Heuristics)    |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 7: Deterministic Privacy Intelligence Scoring Engine & Leak Detection Probe |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 8: Production Web Management Dashboard & Admin Operations Center            |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 9: End-to-End Integration, Automated Test Suites & Security Hardening       |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 21. Major Technical Risks & Mitigations\n\n1. **Kernel vs. Userland WireGuard Compatibility**:\n   - *Risk*: Varying client OS permissions may restrict kernel WireGuard driver installation.\n   - *Mitigation*: Fallback to high-performance userland implementation (`wireguard-go` / `wintun`) if kernel module access is denied.\n2. **WebRTC & IPv6 Leak Vectors**:\n   - *Risk*: Browsers bypassing standard routing tables through local STUN socket queries.\n   - *Mitigation*: Client-side firewall rules (WFP/iptables) enforcing that all non-WG outbound UDP traffic is dropped while connected (Killswitch).\n3. **State Desynchronization in Edge Nodes**:\n   - *Risk*: Network partition causing disparity between Central API database and WireGuard node peers.\n   - *Mitigation*: Node daemon executes a periodic state reconciliation loop querying the API to synchronize active peer public keys every 60 seconds.\n\n---\n\n## 22. Estimated Complexity of Components\n\n| Component | Complexity Tier | Primary Technical Challenges |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Core Database & API** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Strict RBAC, session lifecycle, token rotation, clean entity models. |\n| **Google OAuth / PKCE** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Handling both desktop deep-linking/loopback and web redirects securely. |\n| **WireGuard Node Engine** | **High** (4.5/5) | Kernel netlink integration, `nftables` isolation, dynamic IP pool management. |\n| **Tauri Desktop Client** | **High** (4.5/5) | OS-level network stack manipulation, routing table overriding, killswitch rules. |\n| **Browser Extension** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Manifest V3 declarative rule compilation, accurate Canvas/WebRTC entropy probing. |\n| **Privacy Intelligence Engine** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Deterministic mathematical scoring model, explainable risk breakdown. |\n| **Real-time Bus (WS/Redis)** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Reliable pub/sub delivery across multi-device client sessions. |\n\n---\n\n### Architecture Review Confirmation\n\nThis comprehensive Master Architecture establishes the engineering foundation for **Privacy Gateway**. Every metric, connection, and test is grounded in verifiable cryptographic, network, and heuristic primitives.\n\nPlease review and confirm this architectural blueprint so we may proceed to **Phase 1: Repository Structure, Docker Orchestration, and PostgreSQL Database Migration**."
    thinking: "**Drafting the Blueprint**\n\nI\'m now drafting the comprehensive Phase 0 Master Architecture Blueprint. I\'m focusing on covering all 22 required areas meticulously and exhaustively, ensuring no details are omitted. The initial task is to create a complete, robust architecture foundation for the project.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 0: SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & MASTER SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Complete System Architecture\n\nPrivacy Gateway is engineered as a decoupled, dual-plane architecture comprising a **Cryptographic Network Plane (WireGuard)** and an **Asynchronous Privacy Intelligence Plane**.\n\n```\n                           +-------------------------------------------------------------+\n                           |                    CLIENT ECOSYSTEM                         |\n                           |  +---------------------------+  +------------------------+  |\n                           |  |      Desktop Client       |  |   Browser Extension    |  |\n                           |  |  (Tauri/Rust Core + GUI)  |  |  (Manifest V3 / WEXT)  |  |\n                           |  +-------------+-------------+  +-----------+------------+  |\n                           +----------------|----------------------------|---------------+\n                                            |                            |\n                     Encrypted WG Tunnel    | HTTPS / WSS / gRPC         | HTTPS / WSS Events\n                     (UDP / Kernel WireGuard)|                            |\n                                            v                            v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                    GATEWAY & INGRESS LAYER                                         |\n|                                                                                                    |\n|   +------------------------------------+          +--------------------------------------------+   |\n|   |         WireGuard Edge Node        |          |            API Gateway / Reverse Proxy     |   |\n|   |   (Kernel wg0 / nftables / CoreDNS)|          |                 (Nginx / Envoy)            |   |\n|   +-----------------+------------------+          +---------------------+----------------------+   |\n+---------------------|---------------------------------------------------|--------------------------+\n                      |                                                   |\n                      | Metrics & Telemetry                               | Auth & API Traffic\n                      v                                                   v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                      CONTROL PLANE SERVICES                                        |\n|                                                                                                    |\n|   +-----------------------+     +-----------------------+     +--------------------------------+   |\n|   |   Node Control Daemon |     |   Core API Service    |     |    Privacy Intelligence Engine |   |\n|   |  (wgctrl / Netlink)   |     |    (Auth, Sub, API)   |     | (Trackers, Scoring, Leaks, FP) |   |\n|   +-----------+-----------+     +-----------+-----------+     +---------------+----------------+   |\n|               |                             |                                 |                    |\n|               +-----------------------------+---------------------------------+                    |\n|                                             |                                                      |\n|                                             v                                                      |\n|                              +------------------------------+                                      |\n|                              |   Event Broker & Bus (Redis) |                                      |\n|                              |  (Pub/Sub, Stream Queue, WS) |                                      |\n|                              +--------------+---------------+                                      |\n|                                             |                                                      |\n|                                             v                                                      |\n|                              +------------------------------+                                      |\n|                              |   Persistent Storage (PGSQL) |                                      |\n|                              |  (TimescaleDB / PostgreSQL)  |                                      |\n|                              +------------------------------+                                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. System Components\n\n### A. Client Tier\n1. **Desktop Client (`pg-desktop`)**:\n   - **Engine**: Tauri v2 + Rust Core with native kernel interface bindings (`wireguard-control` / `wintun` on Windows, `wireguard-go` or `wg-quick` on macOS, Linux native kernel `netlink`).\n   - **Frontend UI**: React + Tailwind CSS client running within the Tauri webview.\n   - **Responsibilities**: Device authentication, local WireGuard keypair generation (never uploads private key), tunnel lifecycle management, DNS routing protection, killswitch (firewall rules via `pfctl`/`iptables`/`wfp`), and local diagnostic probes.\n2. **Browser Privacy Extension (`pg-extension`)**:\n   - **Engine**: WebExtension Manifest V3 (Chromium + Firefox Gecko).\n   - **Responsibilities**: Declarative request analysis (`declarativeNetRequest` + `webRequest`), heuristic third-party tracker mapping, local Canvas/WebGL/Font fingerprint entropy assessment, and active WebRTC IP binding inspection.\n3. **Web Dashboard & Admin Center (`pg-web`)**:\n   - **Engine**: Single-Page App + Server-Side Dashboard for subscription billing, active device/peer management, privacy diagnostic drill-downs, and infrastructure node health metrics.\n\n### B. Gateway & Edge Tier\n1. **WireGuard VPN Node (`pg-vpn-node`)**:\n   - High-throughput Linux edge instance running WireGuard kernel module (`wireguard.ko`), `nftables` for secure IPv4/IPv6 NAT masquerading and peer isolation, and a custom `CoreDNS` resolver instance that applies sinkholes and anti-leak routing.\n2. **Node Daemon (`pg-node-daemon`)**:\n   - Unprivileged Go/Rust sidecar daemon running on each edge node with scoped root/CAP_NET_ADMIN capabilities to read `wg show` statistics via netlink, add/remove peers dynamically, and publish keepalive metrics to the central bus.\n3. **Synthetic Leak Testing Prober (`pg-leak-probe`)**:\n   - Authoritative DNS name server (`*.leak.privacygateway.net`) capable of generating single-use cryptographically signed subdomains (`<token>.leak.privacygateway.net`) to detect upstream DNS resolver leaks.\n\n### C. Core & Analytics Tier\n1. **Core API Server (`pg-api`)**:\n   - Node.js/TypeScript (or Go) backend service handling OAuth 2.0 / OIDC user lifecycle, device authentication sessions, WireGuard public key registration, server topology distribution, and RBAC admin actions.\n2. **Privacy Intelligence Engine (`pg-privacy-engine`)**:\n   - Streaming analytics pipeline ingesting sanitized client telemetry and test payloads, calculating the 5-pillar explainable Privacy Score, and generating actionable remediations.\n3. **Real-time Event Broker (`pg-redis`)**:\n   - In-memory Redis Pub/Sub cluster + Redis Streams for real-time WebSocket fan-out, peer handshake monitoring, and live telemetry updates.\n4. **Relational Database (`pg-db`)**:\n   - PostgreSQL 16 relational database maintaining strict referential integrity, row-level security (RLS), and zero unhashed credential/key storage.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Data-Flow Diagram\n\n```\n+----------------+      +-------------------+      +------------------+      +-------------------+\n| Desktop Client |      | Browser Extension |      | Core API Service |      | Privacy Engine    |\n+-------+--------+      +---------+---------+      +--------+---------+      +---------+---------+\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        | 1. Generate Keypair     |                         |                          |\n        |    (PubKey, PrivKey)    |                         |                          |\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        | 2. POST /api/v1/peers   |                         |                          |\n        |    { pubkey, device }   |                         |                          |\n        |-------------------------------------------------->|                          |\n        |                         |                         | 3. Allocate VIP & Node   |\n        | 4. Return WG Config     |                         |    Store in PostgreSQL   |\n        |    { vip, endpoint, pk }|                         |                          |\n        |<--------------------------------------------------|                          |\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        | 5. Initialize WG Tunnel |                         |                          |\n        |    Handshake to Node    |                         |                          |\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        |                         | 6. Intercept Request    |                          |\n        |                         |    Match Disconnect List|                          |\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        |                         | 7. POST /api/v1/telemetry                          |\n        |                         |    { tracker_hash, cat }|                          |\n        |                         |--------------------------------------------------->|\n        |                         |                         |                          | 8. Re-evaluate\n        |                         |                         |                          |    Privacy Score\n        |                         |                         |                          |\n        | 9. WS Event (Real-time update)                    |                          |\n        |<-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n        |    { score: 87, active_trackers: 14, wg_rx_tx: \"12MB/4MB\" }                  |\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Network-Flow Diagram\n\n```\n               [ User Computer / OS Network Stack ]\n                                |\n               +----------------v----------------+\n               |   OS Routing Table Default GW   |\n               | (Redirect all to wg0 interface) |\n               +----------------+----------------+\n                                |\n             +------------------+------------------+\n             |                                     |\n   [ Encrypted WG Tunnel ]                [ Split Local DNS / Probe ]\n             |                                     |\n             v                                     v\n   +--------------------+               +---------------------+\n   | Edge WireGuard VIP |               | Synthetic Subdomain |\n   |  UDP Port 51820    |               |  Authoritative DNS  |\n   +---------+----------+               +----------+----------+\n             |                                     |\n    [ nftables NAT PostRouting ]                   |\n             |                                     |\n   +---------v----------+                          |\n   | CoreDNS (Local WG) |                          |\n   | (10.66.0.1:53)     |                          |\n   +---------+----------+                          |\n             |                                     |\n             +-----------------+-------------------+\n                               |\n                               v\n                     [ Public Internet ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Repository Structure\n\nA monorepo structure designed with strict module boundaries:\n\n```\nprivacy-gateway/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 .github/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 workflows/              # CI/CD: build, test, lint, container scanning\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docker/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docker-compose.yml      # Local dev environment\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docker-compose.prod.yml # Production multi-service deployment\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api/Dockerfile\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 privacy-engine/Dockerfile\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 wireguard/Dockerfile\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 coredns/Dockerfile\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 packages/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 shared-types/           # TypeScript interfaces & DTOs shared across API & UI\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 crypto-utils/           # WireGuard Curve25519 & token validation utilities\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tracker-db/             # Processed tracker domain definitions & blocklists\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 apps/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api/                    # Core REST + WebSocket Server (Node.js/Express/TypeScript)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/         # Environment & security config\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/    # Route controllers\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/     # Auth, RateLimit, RBAC, SecurityHeaders\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/         # Express router endpoints\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/       # WireGuard, OAuth, Device, Billing services\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 ws/             # WebSocket connection & real-time dispatchers\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 privacy-engine/         # Dedicated scoring, fingerprint & leak analyzer\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 analyzers/      # Fingerprint, DNS, WebRTC, Tracker analyzers\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 scoring/        # Deterministic 5-pillar privacy scoring math\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.ts\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 web-dashboard/          # Production React Dashboard (SPA / SSR)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 components/     # High-contrast, accessibility-first UI components\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 hooks/          # Real-time WebSocket & data hooks\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 pages/          # User Dashboard, Admin Center, Diagnostics\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 desktop-client/         # Tauri v2 Desktop Application\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src-tauri/          # Rust Core (Native WFP / iptables / wg-control)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/                # Client React UI\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 browser-extension/      # Chromium & Firefox Manifest V3 Extension\n\342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202       \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 background/     # DeclarativeNetRequest & event listeners\n\342\224\202       \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 content/        # In-page fingerprint & WebRTC probers\n\342\224\202       \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 popup/          # Extension popup UI\n\342\224\202       \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 manifest.json\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 infrastructure/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 migrations/             # Database SQL schema migrations\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 wireguard/              # Linux edge server startup scripts & nftables rules\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 coredns/                # Corefile & blocklist configuration\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tsconfig.base.json\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Docker Architecture\n\nThe local development and edge node setup orchestrates 6 containerized services with minimal privilege execution:\n\n```yaml\nversion: \'3.9\'\n\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\n    container_name: pg_database\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_DB: privacy_gateway\n      POSTGRES_USER: ${DB_USER:-pgadmin}\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD:-pgsecret}\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n      - ../infrastructure/migrations:/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U ${DB_USER:-pgadmin} -d privacy_gateway\"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7-alpine\n    container_name: pg_redis\n    command: [\"redis-server\", \"--appendonly\", \"yes\", \"--requirepass\", \"${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}\"]\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"redis-cli\", \"-a\", \"${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}\", \"ping\"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  api:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/api/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_api\n    depends_on:\n      postgres:\n        condition: service_healthy\n      redis:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    environment:\n      NODE_ENV: production\n      PORT: 3000\n      DATABASE_URL: postgresql://${DB_USER:-pgadmin}:${DB_PASSWORD:-pgsecret}@postgres:5432/privacy_gateway\n      REDIS_URL: redis://:${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}@redis:6379\n      GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID: ${GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID}\n      GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET: ${GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET}\n      JWT_SECRET: ${JWT_SECRET}\n    ports:\n      - \"3000:3000\"\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n      - pg-public-net\n\n  privacy-engine:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/privacy-engine/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_privacy_engine\n    depends_on:\n      - redis\n      - postgres\n    environment:\n      REDIS_URL: redis://:${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}@redis:6379\n      DATABASE_URL: postgresql://${DB_USER:-pgadmin}:${DB_PASSWORD:-pgsecret}@postgres:5432/privacy_gateway\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n\n  wireguard-node:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/wireguard/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_wireguard\n    cap_add:\n      - NET_ADMIN\n      - SYS_MODULE\n    sysctls:\n      - net.ipv4.ip_forward=1\n      - net.ipv4.conf.all.src_valid_mark=1\n    ports:\n      - \"51820:51820/udp\"\n    volumes:\n      - /lib/modules:/lib/modules:ro\n      - wg-config:/etc/wireguard\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n      - pg-vpn-net\n\n  coredns:\n    image: coredns/coredns:latest\n    container_name: pg_coredns\n    command: [\"-conf\", \"/etc/coredns/Corefile\"]\n    volumes:\n      - ../infrastructure/coredns/Corefile:/etc/coredns/Corefile:ro\n    networks:\n      - pg-vpn-net\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\n  wg-config:\n\nnetworks:\n  pg-internal-net:\n    internal: true\n  pg-public-net:\n  pg-vpn-net:\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Relational Database Schema (PostgreSQL DDL)\n\n```sql\n-- Extensions\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS \"uuid-ossp\";\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS \"pgcrypto\";\n\n-- Enums\nCREATE TYPE user_role AS ENUM (\'user\', \'admin\', \'auditor\');\nCREATE TYPE auth_provider AS ENUM (\'google\', \'email_password\', \'github\', \'apple\');\nCREATE TYPE subscription_tier AS ENUM (\'free\', \'premium\', \'business\');\nCREATE TYPE subscription_status AS ENUM (\'active\', \'past_due\', \'canceled\', \'trialing\');\nCREATE TYPE peer_status AS ENUM (\'active\', \'revoked\', \'dormant\');\nCREATE TYPE leak_status AS ENUM (\'safe\', \'warning\', \'leak_detected\');\nCREATE TYPE risk_level AS ENUM (\'low\', \'medium\', \'high\');\n\n-- 1. Users Table\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    display_name VARCHAR(100),\n    avatar_url TEXT,\n    role user_role DEFAULT \'user\' NOT NULL,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 2. Authentication Accounts (OIDC / OAuth identities)\nCREATE TABLE authentication_accounts (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    provider auth_provider NOT NULL,\n    provider_subject_id VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    email_at_provider VARCHAR(255),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    UNIQUE(provider, provider_subject_id)\n);\n\n-- 3. User Sessions\nCREATE TABLE user_sessions (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    refresh_token_hash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    user_agent TEXT,\n    ip_address INET,\n    expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    revoked_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 4. Devices (Registered desktops/mobiles)\nCREATE TABLE devices (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    device_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL, -- \'windows\', \'macos\', \'linux\', \'android\', \'ios\'\n    client_version VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\n    last_active_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 5. VPN Server Infrastructure Nodes\nCREATE TABLE vpn_servers (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    node_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    country_code VARCHAR(2) NOT NULL, -- \'US\', \'DE\', \'SG\', \'IN\'\n    city VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    public_endpoint VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL, -- \'us-east.privacygateway.net:51820\'\n    wireguard_public_key VARCHAR(44) NOT NULL,\n    ipv4_subnet CIDR NOT NULL, -- e.g. \'10.66.1.0/24\'\n    ipv6_subnet CIDR,\n    capacity_peers INTEGER DEFAULT 250 NOT NULL,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE NOT NULL,\n    is_maintenance BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 6. WireGuard Peers\nCREATE TABLE wireguard_peers (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    vpn_server_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES vpn_servers(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,\n    public_key VARCHAR(44) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    allocated_ipv4 INET UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    allocated_ipv6 INET UNIQUE,\n    preshared_key_encrypted TEXT, -- Optional encrypted PSK for post-quantum resistance\n    status peer_status DEFAULT \'active\' NOT NULL,\n    rx_bytes BIGINT DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    tx_bytes BIGINT DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    last_handshake_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 7. Subscriptions & Billing Abstraction\nCREATE TABLE subscriptions (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    tier subscription_tier DEFAULT \'free\' NOT NULL,\n    status subscription_status DEFAULT \'active\' NOT NULL,\n    payment_provider VARCHAR(50), -- e.g. \'stripe\', \'paddle\'\n    external_subscription_id VARCHAR(255),\n    current_period_start TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    current_period_end TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    canceled_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 8. Privacy Scans & Scores (Aggregated Snapshot)\nCREATE TABLE privacy_scans (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    overall_score INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (overall_score BETWEEN 0 AND 100),\n    network_privacy_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    tracking_exposure_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    fingerprint_risk_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    leak_protection_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    dns_leak_status leak_status NOT NULL,\n    webrtc_leak_status leak_status NOT NULL,\n    trackers_blocked_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 9. Privacy Events (Granular Event Feed)\nCREATE TABLE privacy_events (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    event_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL, -- \'tracker_blocked\', \'dns_leak_detected\', \'new_handshake\'\n    severity VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL, -- \'info\', \'warning\', \'critical\'\n    description TEXT NOT NULL,\n    metadata JSONB DEFAULT \'{}\'::JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 10. Audit Logs (Security & Admin Activity)\nCREATE TABLE audit_logs (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    actor_user_id UUID REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    action VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    target_resource VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    target_id UUID,\n    ip_address INET,\n    metadata JSONB DEFAULT \'{}\'::JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Indexes for performance\nCREATE INDEX idx_user_sessions_lookup ON user_sessions(user_id, expires_at);\nCREATE INDEX idx_wg_peers_server ON wireguard_peers(vpn_server_id, status);\nCREATE INDEX idx_privacy_scans_user ON privacy_scans(user_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_privacy_events_user ON privacy_events(user_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_audit_logs_actor ON audit_logs(actor_user_id, created_at DESC);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Authentication Architecture\n\nThe authentication framework uses an **OpenID Connect (OIDC) Token Exchange Model** combined with short-lived JWT Access Tokens and cryptographically rotated Refresh Tokens.\n\n```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                    AUTHENTICATION MODEL                                        |\n|                                                                                                |\n|  [ Client (Desktop / Web) ]                                                                    |\n|             |                                                                                  |\n|             | 1. Initiates Google OAuth Code Flow with PKCE                                    |\n|             v                                                                                  |\n|  [ Google Identity Server (accounts.google.com) ]                                              |\n|             |                                                                                  |\n|             | 2. Grants Authorization Code & ID Token                                          |\n|             v                                                                                  |\n|  [ Client (Desktop / Web) ]                                                                    |\n|             |                                                                                  |\n|             | 3. POST /api/v1/auth/google/callback { code, code_verifier, redirect_uri }       |\n|             v                                                                                  |\n|  [ Privacy Gateway Core API (Server-Side Verification) ]                                       |\n|             |                                                                                  |\n|             | 4. Exchanging code directly with Google OAuth2 Token Endpoint (HTTPS Backend)    |\n|             | 5. Verifies Google ID Token Signature against Google\'s public certs (JWKS)        |\n|             | 6. Extracts & verifies { sub, email, email_verified }                             |\n|             | 7. Performs Upsert in `users` and `authentication_accounts`                      |\n|             | 8. Issues:                                                                       |\n|             |    - Access Token: RS256 JWT (Expiry: 15 minutes, stateless)                     |\n|             |    - Refresh Token: CSPRNG 256-bit hash (Expiry: 30 days, stored in `user_sessions`)|\n|             v                                                                                  |\n|  [ Client (Desktop / Web) ]                                                                    |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Google OAuth 2.0 Flow (PKCE Hardened)\n\n```\nClient (Desktop App)            Google Identity           Privacy Gateway Backend\n       |                               |                              |\n       | 1. Generate code_verifier     |                              |\n       |    & code_challenge (S256)    |                              |\n       |                               |                              |\n       | 2. Open Auth Browser / Loopback                              |\n       |    https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth              |\n       |------------------------------>|                              |\n       |                               |                              |\n       | 3. User approves sign-in      |                              |\n       |                               |                              |\n       | 4. Redirect with auth ?code=..|                              |\n       |<------------------------------|                              |\n       |                                                              |\n       | 5. POST /api/v1/auth/google/verify                           |\n       |    { code, code_verifier, redirect_uri }                     |\n       |------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                                              | 6. Backend exchanges code\n       |                                                              |    for tokens with Google\n       |                                                              |    via https://oauth2.googleapis.com\n       |                                                              | 7. Validate Google certs\n       |                                                              | 8. Ensure email_verified == true\n       |                                                              | 9. Mint PG Access/Refresh tokens\n       | 10. Return { access_token, refresh_token, user_profile }     |\n       |<-------------------------------------------------------------|\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. WireGuard Network & Server Architecture\n\n### A. Dynamic Peer Provisioning Protocol\n1. **Client-Side Key Generation**: The desktop client executes a secure curve25519 scalar multiplication locally using `x25519-dalek` / libsodium to derive a `PrivateKey` and `PublicKey`. The `PrivateKey` **never leaves the device**.\n2. **Registration API**: Client sends `POST /api/v1/vpn/peers` payload `{ public_key, server_id, device_id }` with its JWT Bearer token.\n3. **Subnet IP Allocation**: The API assigns the next available unique IP within the target node\'s CIDR (e.g. `10.66.1.42/32`).\n4. **Node Synchronization**: The API pushes peer configuration to the edge node daemon over TLS gRPC/UNIX socket:\n   ```bash\n   wg set wg0 peer <CLIENT_PUBLIC_KEY> allowed-ips 10.66.1.42/32\n   ```\n5. **Config Output**: Client receives the connection profile:\n   ```ini\n   [Interface]\n   PrivateKey = <CLIENT_LOCAL_PRIVATE_KEY>\n   Address = 10.66.1.42/32\n   DNS = 10.66.0.1\n\n   [Peer]\n   PublicKey = <VPN_NODE_PUBLIC_KEY>\n   Endpoint = us-east.privacygateway.net:51820\n   AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0, ::/0\n   PersistentKeepalive = 25\n   ```\n\n### B. Linux Edge Node Firewall & NAT (`nftables`)\n```nftables\ntable inet filter {\n    chain input {\n        type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;\n        iif \"lo\" accept\n        ct state established,related accept\n        ip protocol icmp accept\n        ip6 nexthdr icmpv6 accept\n        tcp dport 22 accept # Restricted to Bastion SSH\n        udp dport 51820 accept # WireGuard Ingress\n    }\n    chain forward {\n        type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;\n        iif \"wg0\" oif \"eth0\" accept\n        iif \"eth0\" oif \"wg0\" ct state established,related accept\n    }\n}\ntable ip nat {\n    chain postrouting {\n        type nat hook postrouting priority 100; policy accept;\n        oif \"eth0\" masquerade\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Desktop Architecture (Tauri / Rust)\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         TAURI DESKTOP CLIENT                       |\n|                                                                    |\n|  +--------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  |                 Frontend Webview (React + TS)                |  |\n|  |   - Privacy Dashboard, Server Selector, Realtime Bandwidth    |  |\n|  |   - Diagnostic Leak Prober UI, Alert Toast Notifications     |  |\n|  +------------------------------+-------------------------------+  |\n|                                 | IPC (Tauri Invoke Commands)       |\n|  +------------------------------v-------------------------------+  |\n|  |                     Tauri Rust Core Engine                   |  |\n|  |                                                              |  |\n|  |  +---------------------+  +-------------------------------+  |  |\n|  |  | Auth & Token Store  |  | WireGuard Controller          |  |  |\n|  |  | (OS Keyring / Vault)|  | (WFP / Netlink / Wintun)       |  |  |\n|  |  +---------------------+  +---------------+---------------+  |  |\n|  |                                           |                  |  |\n|  |  +---------------------+  +---------------v---------------+  |  |\n|  |  | System Killswitch   |  | Traffic Diagnostics Worker    |  |  |\n|  |  | (Blocks non-WG out) |  | (Latency, RX/TX, Handshake)   |  |  |\n|  |  +---------------------+  +-------------------------------+  |  |\n|  +--------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Local Secret Isolation**: Refresh tokens and WireGuard private keys reside in the OS Keychain/SecretService via the `keyring` Rust crate.\n- **Privilege Separation**: Tunnel activation is handled by a lightweight privileged helper daemon or `setuid`/`WFP` service on Windows/Linux/macOS, preventing the GUI from needing root permissions.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Browser Extension Architecture (Manifest V3)\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    BROWSER EXTENSION (MANIFEST V3)                 |\n|                                                                    |\n|  +-----------------------+           +--------------------------+  |\n|  |     Popup / Panel     |           |   In-Page Content Script |  |\n|  | (Score, Active Blocks)|           | (Canvas/WebGL/WebRTC)    |  |\n|  +-----------+-----------+           +------------+-------------+  |\n|              |                                    |                |\n|              +-----------------+------------------+                |\n|                                | Message Passing                   |\n|              +-----------------v------------------+                |\n|              |      Service Worker (Background)   |                |\n|              |                                    |                |\n|              |  +------------------------------+  |                |\n|              |  | declarativeNetRequest Rules  |  |                |\n|              |  | (Blocks Advertising/Trackers)|  |                |\n|              |  +------------------------------+  |                |\n|              |  +------------------------------+  |                |\n|              |  | Event Aggregator & Telemetry |  |                |\n|              |  | (Hashes domain, posts to API)|  |                |\n|              |  +------------------------------+  |                |\n|              +------------------------------------+                |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n1. **Tracker Identification**: Uses parsed public blocklists (EasyPrivacy, Peter Lowe, Disconnect) compiled into efficient binary Bloom filters / DNR rulesets.\n2. **Fingerprint Surface Detection**: Measures local entropy of `navigator.userAgent`, screen color depths, Canvas 2D rendered hash deviations, and audio context buffer anomalies.\n3. **WebRTC Prober**: Executes an in-memory `RTCPeerConnection` ice candidate test, checks whether raw local/public IPs leak past the interface, and marks status as `SAFE` or `EXPOSED`.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Privacy Intelligence Engine\n\nThe Privacy Engine evaluates 4 orthogonal privacy vectors to compute an overall 100-point index:\n\n```\n                                  +---------------------------------------+\n                                  |       PRIVACY INTELLIGENCE ENGINE     |\n                                  +-------------------+-------------------+\n                                                      |\n                 +--------------------+---------------+--------------------+---------------------+\n                 |                    |                                    |                     |\n                 v                    v                                    v                     v\n       +------------------+  +--------------------+              +-------------------+  +-------------------+\n       | Network Privacy  |  | Tracking Exposure  |              | Fingerprint Risk  |  |  Leak Protection  |\n       |     (Weight 30%) |  |    (Weight 25%)    |              |   (Weight 20%)    |  |   (Weight 25%)    |\n       +--------+---------+  +---------+----------+              +---------+---------+  +---------+---------+\n                |                      |                                   |                      |\n                v                      v                                   v                      v\n        - WG Tunnel Active?    - Trackers Blocked / Visited        - Canvas Entropy       - DNS Leak Clean?\n        - Encrypted DNS?       - Third-Party Cookie Policy         - User-Agent Uniqueness- WebRTC Binding Safe?\n        - VPN Server Trust?    - Telemetry Requests                - AudioContext Entropy - IPv6 Leak Safe?\n```\n\n### Deterministic Scoring Formula:\n$$\\text{PrivacyScore} = (0.30 \\times S_{\\text{network}}) + (0.25 \\times S_{\\text{tracking}}) + (0.20 \\times S_{\\text{fingerprint}}) + (0.25 \\times S_{\\text{leak}})$$\n\nEvery category is explainable down to exact deduction reasons (e.g. *\"-15 points: WebSockets leaking ISP DNS address 198.51.100.1\"*, *\"-10 points: Canvas fingerprint uniqueness matches >99.4% of web cohort\"*).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Real-Time Architecture (WebSocket & Event Stream)\n\n```\n[ Edge VPN Node ]            [ Browser Extension ]         [ Desktop Client ]\n       |                              |                            |\n       | Handshake & RX/TX metrics    | Tracker Block Event        | Diagnostic Result\n       v                              v                            v\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       API & PRIVACY INGESTION PIPELINE                          |\n+---------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n                                        |\n                                        v\n                       +---------------------------------+\n                       | Redis Pub/Sub (`pg:events:user`) |\n                       +----------------+----------------+\n                                        |\n                                        v\n                       +---------------------------------+\n                       | WebSocket Gateway (Socket.IO/ws)|\n                       +----------------+----------------+\n                                        |\n                +-----------------------+------------------------+\n                |                                                |\n                v                                                v\n    [ Web Dashboard Client ]                         [ Desktop Client GUI ]\n    (Real-time State Mutation)                       (Real-time State Mutation)\n```\n\n- **Protocol**: Standard WebSocket with heartbeat ping/pong every 25 seconds.\n- **Payload Format**: Standardized Envelope `{ event: \"TELEMETRY_UPDATE\" | \"PEER_HANDSHAKE\" | \"LEAK_ALERT\", payload: { ... }, timestamp: 1771720000 }`.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Multi-Server Node Topology\n\n```\n                                +---------------------------+\n                                |  Central API / Dispatcher |\n                                +-------------+-------------+\n                                              |\n                     +------------------------+------------------------+\n                     |                        |                        |\n                     v                        v                        v\n            +-----------------+      +-----------------+      +-----------------+\n            |   Node: US-East |      |   Node: EU-West |      |  Node: AP-South |\n            | (Virginia, USA) |      | (Frankfurt, DE) |      | (Singapore, SG) |\n            +--------+--------+      +--------+--------+      +--------+--------+\n                     |                        |                        |\n                     |                        |                        |\n             Capacity: 250 peers      Capacity: 250 peers      Capacity: 250 peers\n             Subnet: 10.66.1.0/24     Subnet: 10.66.2.0/24     Subnet: 10.66.3.0/24\n```\n\n1. **Topology Distribution**: Client queries `GET /api/v1/vpn/servers` to receive live list of active nodes, geolocations, latency metrics, and real-time load percentages.\n2. **Graceful Failover**: If a node goes to maintenance mode (`is_maintenance = true`), the control plane notifies connected clients via WebSocket to automatically transition to the nearest available server without dropping authentication sessions.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Security Architecture\n\n1. **Defense in Depth**:\n   - Zero open listening ports on backend internal networks; only port 443 (HTTPS) and port 51820 (WireGuard UDP) are publicly exposed.\n2. **Cryptographic Primitives**:\n   - Authentication: JWT signed with RS256 (RSA 4096-bit keypair) or EdDSA.\n   - Tunnel: WireGuard (Curve25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305, BLAKE2s, SipHash24).\n   - Secret Storage: PBKDF2 / Argon2id with random salt for all sensitive tokens.\n3. **Privilege Boundaries**:\n   - The web app runs as unprivileged user `node` / `appuser` (UID 10001).\n   - WireGuard management commands are executed exclusively via a dedicated Go/Rust daemon over a restricted local UNIX socket requiring `CAP_NET_ADMIN`.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Threat Model (STRIDE)\n\n| Threat Category | Potential Attack Vector | System Countermeasure & Mitigation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Spoofing** | Rogue client attempts to claim another user\'s allocated VIP. | WireGuard cryptographic binding: `AllowedIPs` on server matches only the registered client public key. Packets with forged source IPs are dropped by the kernel. |\n| **Tampering** | In-transit tampering of DNS queries to induce leaks. | All DNS traffic within the tunnel is routed strictly to internal `CoreDNS` (10.66.0.1:53) with DNSSEC validation and encrypted DoH upstream. |\n| **Repudiation** | Malicious administrator deletes or modifies audit trails. | Write-only, append-only PostgreSQL `audit_logs` table with non-superuser grants and immutable timestamps. |\n| **Information Disclosure** | Memory dump of API server revealing user private keys. | Zero-Knowledge Key Principle: Private keys are generated on client devices and **never transmitted to or stored on servers**. |\n| **Denial of Service** | Volumetric SYN flood or UDP flood against API / WG node. | Kernel-level `syncookies`, WireGuard built-in Cookie mechanism (BLAKE2s MACs against DoS), and rate-limiting middleware (Redis token bucket). |\n| **Elevation of Privilege** | Container escape from API server to host network stack. | Container isolation: rootless containers, Read-Only root filesystem, `no-new-privileges:true`, and dropped Linux capabilities. |\n\n---\n\n## 18. Privacy & Data-Retention Model\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        ZERO BROWSING LOGS GUARANTEE                               |\n|                                                                                   |\n|  [ WHAT WE NEVER RECORD OR LOG ]                                                  |\n|  x Full URLs or HTTP/HTTPS request bodies                                         |\n|  x DNS query histories or destination IPs                                         |\n|  x Unencrypted user traffic or payload contents                                   |\n|  x WireGuard Private Keys (Never touch server)                                    |\n|                                                                                   |\n|  [ WHAT IS MINIMALLY RETAINED & CONFIGURABLE ]                                    |\n|  \342\234\223 Account metadata (User ID, Email, Role, Subscription)                         |\n|  \342\234\223 WireGuard Public Key & Allocated VIP (Active lifetime only)                    |\n|  \342\234\223 Aggregated Bandwidth (Total RX/TX bytes for capacity planning)                 |\n|  \342\234\223 Diagnostic Test Snapshots (User-controlled retention: 24h / 7d / 30d / Purge)  |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 19. Production Deployment Strategy\n\n1. **CI/CD Pipeline**:\n   - GitHub Actions automated matrix testing: unit tests, linter, static security analysis (Trivy, Semgrep), and Docker container builds.\n2. **Infrastructure as Code (IaC)**:\n   - Terraform / OpenTofu provisioning of edge cloud compute instances with pinned Ubuntu 24.04 LTS kernel across geographical regions.\n3. **Rolling Updates**:\n   - Zero-downtime blue/green deployment for the Central API and WebSocket cluster.\n   - WireGuard servers updated node-by-node with automated drainage of active peers.\n\n---\n\n## 20. Development Roadmap\n\n```\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 0: Master Architecture Specification & Security Blueprint (COMPLETED)       |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 1: Repository Structure, Docker Orchestration & PostgreSQL Schema Setup     |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 2: OpenID Connect & Google OAuth Authentication Engine (Secure PKCE)        |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 3: Real WireGuard Server Node Controller, IP Allocation & Netlink Daemon    |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 4: Core REST & WebSocket Real-time Telemetry API                            |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 5: Tauri Desktop Client Engine (Cross-Platform Tunnel & Killswitch)         |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 6: Browser Privacy Extension (Declarative Tracker Blocking & Heuristics)    |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 7: Deterministic Privacy Intelligence Scoring Engine & Leak Detection Probe |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 8: Production Web Management Dashboard & Admin Operations Center            |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase 9: End-to-End Integration, Automated Test Suites & Security Hardening       |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 21. Major Technical Risks & Mitigations\n\n1. **Kernel vs. Userland WireGuard Compatibility**:\n   - *Risk*: Varying client OS permissions may restrict kernel WireGuard driver installation.\n   - *Mitigation*: Fallback to high-performance userland implementation (`wireguard-go` / `wintun`) if kernel module access is denied.\n2. **WebRTC & IPv6 Leak Vectors**:\n   - *Risk*: Browsers bypassing standard routing tables through local STUN socket queries.\n   - *Mitigation*: Client-side firewall rules (WFP/iptables) enforcing that all non-WG outbound UDP traffic is dropped while connected (Killswitch).\n3. **State Desynchronization in Edge Nodes**:\n   - *Risk*: Network partition causing disparity between Central API database and WireGuard node peers.\n   - *Mitigation*: Node daemon executes a periodic state reconciliation loop querying the API to synchronize active peer public keys every 60 seconds.\n\n---\n\n## 22. Estimated Complexity of Components\n\n| Component | Complexity Tier | Primary Technical Challenges |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Core Database & API** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Strict RBAC, session lifecycle, token rotation, clean entity models. |\n| **Google OAuth / PKCE** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Handling both desktop deep-linking/loopback and web redirects securely. |\n| **WireGuard Node Engine** | **High** (4.5/5) | Kernel netlink integration, `nftables` isolation, dynamic IP pool management. |\n| **Tauri Desktop Client** | **High** (4.5/5) | OS-level network stack manipulation, routing table overriding, killswitch rules. |\n| **Browser Extension** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Manifest V3 declarative rule compilation, accurate Canvas/WebRTC entropy probing. |\n| **Privacy Intelligence Engine** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Deterministic mathematical scoring model, explainable risk breakdown. |\n| **Real-time Bus (WS/Redis)** | **Moderate** (3/5) | Reliable pub/sub delivery across multi-device client sessions. |\n\n---\n\n### Architecture Review Confirmation\n\nThis comprehensive Master Architecture establishes the engineering foundation for **Privacy Gateway**. Every metric, connection, and test is grounded in verifiable cryptographic, network, and heuristic primitives.\n\nPlease review and confirm this architectural blueprint so we may proceed to **Phase 1: Repository Structure, Docker Orchestration, and PostgreSQL Database Migration**."
