  user_input {
    user_response: "I reviewed your Phase 0 architecture for Privacy Gateway.\n\nThe overall architecture and roadmap are approved as the direction of the project, but DO NOT proceed to Phase 1 yet.\n\nBefore writing any implementation code, revise the architecture to address the following issues.\n\n## 1. WireGuard Security / Privilege Model\n\nThe current design gives the WireGuard Docker container:\n\n* NET_ADMIN\n* SYS_MODULE\n* access to /lib/modules\n\nThis may be acceptable for development, but we are ultimately building a real VPN service.\n\nRe-evaluate whether WireGuard should run:\n\nA. directly on the Linux host/edge server, with a tightly scoped node-control daemon\n\nOR\n\nB. inside a privileged container.\n\nCompare both approaches and recommend the safer production architecture.\n\nThe final design must:\n\n* minimize privileges\n* isolate the control plane from the VPN data plane\n* prevent the web/API application from having root access\n* use a narrowly scoped privileged component for WireGuard management\n* clearly separate development deployment from production deployment\n\nDo not simply keep the existing design without analysis.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Privacy / Logging Model\n\nThe architecture currently describes a \"zero browsing logs guarantee.\"\n\nReplace this with a technically precise privacy model.\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\n### Data we intentionally do NOT collect\n\nExamples:\n\n* browsing history\n* full URLs\n* page contents\n* traffic payloads\n* DNS query history\n* unnecessary destination history\n* WireGuard private keys\n\n### Data required for operating the service\n\nExamples:\n\n* account information\n* device information\n* WireGuard public key\n* allocated VPN address\n* server assignment\n* aggregate bandwidth\n* operational health metrics\n\n### Data used by the privacy-intelligence system\n\nExamples:\n\n* tracker events\n* privacy test results\n* fingerprint-analysis results\n* DNS/WebRTC diagnostic results\n\nFor each category specify:\n\n* whether it is stored\n* retention period\n* whether the user can delete it\n* whether it is required for the service\n\nAlso explain what may still be visible to:\n\n* the VPN server\n* cloud infrastructure provider\n* DNS provider\n* authentication provider\n* administrators\n\nDo not make absolute anonymity or privacy claims that the architecture cannot technically guarantee.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Privacy Score\n\nThe current architecture proposes:\n\nNetwork Privacy = 30%\nTracking Exposure = 25%\nFingerprint Risk = 20%\nLeak Protection = 25%\n\nDo NOT automatically treat these weights as final.\n\nInstead:\n\n1. Define exactly what each category measures.\n2. Define the inputs.\n3. Define how each input is normalized.\n4. Define how deductions/bonuses work.\n5. Explain why each weight is appropriate.\n6. Identify limitations of the score.\n7. Make the score deterministic and reproducible.\n8. Make every score change explainable to the user.\n\nThe system must never generate a random or arbitrary privacy score.\n\nAlso distinguish between:\n\n\"privacy exposure detected\"\n\nand\n\n\"identity confirmed.\"\n\nWe must never claim that fingerprint analysis identifies a real person.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Browser Extension Capability\n\nReview the proposed browser extension architecture.\n\nFor each planned feature, explicitly identify:\n\n* what the browser extension can reliably detect\n* what it can only estimate\n* what browser APIs restrict\n* what cannot be detected reliably\n\nReview:\n\n* tracker detection\n* Canvas fingerprinting\n* WebGL fingerprinting\n* Audio fingerprinting\n* WebRTC exposure\n* third-party requests\n* cookies\n* browser characteristics\n\nDo not promise universal fingerprint detection.\n\nIf something is an estimate, label it as an estimate.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Real-Time Architecture\n\nKeep the Redis + WebSocket architecture, but define exactly what \"real-time\" means.\n\nSpecify:\n\n* event producers\n* event broker\n* event consumers\n* WebSocket authentication\n* event authorization\n* reconnect behavior\n* heartbeat\n* event ordering\n* duplicate events\n* lost events\n* backpressure\n* persistence requirements\n\nThe system must never create fake real-time events.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Production vs Development\n\nCreate two clearly separated architectures:\n\n### Development\n\nSuitable for:\n\n* local Docker development\n* testing\n* one VPN server\n* development credentials\n\n### Production\n\nSuitable for:\n\n* real users\n* real VPN traffic\n* multiple VPN servers\n* TLS\n* secrets management\n* monitoring\n* backups\n* disaster recovery\n* server provisioning\n* server removal\n* security updates\n* key rotation\n\nClearly mark which components are NOT production-ready yet.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Commercial VPN Architecture\n\nThe long-term goal is to operate Privacy Gateway as a real VPN service.\n\nTherefore revise the architecture to account for:\n\n* multiple geographic VPN nodes\n* user/device limits\n* server capacity\n* peer allocation\n* node health\n* node draining\n* maintenance\n* provisioning\n* decommissioning\n* subscription plans\n* payment-provider integration\n* abuse handling\n* account suspension\n* security incidents\n* operational monitoring\n* customer support requirements\n\nDo not claim that these are implemented if they are only architectural plans.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Authentication\n\nKeep Google OAuth/OIDC with PKCE.\n\nHowever, clearly separate:\n\n### Web authentication\n\nBrowser \342\206\222 Google \342\206\222 Backend\n\n### Desktop authentication\n\nDesktop \342\206\222 System Browser \342\206\222 Google \342\206\222 Backend \342\206\222 Desktop\n\nEnsure:\n\n* PKCE\n* state validation\n* redirect URI validation\n* token verification\n* issuer validation\n* audience validation\n* email_verified validation\n* secure session handling\n\nDo not store Google access tokens unless there is a specific requirement.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Secrets and Keys\n\nReview all secret handling.\n\nWireGuard:\n\n* client private key must remain on client\n* server private key must remain securely on server\n* API must never return client private keys\n* logs must never contain private keys\n\nApplication:\n\n* Google OAuth secrets\n* JWT signing keys\n* database credentials\n* Redis credentials\n* TLS certificates\n\nUse a proper secret-management strategy.\n\nDo not put production secrets in Git or Docker images.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Docker Production Security\n\nReview the Docker design.\n\nFor every container specify:\n\n* user\n* capabilities\n* network access\n* filesystem access\n* volumes\n* exposed ports\n* secrets\n* whether it needs root\n* whether it needs host networking\n\nUse:\n\n* least privilege\n* read-only filesystems where practical\n* no-new-privileges\n* dropped capabilities\n* pinned image versions\n* health checks\n* resource limits\n\nDo not use `latest` tags for production dependencies.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Threat Model\n\nExpand the existing STRIDE model.\n\nInclude threats involving:\n\n* compromised desktop client\n* malicious browser extension\n* compromised VPN node\n* compromised API\n* compromised database\n* malicious administrator\n* stolen authentication credentials\n* stolen WireGuard keys\n* cloud provider compromise\n* container escape\n* supply-chain attack\n* DDoS\n* malicious users\n* abuse of the VPN service\n\nFor every threat specify:\n\n* attack\n* impact\n* mitigation\n* residual risk\n\n---\n\n## 12. Important Architectural Principle\n\nThe final architecture must preserve this separation:\n\nCLIENTS\n\342\206\223\nVPN DATA PLANE\n\342\206\223\nINTERNET\n\nand separately:\n\nCLIENTS\n\342\206\223\nCONTROL PLANE\n\342\206\223\nPRIVACY INTELLIGENCE\n\342\206\223\nREAL-TIME EVENTS\n\342\206\223\nDASHBOARD\n\nThe VPN data plane should not automatically become a browsing-history collection system.\n\nPrivacy analysis should use the minimum information necessary.\n\n---\n\n# Required Output\n\nDo NOT write implementation code.\n\nProduce a revised **Phase 0.1 Architecture Specification** containing:\n\n1. Revised architecture diagram\n2. Revised component responsibilities\n3. Revised network flow\n4. Revised data flow\n5. Development architecture\n6. Production architecture\n7. Revised Docker architecture\n8. Revised database model\n9. Authentication model\n10. WireGuard privilege model\n11. Browser-extension capability matrix\n12. Privacy-data model\n13. Privacy-score methodology\n14. Real-time event architecture\n15. Multi-server architecture\n16. Production infrastructure architecture\n17. Expanded threat model\n18. Security boundaries\n19. Known limitations\n20. What is actually implementable now\n21. What requires future infrastructure\n22. Revised development roadmap\n\nAt the end, provide a section:\n\n### \"Changes From Phase 0\"\n\nList every change you made and explain why.\n\nDo NOT proceed to Phase 1.\n\nWait for my approval after producing Phase 0.1."
    items {
      text: "I reviewed your Phase 0 architecture for Privacy Gateway.\n\nThe overall architecture and roadmap are approved as the direction of the project, but DO NOT proceed to Phase 1 yet.\n\nBefore writing any implementation code, revise the architecture to address the following issues.\n\n## 1. WireGuard Security / Privilege Model\n\nThe current design gives the WireGuard Docker container:\n\n* NET_ADMIN\n* SYS_MODULE\n* access to /lib/modules\n\nThis may be acceptable for development, but we are ultimately building a real VPN service.\n\nRe-evaluate whether WireGuard should run:\n\nA. directly on the Linux host/edge server, with a tightly scoped node-control daemon\n\nOR\n\nB. inside a privileged container.\n\nCompare both approaches and recommend the safer production architecture.\n\nThe final design must:\n\n* minimize privileges\n* isolate the control plane from the VPN data plane\n* prevent the web/API application from having root access\n* use a narrowly scoped privileged component for WireGuard management\n* clearly separate development deployment from production deployment\n\nDo not simply keep the existing design without analysis.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Privacy / Logging Model\n\nThe architecture currently describes a \"zero browsing logs guarantee.\"\n\nReplace this with a technically precise privacy model.\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\n### Data we intentionally do NOT collect\n\nExamples:\n\n* browsing history\n* full URLs\n* page contents\n* traffic payloads\n* DNS query history\n* unnecessary destination history\n* WireGuard private keys\n\n### Data required for operating the service\n\nExamples:\n\n* account information\n* device information\n* WireGuard public key\n* allocated VPN address\n* server assignment\n* aggregate bandwidth\n* operational health metrics\n\n### Data used by the privacy-intelligence system\n\nExamples:\n\n* tracker events\n* privacy test results\n* fingerprint-analysis results\n* DNS/WebRTC diagnostic results\n\nFor each category specify:\n\n* whether it is stored\n* retention period\n* whether the user can delete it\n* whether it is required for the service\n\nAlso explain what may still be visible to:\n\n* the VPN server\n* cloud infrastructure provider\n* DNS provider\n* authentication provider\n* administrators\n\nDo not make absolute anonymity or privacy claims that the architecture cannot technically guarantee.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Privacy Score\n\nThe current architecture proposes:\n\nNetwork Privacy = 30%\nTracking Exposure = 25%\nFingerprint Risk = 20%\nLeak Protection = 25%\n\nDo NOT automatically treat these weights as final.\n\nInstead:\n\n1. Define exactly what each category measures.\n2. Define the inputs.\n3. Define how each input is normalized.\n4. Define how deductions/bonuses work.\n5. Explain why each weight is appropriate.\n6. Identify limitations of the score.\n7. Make the score deterministic and reproducible.\n8. Make every score change explainable to the user.\n\nThe system must never generate a random or arbitrary privacy score.\n\nAlso distinguish between:\n\n\"privacy exposure detected\"\n\nand\n\n\"identity confirmed.\"\n\nWe must never claim that fingerprint analysis identifies a real person.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Browser Extension Capability\n\nReview the proposed browser extension architecture.\n\nFor each planned feature, explicitly identify:\n\n* what the browser extension can reliably detect\n* what it can only estimate\n* what browser APIs restrict\n* what cannot be detected reliably\n\nReview:\n\n* tracker detection\n* Canvas fingerprinting\n* WebGL fingerprinting\n* Audio fingerprinting\n* WebRTC exposure\n* third-party requests\n* cookies\n* browser characteristics\n\nDo not promise universal fingerprint detection.\n\nIf something is an estimate, label it as an estimate.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Real-Time Architecture\n\nKeep the Redis + WebSocket architecture, but define exactly what \"real-time\" means.\n\nSpecify:\n\n* event producers\n* event broker\n* event consumers\n* WebSocket authentication\n* event authorization\n* reconnect behavior\n* heartbeat\n* event ordering\n* duplicate events\n* lost events\n* backpressure\n* persistence requirements\n\nThe system must never create fake real-time events.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Production vs Development\n\nCreate two clearly separated architectures:\n\n### Development\n\nSuitable for:\n\n* local Docker development\n* testing\n* one VPN server\n* development credentials\n\n### Production\n\nSuitable for:\n\n* real users\n* real VPN traffic\n* multiple VPN servers\n* TLS\n* secrets management\n* monitoring\n* backups\n* disaster recovery\n* server provisioning\n* server removal\n* security updates\n* key rotation\n\nClearly mark which components are NOT production-ready yet.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Commercial VPN Architecture\n\nThe long-term goal is to operate Privacy Gateway as a real VPN service.\n\nTherefore revise the architecture to account for:\n\n* multiple geographic VPN nodes\n* user/device limits\n* server capacity\n* peer allocation\n* node health\n* node draining\n* maintenance\n* provisioning\n* decommissioning\n* subscription plans\n* payment-provider integration\n* abuse handling\n* account suspension\n* security incidents\n* operational monitoring\n* customer support requirements\n\nDo not claim that these are implemented if they are only architectural plans.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Authentication\n\nKeep Google OAuth/OIDC with PKCE.\n\nHowever, clearly separate:\n\n### Web authentication\n\nBrowser \342\206\222 Google \342\206\222 Backend\n\n### Desktop authentication\n\nDesktop \342\206\222 System Browser \342\206\222 Google \342\206\222 Backend \342\206\222 Desktop\n\nEnsure:\n\n* PKCE\n* state validation\n* redirect URI validation\n* token verification\n* issuer validation\n* audience validation\n* email_verified validation\n* secure session handling\n\nDo not store Google access tokens unless there is a specific requirement.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Secrets and Keys\n\nReview all secret handling.\n\nWireGuard:\n\n* client private key must remain on client\n* server private key must remain securely on server\n* API must never return client private keys\n* logs must never contain private keys\n\nApplication:\n\n* Google OAuth secrets\n* JWT signing keys\n* database credentials\n* Redis credentials\n* TLS certificates\n\nUse a proper secret-management strategy.\n\nDo not put production secrets in Git or Docker images.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Docker Production Security\n\nReview the Docker design.\n\nFor every container specify:\n\n* user\n* capabilities\n* network access\n* filesystem access\n* volumes\n* exposed ports\n* secrets\n* whether it needs root\n* whether it needs host networking\n\nUse:\n\n* least privilege\n* read-only filesystems where practical\n* no-new-privileges\n* dropped capabilities\n* pinned image versions\n* health checks\n* resource limits\n\nDo not use `latest` tags for production dependencies.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Threat Model\n\nExpand the existing STRIDE model.\n\nInclude threats involving:\n\n* compromised desktop client\n* malicious browser extension\n* compromised VPN node\n* compromised API\n* compromised database\n* malicious administrator\n* stolen authentication credentials\n* stolen WireGuard keys\n* cloud provider compromise\n* container escape\n* supply-chain attack\n* DDoS\n* malicious users\n* abuse of the VPN service\n\nFor every threat specify:\n\n* attack\n* impact\n* mitigation\n* residual risk\n\n---\n\n## 12. Important Architectural Principle\n\nThe final architecture must preserve this separation:\n\nCLIENTS\n\342\206\223\nVPN DATA PLANE\n\342\206\223\nINTERNET\n\nand separately:\n\nCLIENTS\n\342\206\223\nCONTROL PLANE\n\342\206\223\nPRIVACY INTELLIGENCE\n\342\206\223\nREAL-TIME EVENTS\n\342\206\223\nDASHBOARD\n\nThe VPN data plane should not automatically become a browsing-history collection system.\n\nPrivacy analysis should use the minimum information necessary.\n\n---\n\n# Required Output\n\nDo NOT write implementation code.\n\nProduce a revised **Phase 0.1 Architecture Specification** containing:\n\n1. Revised architecture diagram\n2. Revised component responsibilities\n3. Revised network flow\n4. Revised data flow\n5. Development architecture\n6. Production architecture\n7. Revised Docker architecture\n8. Revised database model\n9. Authentication model\n10. WireGuard privilege model\n11. Browser-extension capability matrix\n12. Privacy-data model\n13. Privacy-score methodology\n14. Real-time event architecture\n15. Multi-server architecture\n16. Production infrastructure architecture\n17. Expanded threat model\n18. Security boundaries\n19. Known limitations\n20. What is actually implementable now\n21. What requires future infrastructure\n22. Revised development roadmap\n\nAt the end, provide a section:\n\n### \"Changes From Phase 0\"\n\nList every change you made and explain why.\n\nDo NOT proceed to Phase 1.\n\nWait for my approval after producing Phase 0.1."
    }
