  planner_response {
    response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 0.1: REVISED MASTER SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & SECURITY SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Revised Architecture Diagram\n\nPrivacy Gateway enforces a strict structural separation between the **Cryptographic VPN Data Plane** (network transit and routing) and the **Control / Privacy Intelligence Plane** (authentication, peer allocation, telemetry processing, and real-time events).\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                                  CLIENT ECOSYSTEM\n========================================================================================================================\n       +--------------------------------------------------+        +-----------------------------------------+\n       |                  DESKTOP CLIENT                  |        |            BROWSER EXTENSION            |\n       |  +--------------------------------------------+  |        |  +-----------------------------------+  |\n       |  | React / Tailwind UI (Tauri Webview)        |  |        |  | Popup & Diagnostics UI            |  |\n       |  +---------------------+----------------------+  |        |  +-----------------+-----------------+  |\n       |                        | IPC                           |                         | Messaging            |\n       |  +---------------------v----------------------+  |        |  +-----------------v-----------------+  |\n       |  | Tauri / Rust Core                          |  |        |  | Background Service Worker (MV3)   |  |\n       |  |  * Keyring (Client PrivKey Stored Locally) |  |        |  |  * DeclarativeNetRequest Blocker  |  |\n       |  |  * WFP / pf / iptables Killswitch Engine   |  |        |  |  * In-page Heuristic Probers      |  |\n       |  +---------------------+----------------------+  |        |  +-----------------+-----------------+  |\n       +------------------------|-------------------------+        +--------------------|--------------------+\n                                |                                                       |\n        ========================|=======================================================|=======================\n                                | 1. Encrypted WG Tunnel (UDP 51820)                    | 2. Anonymized Telemetry\n                                |    (Zero Data Logging)                                |    (HTTPS / WSS)\n                                v                                                       v\n========================================================================================================================\n                                     DATA PLANE vs. CONTROL PLANE SEPARATION\n========================================================================================================================\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+ +------------------------------------------------------+\n|                 DATA PLANE: EDGE VPN NODES                  | |             CONTROL & INTELLIGENCE PLANE             |\n|                                                             | |                                                      |\n|  [ Linux Host (Bare Metal / Edge VM) ]                      | |  [ Ingress & Edge Proxy (Envoy / TLS 1.3) ]          |\n|    * Linux Kernel 6.x WireGuard Module (`wg0`)              | |                         |                            |\n|    * `nftables` Packet Filtering & NAT Masquerade           | |                         v                            |\n|    * Local CoreDNS (10.66.0.1:53 - Unbound recursive DoT)   | |  [ Core API Service (Node/TypeScript) ]              |\n|                         ^                                   | |    * OIDC / Google OAuth PKCE Auth                   |\n|                         | UNIX Domain Socket (Root/Scoped)  | |    * Device & Peer State Manager                     |\n|  +----------------------+--------------------------------+  | |    * Subscriptions & RBAC                            |\n|  | Node Daemon (`pg-node-daemon`)                        |  | |                         |                            |\n|  |  * Read-only `wgctrl` statistics & Netlink peer sync  |  | |                         v                            |\n|  |  * Drops all privileges after interface binding       |  | |  [ Privacy Intelligence Engine ]                     |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------+  | |    * Deterministic 4-Pillar Scoring Math             |\n|                                                             | |    * Synthetic DNS / WebRTC Diagnostic Processor     |\n|                                                             | |                         |                            |\n|                                                             | |                         v                            |\n|                                                             | |  [ Real-Time Message Bus (Redis Streams / PubSub) ]  |\n|                                                             | |                         |                            |\n|                                                             | |                         v                            |\n|                                                             | |  [ Storage Tier: PostgreSQL 16 (Relational/Audit) ]  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+ +------------------------------------------------------+\n                                |                                                       |\n                                v                                                       v\n                        [ PUBLIC INTERNET ]                             [ REAL-TIME WEBSOCKET DISPATCH ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Revised Component Responsibilities\n\n| Component | Execution Environment | Key Responsibilities | Privilege Level | What It CANNOT Do |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Desktop Client** | Host OS (Win/macOS/Linux) | Local Curve25519 keypair generation, OS routing table modification, WFP/pf firewall killswitch, OAuth loopback server. | User-space UI; scoped helper for routing/WFP | Cannot access other users\' keys or credentials; cannot modify arbitrary network interfaces outside its virtual adapter. |\n| **Browser Extension** | Browser Sandbox (MV3) | Intercepts known tracking domains via `declarativeNetRequest`, executes local Canvas/WebGL entropy measurements, tests WebRTC candidate binding. | Standard browser extension sandbox | Cannot read arbitrary page contents/passwords; cannot intercept traffic outside the browser; cannot claim 100% fingerprint attribution. |\n| **Edge VPN Node** | Linux Host (Host-level) | Ingress WireGuard UDP termination, high-throughput packet routing, IP masquerade via `nftables`, recursive DNS sinkholing via CoreDNS. | Kernel module + Scoped System Daemon | No database access; no user credential store; zero browsing or URL packet inspection. |\n| **Node Daemon** | Linux Host System Service | Manages `wg0` peers over Netlink via UNIX domain socket; monitors RX/TX byte counters and latest handshake timestamps. | System user with `CAP_NET_ADMIN` ONLY | Cannot access user accounts, billing, or central database directly; cannot modify host files outside `/run/wireguard`. |\n| **Core API** | Containerized Service | Google OIDC validation, device session lifecycle, VIP allocation, RBAC policy enforcement, peer public key registry. | Unprivileged (`UID 10001`, `non-root`) | Has no direct access to WireGuard server host kernel; never touches or stores WireGuard private keys. |\n| **Privacy Engine** | Containerized Service | Normalizes diagnostic inputs, executes deterministic scoring algorithm, parses synthetic leak tokens, detects domain classification. | Unprivileged (`UID 10001`, `non-root`) | Cannot inspect user network packets; receives only structured diagnostic telemetry. |\n| **Real-Time Bus (Redis)** | Containerized Service | In-memory pub/sub fanout, stream-based queueing for peer handshakes, rate-limit buckets, and WebSocket session states. | Unprivileged (`UID 999`) | Volatile memory only; no unencrypted secrets persisted to disk. |\n| **PostgreSQL DB** | Managed / Containerized | ACID storage for users, sessions, devices, registered peer public keys, subscriptions, and immutable audit logs. | Database user with strict RLS | No plaintext storage of tokens or keys. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Revised Network Flow\n\n```\n                           [ CLIENT OS / APPLICATION TRAFFIC ]\n                                            |\n                         +------------------v-------------------+\n                         | Desktop Routing Engine / Killswitch  |\n                         | (Default Route: `wg0` Virtual TUN)   |\n                         +------------------+-------------------+\n                                            |\n                         +------------------+-------------------+\n                         |                                      |\n       [ DATA PLANE TRAFFIC ]                         [ CONTROL PLANE TRAFFIC ]\n                 |                                              |\n      Encrypted ChaCha20-Poly1305                     TLS 1.3 Encrypted HTTPS/WSS\n      UDP Port 51820 (WireGuard)                      TCP Port 443 (API Gateway)\n                 |                                              |\n                 v                                              v\n      +----------------------+                       +----------------------+\n      | Edge VPN Node Host   |                       | Central Ingress API  |\n      | (Public IP: 198.51..) |                       | (api.privacygw.net)  |\n      +----------+-----------+                       +----------+-----------+\n                 |                                              |\n     [ nftables Ingress Match ]                      [ Google OIDC / Token Validation ]\n                 |                                              |\n      +----------v-----------+                       +----------v-----------+\n      | wg0 Interface Decrypt|                       | Ingest Diagnostic    |\n      | Allocated VIP:       |                       | Telemetry Payload    |\n      | 10.66.1.42           |                       +----------+-----------+\n      +----------+-----------+                                  |\n                 |                                   [ Re-Evaluate 4-Pillar Score ]\n    +------------+------------+                                 |\n    |                         |                                 v\n[ DNS: 10.66.0.1:53 ]   [ NAT Forward eth0 ]         [ Publish Redis Event Bus ]\n    |                         |                                 |\n[ CoreDNS Sinkhole ]          |                                 v\n    |                         |                      [ Dispatch WSS to Clients ]\n    +------------+------------+\n                 |\n                 v\n        [ PUBLIC INTERNET ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Revised Data Flow\n\n```\n[ Browser / Desktop Prober ]       [ Central Core API ]       [ Privacy Engine ]       [ Redis Bus ]       [ WebSocket Client ]\n            |                               |                         |                      |                    |\n            | 1. Submit Diagnostic Payload  |                         |                      |                    |\n            |    POST /api/v1/telemetry     |                         |                      |                    |\n            |    { dns_probe_token,         |                         |                      |                    |\n            |      webrtc_exposed_ips,      |                         |                      |                    |\n            |      canvas_hash_entropy }    |                         |                      |                    |\n            |------------------------------>|                         |                      |                    |\n            |                               | 2. Validate Schema &    |                      |                    |\n            |                               |    Forward Job          |                      |                    |\n            |                               |------------------------>|                      |                    |\n            |                               |                         | 3. Query Synthetic   |                    |\n            |                               |                         |    DNS Probe Logger  |                    |\n            |                               |                         | 4. Compute 4-Pillar  |                    |\n            |                               |                         |    Deterministic Math|                    |\n            |                               |                         | 5. Generate Reasons  |                    |\n            |                               |                         | 6. Publish Event     |                    |\n            |                               |                         |--------------------->|                    |\n            |                               |                         |                      | 7. Deliver to      |\n            |                               |                         |                      |    User Channels   |\n            |                               |                         |                      |------------------->|\n            |                               |                         |                      |    { score: 84,    |\n            |                               |                         |                      |      deductions:[]}|\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Development Architecture vs. Production Architecture\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\nDEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT (Local Machine / Single Node)\n========================================================================================================================\n* Orchestration: Docker Compose (All services in one bridge network).\n* WireGuard Engine: Containerized using `wireguard-go` or host-mounted `/dev/net/tun` with mock rootless network bridge.\n* Database: Single PostgreSQL 16 container with pre-seeded test fixtures.\n* Cache/Bus: Single standalone Redis container.\n* Ingress: Local reverse proxy on `http://localhost:3000`.\n* DNS Prober: Mock DNS server returning pre-configured lookup responses.\n* Cryptographic Secrets: Static development `.env` credentials with explicit mock banners.\n* Status: **DEVELOPMENT IMPLEMENTATION \342\200\224 NOT PRODUCTION READY**.\n\n========================================================================================================================\nPRODUCTION ENVIRONMENT (High-Availability Cloud / Edge Compute)\n========================================================================================================================\n* Orchestration: Kubernetes / Managed Nomad for Control Plane; Immutable Bare-Metal / Edge VMs for VPN Nodes.\n* WireGuard Engine: Native Linux Kernel 6.x Module (`wireguard.ko`) running directly on edge OS.\n* Daemon: Isolated Go/Rust `pg-node-daemon` running under systemd with strict AppArmor/SELinux profile.\n* Database: Managed High-Availability PostgreSQL Cluster (Multi-AZ, read-replicas, encrypted at rest via AES-256).\n* Cache/Bus: Managed Redis Cluster with TLS encryption and automatic failover.\n* Ingress: Dual-redundant Cloudflare Magic Transit / Envoy API Gateway terminating TLS 1.3 with strict HSTS.\n* DNS Prober: Geographically distributed Authoritative DNS Name Servers (`ns1-ns4.leak.privacygateway.net`).\n* Secrets: HashiCorp Vault / AWS Secrets Manager with dynamic IAM short-lived credential rotation.\n* Status: **TARGET PRODUCTION ARCHITECTURE**.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Detailed Production vs. Development Comparison\n\n| Dimension | Development Environment | Production Architecture |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **WireGuard Execution** | Container with `/dev/net/tun` or userland `wireguard-go` | Native Host Kernel Module with hardware offloading |\n| **Privilege Model** | Docker `CAP_NET_ADMIN` | Bare-metal host systemd service; dropped capabilities |\n| **Server Nodes** | Single loopback server node (`127.0.0.1:51820`) | Multi-region nodes (US, EU, AP, LATAM) with healthchecks |\n| **Secrets Management** | `.env.local` file (Never committed) | HashiCorp Vault / Cloud Secret Manager with KMS encryption |\n| **TLS / Ingress** | Self-signed or local plaintext HTTP proxy | Strict TLS 1.3 (ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256), HSTS |\n| **DNS Resolution** | Containerized CoreDNS with static forwarders | Hardened Unbound + CoreDNS with DNSSEC validation |\n| **Backup & DR** | Ephemeral Docker volumes | Point-in-time recovery (PITR) WAL archiving + hourly encrypted backups |\n| **Monitoring** | Console output & basic Docker stats | Prometheus + Grafana + OpenTelemetry tracing (Zero PII) |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Revised Docker Architecture (Development & Service Containers)\n\nEvery container is explicitly configured according to the Principle of Least Privilege:\n\n```yaml\nversion: \'3.9\'\n\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16.2-alpine\n    container_name: pg_database\n    user: \"postgres\"\n    read_only: true\n    tmpfs:\n      - /tmp\n      - /var/run/postgresql\n    security_opt:\n      - no-new-privileges:true\n    cap_drop:\n      - ALL\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_DB: privacy_gateway\n      POSTGRES_USER_FILE: /run/secrets/db_user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data:rw\n      - ../infrastructure/migrations:/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d:ro\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U `cat /run/secrets/db_user` -d privacy_gateway\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n    deploy:\n      resources:\n        limits:\n          cpus: \'1.0\'\n          memory: 512M\n\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7.2-alpine\n    container_name: pg_redis\n    user: \"999:999\"\n    read_only: true\n    security_opt:\n      - no-new-privileges:true\n    cap_drop:\n      - ALL\n    command: [\"redis-server\", \"--appendonly\", \"yes\", \"--requirepass\", \"${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}\"]\n    volumes:\n      - redisdata:/data:rw\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"redis-cli\", \"ping\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  api:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/api/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_api\n    user: \"10001:10001\" # non-root unprivileged node user\n    read_only: true\n    tmpfs:\n      - /tmp\n    security_opt:\n      - no-new-privileges:true\n    cap_drop:\n      - ALL\n    depends_on:\n      postgres:\n        condition: service_healthy\n      redis:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    environment:\n      NODE_ENV: production\n      PORT: 3000\n    ports:\n      - \"3000:3000\"\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n      - pg-public-net\n\n  privacy-engine:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/privacy-engine/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_privacy_engine\n    user: \"10001:10001\"\n    read_only: true\n    tmpfs:\n      - /tmp\n    security_opt:\n      - no-new-privileges:true\n    cap_drop:\n      - ALL\n    depends_on:\n      - redis\n      - postgres\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\n  redisdata:\n\nnetworks:\n  pg-internal-net:\n    internal: true\n  pg-public-net:\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Revised Database Model (PostgreSQL 16)\n\n```sql\n-- Security Extensions\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS \"uuid-ossp\";\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS \"pgcrypto\";\n\n-- Enums\nCREATE TYPE user_role AS ENUM (\'user\', \'admin\', \'auditor\');\nCREATE TYPE auth_provider AS ENUM (\'google\', \'email_password\', \'github\', \'apple\');\nCREATE TYPE subscription_tier AS ENUM (\'free\', \'premium\', \'business\');\nCREATE TYPE subscription_status AS ENUM (\'active\', \'past_due\', \'canceled\', \'trialing\');\nCREATE TYPE peer_status AS ENUM (\'active\', \'revoked\', \'dormant\');\nCREATE TYPE leak_verdict AS ENUM (\'safe\', \'warning\', \'leak_detected\');\nCREATE TYPE risk_level AS ENUM (\'low\', \'medium\', \'high\');\n\n-- 1. Users Table\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    display_name VARCHAR(100),\n    avatar_url TEXT,\n    role user_role DEFAULT \'user\' NOT NULL,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE NOT NULL,\n    data_retention_days INTEGER DEFAULT 30 NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 2. Authentication Identities (OIDC / OAuth Accounts)\nCREATE TABLE authentication_accounts (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    provider auth_provider NOT NULL,\n    provider_subject_id VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    email_at_provider VARCHAR(255),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    UNIQUE(provider, provider_subject_id)\n);\n\n-- 3. User Sessions (Refresh Tokens)\nCREATE TABLE user_sessions (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    refresh_token_hash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    user_agent TEXT,\n    client_ip_anonymized INET, -- Truncated to /24 (IPv4) or /48 (IPv6)\n    expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    revoked_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 4. Registered Devices\nCREATE TABLE devices (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    device_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\n    client_version VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\n    is_revoked BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL,\n    last_active_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 5. VPN Server Nodes\nCREATE TABLE vpn_servers (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    node_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    country_code VARCHAR(2) NOT NULL,\n    city VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    public_endpoint VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    wireguard_public_key VARCHAR(44) NOT NULL,\n    ipv4_subnet CIDR NOT NULL,\n    ipv6_subnet CIDR,\n    capacity_peers INTEGER DEFAULT 250 NOT NULL,\n    current_active_peers INTEGER DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE NOT NULL,\n    is_maintenance BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL,\n    health_status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT \'healthy\' NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 6. WireGuard Peers (Public keys ONLY)\nCREATE TABLE wireguard_peers (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    vpn_server_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES vpn_servers(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,\n    public_key VARCHAR(44) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    allocated_ipv4 INET UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    allocated_ipv6 INET UNIQUE,\n    status peer_status DEFAULT \'active\' NOT NULL,\n    total_rx_bytes BIGINT DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    total_tx_bytes BIGINT DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    last_handshake_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 7. Subscriptions & Billing\nCREATE TABLE subscriptions (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    tier subscription_tier DEFAULT \'free\' NOT NULL,\n    status subscription_status DEFAULT \'active\' NOT NULL,\n    payment_provider VARCHAR(50),\n    external_subscription_id VARCHAR(255),\n    max_devices INTEGER DEFAULT 2 NOT NULL,\n    current_period_start TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    current_period_end TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    canceled_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 8. Privacy Diagnostic Scans (Aggregated Snapshots)\nCREATE TABLE privacy_scans (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    overall_score INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (overall_score BETWEEN 0 AND 100),\n    network_privacy_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    tracking_exposure_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    fingerprint_risk_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    leak_protection_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    dns_leak_verdict leak_verdict NOT NULL,\n    webrtc_leak_verdict leak_verdict NOT NULL,\n    trackers_blocked_total INTEGER DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    scan_explanation JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 9. Privacy Events Stream (Configurable User Retention)\nCREATE TABLE privacy_events (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    event_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\n    severity VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\n    description TEXT NOT NULL,\n    metadata JSONB DEFAULT \'{}\'::JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 10. Audit Logs (Immutable Security Log)\nCREATE TABLE audit_logs (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    actor_user_id UUID REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    action VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    target_resource VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    target_id UUID,\n    ip_address_anonymized INET,\n    metadata JSONB DEFAULT \'{}\'::JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Optimization & Partition Indexes\nCREATE INDEX idx_user_sessions_lookup ON user_sessions(user_id, expires_at);\nCREATE INDEX idx_wg_peers_server_active ON wireguard_peers(vpn_server_id, status);\nCREATE INDEX idx_privacy_scans_user_time ON privacy_scans(user_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_privacy_events_user_time ON privacy_events(user_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_audit_logs_actor_time ON audit_logs(actor_user_id, created_at DESC);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Authentication Model & Separation (Google OAuth / OIDC PKCE)\n\n### A. Web Flow vs. Desktop Flow Separation\n\n```\n[ WEB AUTHENTICATION FLOW ]\n1. User clicks \"Sign in with Google\" on Web UI.\n2. Web App generates cryptographically random `state` + `code_verifier` (stored in secure `sessionStorage`).\n3. Derives `code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier))`.\n4. Browser redirects to:\n   https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?\n     client_id=...&response_type=code&scope=openid%20email%20profile&\n     redirect_uri=https://app.privacygateway.net/auth/callback&\n     state=...&code_challenge=...&code_challenge_method=S256\n5. Google returns `code` to Web UI callback.\n6. Web UI POSTs to backend `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` with `{ code, code_verifier, state }`.\n\n[ DESKTOP CLIENT AUTHENTICATION FLOW (RFC 8252 Loopback) ]\n1. Desktop client binds a temporary local HTTP loopback server on `http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>/callback`.\n2. Rust Core generates `code_verifier` and `code_challenge` (S256).\n3. Opens default system OS browser to Google Auth endpoint with `redirect_uri=http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>/callback`.\n4. Browser redirects to local loopback server after login.\n5. Desktop client loopback catches `code`, closes local socket, and POSTs `{ code, code_verifier }` to backend `/api/v1/auth/google/callback`.\n```\n\n### B. Backend Cryptographic Verification Checklist\nEvery OIDC assertion must pass all of the following validations:\n1. **Exchange Code**: Server exchanges code directly with `https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token` using TLS.\n2. **Signature Verification**: Validates JWT signature of `id_token` against Google\342\200\231s published JWKS (`https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs`).\n3. **Issuer Assertion**: `iss` must equal `https://accounts.google.com` or `accounts.google.com`.\n4. **Audience Assertion**: `aud` must match the application\'s registered `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`.\n5. **Freshness**: Current timestamp must be before `exp` and after `iat`.\n6. **Email Verification**: `email_verified` boolean must be explicitly `true`.\n7. **Zero Google Access Token Storage**: The Google access token is discarded immediately after identity validation; only the Privacy Gateway session JWT is issued.\n\n---\n\n## 10. WireGuard Security & Privilege Model: Container vs. Host Analysis\n\n### Architectural Trade-off Evaluation:\n\n| Criterion | Option A: Host-Level Kernel Execution with Daemon | Option B: Privileged Docker Container |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Privilege Scope** | **Narrowly Scoped**: Daemon runs with single `CAP_NET_ADMIN` capability via systemd. | **Broad/Dangerous**: Container requires `--privileged`, `--cap-add=NET_ADMIN`, `--cap-add=SYS_MODULE`, and `/lib/modules` host bind-mount. |\n| **Container Escape Risk** | **Zero**: No Docker daemon involved in kernel networking path. | **High**: A compromised container with `SYS_MODULE` can load arbitrary kernel rootkits onto the host. |\n| **Performance / Throughput** | **Native**: 10+ Gbps kernel packet processing with zero virtualization overhead. | **Degraded**: Virtual network bridge and namespace crossing introduce 15\342\200\22325% throughput penalty. |\n| **Blast Radius** | API application is on a separate VM/container with no access to the WireGuard host. | If API and WG container share a host, Docker socket compromise grants full host root. |\n\n### Production Recommendation: **Option A (Host-Level Kernel Module with Scoped Systemd Daemon)**\n- The WireGuard data plane runs in the host Linux kernel (`wg0`).\n- The `pg-node-daemon` is a standalone Go/Rust binary that listens **only on a local UNIX domain socket** (`/run/wireguard/daemon.sock`) with file permissions `0600` owned by `wgdaemon:wgdaemon`.\n- The daemon communicates with the central API over mTLS (Mutual TLS 1.3) using dedicated client certificates.\n- The Central API has **zero root access** and cannot execute arbitrary shell commands on edge nodes.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Browser Extension Capability Matrix\n\n| Feature / Surface | What Can Be Reliably Detected | What Is an Estimate / Heuristic | What Browser APIs Restrict | What CANNOT Be Detected |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Tracker Detection** | Known third-party request URLs matching EasyPrivacy / Disconnect lists via `declarativeNetRequest`. | Classification category (Advertising vs. Analytics vs. Fingerprinter). | Cannot inspect encrypted request payloads or WebSockets frames content in MV3. | First-party tracking scripts hosted on the same root domain (CNAME cloaking detection is heuristic only). |\n| **Canvas Fingerprinting** | Read-back API calls (`toDataURL`, `getImageData`) executed by background scripts without user action. | Uniqueness score of the generated Canvas hash against statistical cohorts. | Cannot determine if Canvas usage is benign (e.g. video rendering) vs. tracking without behavioral heuristics. | Server-side image generation or passive CSS canvas measurements. |\n| **WebGL Fingerprinting** | Access to unmasked GPU renderer strings (`UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL`). | Hardware entropy contribution to overall device uniqueness. | Chrome restricts blocking GPU queries without breaking 3D WebGL page rendering. | Underlying physical GPU clock drift or microarchitectural side-channels. |\n| **Audio Fingerprinting** | Calls to `OfflineAudioContext` and `AnalyserNode` frequency buffer rendering. | Audio rendering uniqueness estimation. | Cannot alter audio buffer values without causing audible artifacts in valid media. | Passive acoustic tracking via external device microphones. |\n| **WebRTC Exposure** | Local private IP addresses (e.g. `192.168.x.x`) and public STUN candidate IP reflection. | Whether candidate represents an active VPN bypass or valid local interface. | Chrome `webrtcIPHandlingPolicy` permits restricting candidates to default route. | Cannot inspect WebRTC end-to-end encrypted media payload contents. |\n\n*Disclaimer Labeling*: All extension fingerprint cards must explicitly display the label **\"HEURISTIC ESTIMATE \342\200\224 Does not identify individual human identity\"**.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Technical Privacy & Data-Retention Model\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                     TECHNICAL PRIVACY & RETENTION SCHEDULE\n========================================================================================================================\n\nCATEGORY 1: DATA WE INTENTIONALLY DO NOT COLLECT OR RECORD\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* User Browsing History: We do not log destination IP addresses, visited hostnames, or full URLs.\n* Traffic Payloads: WireGuard tunnel packets pass through Linux kernel memory and are immediately forwarded; no packet capture.\n* DNS Query Logs: CoreDNS runs with in-memory logging disabled; query history is never written to disk.\n* WireGuard Private Keys: Generated strictly on the client; the API accepts only 32-byte Base64 Public Keys.\n* Plaintext Passwords / Credentials: We use OIDC token verification; passwords never touch our servers.\n\nCATEGORY 2: OPERATIONAL DATA REQUIRED TO OPERATE THE SERVICE\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* Account Information (Email, Display Name, User ID):\n  - Storage: PostgreSQL `users` table.\n  - Retention: Maintained while account is active; purged within 72 hours of user account deletion.\n* WireGuard Public Key & Allocated VIP:\n  - Storage: PostgreSQL `wireguard_peers` table + Edge Node active peer table.\n  - Retention: Maintained while peer is registered; instantly purged upon peer revocation.\n* Aggregate Bandwidth (Total RX / TX Bytes):\n  - Storage: Monotonic byte counter in `wireguard_peers`.\n  - Retention: Aggregated for capacity planning; reset monthly.\n* Operational Node Health (CPU, RAM, Active Peer Count):\n  - Storage: Prometheus time-series metrics (Aggregated across node, zero user identifiers).\n  - Retention: 14 days rolling window.\n\nCATEGORY 3: PRIVACY INTELLIGENCE & DIAGNOSTIC DATA (USER CONFIGURABLE)\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* Tracker Block Events (Domain Hash, Category, Timestamp):\n  - Storage: PostgreSQL `privacy_events`.\n  - Retention: Configurable by user (24 Hours / 7 Days / 30 Days / Immediate Purge).\n* Diagnostic Test Snapshots (Score, DNS Leak Verdict, WebRTC Verdict):\n  - Storage: PostgreSQL `privacy_scans`.\n  - Retention: User-configurable; deleted on demand.\n\nTHIRD-PARTY VISIBILITY DISCLOSURE\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* Edge VPN Server Host: Sees incoming client IP and encrypted WireGuard UDP packets. Cannot see unencrypted contents.\n* Cloud Infrastructure Provider (AWS/Hetzner/GCP): Sees aggregate node ingress/egress network flows.\n* DNS Upstream Resolver: Sees recursive queries originating from the VPN Server IP (Not the client\'s residential IP).\n* Authentication Provider (Google): Knows you authenticated with Privacy Gateway; has no visibility into VPN usage.\n* Service Administrators: Can see active account emails, registered public keys, and aggregate server loads; CANNOT see browsing history.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13. Deterministic Privacy Score Methodology\n\nThe Privacy Score is an explainable 100-point integer computed from 4 weighted pillars:\n\n$$\\text{PrivacyScore} = (0.30 \\times S_{\\text{network}}) + (0.25 \\times S_{\\text{tracking}}) + (0.20 \\times S_{\\text{fingerprint}}) + (0.25 \\times S_{\\text{leak}})$$\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. NETWORK PRIVACY PILLAR (Weight: 30% | Base: 100 Points)                                                           |\n| Inputs:                                                                                                              |\n|   * WireGuard Tunnel State: Connected (+50), Disconnected (-50)                                                      |\n|   * DNS Encryption Status: Verified Internal DoT (+30), Plaintext / External (-30)                                   |\n|   * Server Node Trust & Attestation: Active Node in Good Health (+20), Unverified (-20)                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 2. TRACKING EXPOSURE PILLAR (Weight: 25% | Base: 100 Points)                                                         |\n| Inputs:                                                                                                              |\n|   * Tracker Blocking Efficiency: (Trackers Blocked / Total Trackers Encountered) * 100                               |\n|   * Deductions: High-risk CNAME-cloaked trackers detected (-15 per unblocked instance)                               |\n|   * Deductions: Unblocked third-party analytics scripts (-10 per script)                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 3. FINGERPRINT RISK PILLAR (Weight: 20% | Base: 100 Points)                                                          |\n| Inputs:                                                                                                              |\n|   * Canvas Hash Entropy: Unique hash in top 5% uniqueness (-30), Common cohort (+20)                                 |\n|   * WebGL Unmasked GPU Renderer exposed (-20)                                                                        |\n|   * AudioContext frequency buffer fingerprinting detected (-20)                                                      |\n|   * Standard User-Agent & Language headers matched (+30)                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 4. LEAK PROTECTION PILLAR (Weight: 25% | Base: 100 Points)                                                           |\n| Inputs:                                                                                                              |\n|   * DNS Leak Test: SAFE (+50), LEAK DETECTED (-50)                                                                   |\n|   * WebRTC Binding Test: SAFE (+50), EXPOSED Residential IP (-50)                                                    |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n*Explainability Guarantee*: Every score output returns a structured `explanation` array listing exact point deductions and remediation steps (e.g. `{\"pillar\": \"Leak Protection\", \"deduction\": -50, \"reason\": \"WebRTC STUN query exposed residential IP 203.0.113.4\"}`).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Real-Time Event Bus & WebSocket Architecture\n\n```\n                                      [ REAL-TIME EVENT PIPELINE ]\n\n+-----------------------+     +------------------------+     +------------------------+     +------------------------+\n|  Node Daemon Handshake|     | Extension Tracker Event|     | Desktop Prober Test    |     | API Billing Change     |\n+-----------+-----------+     +-----------+------------+     +-----------+------------+     +-----------+------------+\n            |                             |                              |                              |\n            v                             v                              v                              v\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                    REDIS STREAMS PRODUCER (`pg:stream:events`)                                     |\n|  * Message Schema: { id: \"1771720000-0\", user_id: \"uuid\", type: \"WG_HANDSHAKE\", payload: {...}, trace_id: \"...\" }   |\n+-------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                                  |\n                                                  v\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                REDIS CONSUMER GROUP (`pg-event-dispatchers`)                                       |\n|  * Acknowledges messages (`XACK`) to prevent lost events                                                           |\n|  * Dispatches to Redis Pub/Sub User Channel (`pg:channel:user:<user_id>`)                                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                                  |\n                                                  v\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                     WEBSOCKET SERVER (Node.js Cluster)                                             |\n|                                                                                                                    |\n|  * Authenticates WebSocket handshake via Bearer JWT query parameter or Authorization header                        |\n|  * Subscribes socket to Redis channel `pg:channel:user:<user_id>`                                                  |\n|  * Heartbeat: Ping/Pong frame sent every 25 seconds; terminates dead sockets after 60s timeout                     |\n|  * Backpressure: High-watermark buffer dropped if client socket is stalled (>64KB buffer)                          |\n|  * Reconnect Handling: Client includes `last_event_id`; server replays missed events from Redis Stream              |\n+-------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                                  |\n                         +------------------------+------------------------+\n                         |                                                 |\n                         v                                                 v\n             [ Web Dashboard React UI ]                         [ Desktop Client GUI ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 15. Multi-Server Node Lifecycle & Geographic Topology\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                           SERVER NODE LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT                                         |\n|                                                                                                                    |\n|  1. PROVISIONING           2. ACTIVE SERVING            3. DRAINING / MAINTENANCE        4. DECOMMISSIONING        |\n|  +--------------------+    +--------------------+       +--------------------+           +--------------------+    |\n|  | * Terraform spins  |    | * Heartbeat healthy|       | * `is_maintenance` |           | * Revoke all peer  |    |\n|  |   up node instance |    | * Low latency      |       |   set to TRUE      |           |   public keys      |    |\n|  | * mTLS certs issued|--->| * Accepts new peer |--->   | * No new peers     |---------->| * Zero disk wipe   |    |\n|  | * WireGuard pubkey |    |   allocations      |       |   allocated        |           | * Destroy cloud    |    |\n|  |   registered to DB |    | * Netlink sync     |       | * WS notifies peers|           |   compute instance |    |\n|  +--------------------+    +--------------------+       |   to switch node   |           +--------------------+    |\n|                                                         +--------------------+                                     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n### Geographic Node Distribution Plan:\n1. **US-East** (Ashburn, VA) \342\200\224 `us-east.privacygateway.net` (CIDR: `10.66.1.0/24`)\n2. **EU-Central** (Frankfurt, DE) \342\200\224 `eu-central.privacygateway.net` (CIDR: `10.66.2.0/24`)\n3. **AP-South** (Singapore, SG) \342\200\224 `ap-south.privacygateway.net` (CIDR: `10.66.3.0/24`)\n4. **IN-West** (Mumbai, IN) \342\200\224 `in-west.privacygateway.net` (CIDR: `10.66.4.0/24`)\n\n---\n\n## 16. Production Infrastructure Architecture\n\n```\n[ Edge Cloud / AWS / Hetzner ]\n  \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 VPC 1: Control Plane (Private Subnets)\n  \342\224\202     \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 EKS / Managed Nomad Cluster (API, Privacy Engine, Worker pods)\n  \342\224\202     \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Managed PostgreSQL (Multi-AZ, Encrypted EBS volumes)\n  \342\224\202     \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Managed Redis Cluster (In-memory, Auth enabled)\n  \342\224\202     \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Ingress Envoy Gateway (Public Subnet, Port 443 only)\n  \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 VPC 2..N: Geographic Edge Nodes (Isolated VPCs per region)\n        \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Linux Kernel 6.x Bare-Metal Instances (Port 51820 UDP Ingress)\n        \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Isolated CoreDNS Resolver Container (Port 53 local UDP only)\n        \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Scoped `pg-node-daemon` connecting back to Control Plane via mTLS\n```\n\n---\n\n## 17. Expanded Threat Model (STRIDE Matrix)\n\n| Threat & Vector | Target Component | Attack Description | Impact | Technical Mitigation | Residual Risk |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Compromised Desktop Client** | Control Plane API | Malicious user reverse-engineers desktop app and sends malformed telemetry/peer requests. | DoS on API or attempts to register invalid public keys. | Strict JWT Bearer validation, rate-limiting per User ID (Redis token bucket), and strict schema validation with Zod. | User can always modify local client UI state on their own machine. |\n| **Malicious Browser Extension** | Privacy Engine | Malicious extension injects fabricated tracker events. | Distorted privacy scores. | Extension signs telemetry with temporary device-bound session token; anomaly detection on event frequencies. | Compromised browser can report false local DOM heuristics. |\n| **Compromised VPN Edge Node** | WireGuard Node | Attacker gains root on an edge node. | Can monitor transit metadata for connected peers on that specific node. | Node has zero user accounts or DB passwords. All storage is ephemeral memory. Other nodes in different regions are isolated. | Traffic traversing the compromised node during the intrusion window could be metadata-profiled. |\n| **Compromised Database** | PostgreSQL DB | SQL injection or unauthorized DB access. | Data breach of account records and peer public keys. | Parametrized SQL queries, zero plaintext passwords, zero private keys stored, AES-256 encrypted at rest. | Stored email addresses and device names exposed. |\n| **Malicious Administrator** | Control Plane | Rogue employee accesses customer data. | Attempts to inspect user activity. | Audit logs are write-only and shipped to immutable append-only storage (WORM bucket); zero browsing logs exist to inspect. | Admin can revoke accounts or force subscription tier updates. |\n| **Stolen WireGuard Private Key** | Client Device | Attacker extracts private key from user laptop. | Attacker can connect to VPN as that peer until revoked. | Keys stored in OS secure enclave (Keychain/DPAPI). User can click \"Revoke Device\" from web dashboard instantly. | Attacker has VPN access until revocation timestamp. |\n| **DDoS on VPN UDP Port** | Edge Node | High-bandwidth UDP flood against port 51820. | Service disruption on single node. | WireGuard built-in BLAKE2s cookie protocol for cryptographic handshake rate-limiting; BGP Anycast scrubbing. | Extreme volumetric attacks can saturate uplink bandwidth. |\n| **VPN Service Abuse (Spam/Botnet)** | Exit Traffic | Malicious user uses VPN for cyberattacks or spam. | Server IP blocklisted by external CDNs/services. | Outbound SMTP port 25 blocked by default; connection-rate anomalies trigger automated peer suspension. | Potential blacklisting of specific exit IPs by aggressive third-party firewalls. |\n\n---\n\n## 18. Security Boundaries & Enclaves\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| TRUST BOUNDARY 1: BROWSER / CLIENT USER-SPACE                                                     |\n| * Untrusted inputs: DOM events, WebExtension scripts, local user filesystem.                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                      | HTTPS / WSS / gRPC (Strict Schema Validation)\n                                      v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| TRUST BOUNDARY 2: CENTRAL API & CONTROL PLANE                                                      |\n| * Authenticated zone: Validated JWT Bearers, RBAC enforcement, parameterized SQL operations.      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                      | Mutual TLS 1.3 (Client Certificate Authentication)\n                                      v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| TRUST BOUNDARY 3: EDGE NODE DAEMON & WIREGUARD KERNEL                                              |\n| * Cryptographic zone: Curve25519 authentication, netlink isolation, zero database connection.       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 19. Known Technical Limitations\n\n1. **Browser Extension Manifest V3 Limitations**: Extension cannot inspect WebSocket frames or dynamically rewritten WebRTC streams inside WebWorkers on certain Chromium builds.\n2. **Kernel WireGuard Driver Installation**: On corporate or restricted Windows workstations, installing the `wintun` virtual adapter driver requires initial Administrator approval.\n3. **Synthetic Leak Detection Resolution**: Synthetic DNS subdomain testing requires public DNS resolution latency (~100\342\200\223300ms) before the verdict is marked verified.\n4. **Residential IP Spoofing Prevention**: While WireGuard prevents IP spoofing inside the tunnel (`AllowedIPs`), an ISP can always observe that an encrypted UDP connection is occurring between the user and the VPN server IP.\n\n---\n\n## 20. What Is Actually Implementable Now (Phase 1\342\200\2234)\n\n1. **Complete Monorepo & TypeScript Ecosystem** (API, Shared Types, React Web Dashboard).\n2. **PostgreSQL Relational Schema & Migration Pipeline** with Row-Level Security.\n3. **Google OAuth 2.0 / OIDC with PKCE Verification** for both Web and Desktop flows.\n4. **WireGuard Peer Lifecycle Management Engine** (Key generation, Subnet IP allocation, configuration formatting).\n5. **Deterministic 4-Pillar Privacy Scoring & Diagnostics Evaluator**.\n6. **Real-Time WebSocket Ingestion Bus & Event Broadcaster** (Redis Streams + Socket.IO).\n7. **Local Docker Environment** running with non-root security profiles.\n\n---\n\n## 21. What Requires Future Infrastructure (Phase 12+)\n\n1. **Multi-Region Bare-Metal Edge Node Deployments** (Requires dedicated cloud provider accounts in US, EU, AP).\n2. **Production Authoritative DNS Server Fleet** (`ns1.leak.privacygateway.net` binding for synthetic leak tests).\n3. **Stripe / Payment Gateway Live Merchant Integration** (Requires active commercial business credentials).\n4. **Code-Signing Certificates** (Apple Developer ID & Microsoft Authenticode for seamless desktop client installation).\n\n---\n\n## 22. Revised Incremental Development Roadmap\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 0.1: REVISED MASTER SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & SECURITY SPECIFICATION (CURRENT)                  |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 1: Monorepo Setup, Docker Orchestration, and PostgreSQL Database Migrations                 |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 2: OpenID Connect & Google OAuth 2.0 PKCE Authentication (Web + Desktop Handshakes)         |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 3: WireGuard Server Node Controller & Netlink Peer Management Module                         |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 4: Core REST API & Real-Time WebSocket Event Bus (Redis Streams Ingestion)                  |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 5: Desktop Client Core (Tauri / Rust Engine + Virtual Adapter & Killswitch)                 |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 6: Browser Privacy Extension (Manifest V3 Tracker Blocking & Heuristic Engine)              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 7: Deterministic Privacy Scoring Engine & Synthetic Leak Prober Engine                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 8: Production Web Management Dashboard & Admin Operations Center                            |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 9: End-to-End Integration, Automated Test Suites & Security Hardening Audits                |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## Changes From Phase 0\n\n1. **WireGuard Privilege Architecture Overhaul**: Replaced the initial privileged Docker container concept with a **Host-Level Linux Kernel Module + Scoped Systemd Daemon** communicating over a protected local UNIX socket with mTLS, removing `SYS_MODULE` and host-root escape risks.\n2. **Technically Precise Privacy & Logging Specification**: Replaced the informal \"zero logs guarantee\" with an exhaustive, 3-category technical disclosure clarifying exactly what is never logged, what is required for operations, and what third parties (ISPs, Google, Cloud providers) can observe.\n3. **Deterministic & Explainable 4-Pillar Privacy Scoring Formula**: Formulated explicit mathematical weighting, normalization inputs, penalty deductions, and mandatory explanation payloads, explicitly distinguishing heuristic exposure from identity confirmation.\n4. **Browser Extension Realism & Limitation Matrix**: Explicitly defined the boundaries of WebExtension MV3 APIs, clarifying that Canvas/WebGL/Audio measurements are statistical heuristics and forbidding any claims of 100% fingerprint attribution.\n5. **Hardened Google OIDC / PKCE Dual-Flow Architecture**: Separated standard Web redirects from Desktop RFC 8252 local loopback authentication, ensuring strict ID token signature verification against Google JWKS with zero storage of Google access tokens.\n6. **Robust Real-Time Redis Streams & WebSocket Delivery**: Added consumer groups (`XACK`), message sequence ordering, heartbeat timeouts, and backpressure policies to prevent lost or fake real-time events.\n7. **Strict Non-Root Docker & Production Hardening**: Pinned all container versions, enforced `read_only` filesystems, added `cap_drop: ALL`, dropped all `latest` tags, and isolated internal networks from public ingress.\n8. **Commercial Multi-Server Lifecycle Design**: Added node draining, maintenance transitions, peer capacity counters, and regional failover specifications.\n\n---\n\n### Architectural Review Confirmation\n\nThis revised **Phase 0.1 Architecture Specification** resolves all privilege boundaries, mathematical scoring ambiguities, privacy disclosures, and operational constraints.\n\nPlease review and provide your approval so we may proceed to **Phase 1: Monorepo Setup, Docker Orchestration, and PostgreSQL Database Migrations**."
    modified_response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 0.1: REVISED MASTER SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & SECURITY SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Revised Architecture Diagram\n\nPrivacy Gateway enforces a strict structural separation between the **Cryptographic VPN Data Plane** (network transit and routing) and the **Control / Privacy Intelligence Plane** (authentication, peer allocation, telemetry processing, and real-time events).\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                                  CLIENT ECOSYSTEM\n========================================================================================================================\n       +--------------------------------------------------+        +-----------------------------------------+\n       |                  DESKTOP CLIENT                  |        |            BROWSER EXTENSION            |\n       |  +--------------------------------------------+  |        |  +-----------------------------------+  |\n       |  | React / Tailwind UI (Tauri Webview)        |  |        |  | Popup & Diagnostics UI            |  |\n       |  +---------------------+----------------------+  |        |  +-----------------+-----------------+  |\n       |                        | IPC                           |                         | Messaging            |\n       |  +---------------------v----------------------+  |        |  +-----------------v-----------------+  |\n       |  | Tauri / Rust Core                          |  |        |  | Background Service Worker (MV3)   |  |\n       |  |  * Keyring (Client PrivKey Stored Locally) |  |        |  |  * DeclarativeNetRequest Blocker  |  |\n       |  |  * WFP / pf / iptables Killswitch Engine   |  |        |  |  * In-page Heuristic Probers      |  |\n       |  +---------------------+----------------------+  |        |  +-----------------+-----------------+  |\n       +------------------------|-------------------------+        +--------------------|--------------------+\n                                |                                                       |\n        ========================|=======================================================|=======================\n                                | 1. Encrypted WG Tunnel (UDP 51820)                    | 2. Anonymized Telemetry\n                                |    (Zero Data Logging)                                |    (HTTPS / WSS)\n                                v                                                       v\n========================================================================================================================\n                                     DATA PLANE vs. CONTROL PLANE SEPARATION\n========================================================================================================================\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+ +------------------------------------------------------+\n|                 DATA PLANE: EDGE VPN NODES                  | |             CONTROL & INTELLIGENCE PLANE             |\n|                                                             | |                                                      |\n|  [ Linux Host (Bare Metal / Edge VM) ]                      | |  [ Ingress & Edge Proxy (Envoy / TLS 1.3) ]          |\n|    * Linux Kernel 6.x WireGuard Module (`wg0`)              | |                         |                            |\n|    * `nftables` Packet Filtering & NAT Masquerade           | |                         v                            |\n|    * Local CoreDNS (10.66.0.1:53 - Unbound recursive DoT)   | |  [ Core API Service (Node/TypeScript) ]              |\n|                         ^                                   | |    * OIDC / Google OAuth PKCE Auth                   |\n|                         | UNIX Domain Socket (Root/Scoped)  | |    * Device & Peer State Manager                     |\n|  +----------------------+--------------------------------+  | |    * Subscriptions & RBAC                            |\n|  | Node Daemon (`pg-node-daemon`)                        |  | |                         |                            |\n|  |  * Read-only `wgctrl` statistics & Netlink peer sync  |  | |                         v                            |\n|  |  * Drops all privileges after interface binding       |  | |  [ Privacy Intelligence Engine ]                     |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------+  | |    * Deterministic 4-Pillar Scoring Math             |\n|                                                             | |    * Synthetic DNS / WebRTC Diagnostic Processor     |\n|                                                             | |                         |                            |\n|                                                             | |                         v                            |\n|                                                             | |  [ Real-Time Message Bus (Redis Streams / PubSub) ]  |\n|                                                             | |                         |                            |\n|                                                             | |                         v                            |\n|                                                             | |  [ Storage Tier: PostgreSQL 16 (Relational/Audit) ]  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+ +------------------------------------------------------+\n                                |                                                       |\n                                v                                                       v\n                        [ PUBLIC INTERNET ]                             [ REAL-TIME WEBSOCKET DISPATCH ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Revised Component Responsibilities\n\n| Component | Execution Environment | Key Responsibilities | Privilege Level | What It CANNOT Do |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Desktop Client** | Host OS (Win/macOS/Linux) | Local Curve25519 keypair generation, OS routing table modification, WFP/pf firewall killswitch, OAuth loopback server. | User-space UI; scoped helper for routing/WFP | Cannot access other users\' keys or credentials; cannot modify arbitrary network interfaces outside its virtual adapter. |\n| **Browser Extension** | Browser Sandbox (MV3) | Intercepts known tracking domains via `declarativeNetRequest`, executes local Canvas/WebGL entropy measurements, tests WebRTC candidate binding. | Standard browser extension sandbox | Cannot read arbitrary page contents/passwords; cannot intercept traffic outside the browser; cannot claim 100% fingerprint attribution. |\n| **Edge VPN Node** | Linux Host (Host-level) | Ingress WireGuard UDP termination, high-throughput packet routing, IP masquerade via `nftables`, recursive DNS sinkholing via CoreDNS. | Kernel module + Scoped System Daemon | No database access; no user credential store; zero browsing or URL packet inspection. |\n| **Node Daemon** | Linux Host System Service | Manages `wg0` peers over Netlink via UNIX domain socket; monitors RX/TX byte counters and latest handshake timestamps. | System user with `CAP_NET_ADMIN` ONLY | Cannot access user accounts, billing, or central database directly; cannot modify host files outside `/run/wireguard`. |\n| **Core API** | Containerized Service | Google OIDC validation, device session lifecycle, VIP allocation, RBAC policy enforcement, peer public key registry. | Unprivileged (`UID 10001`, `non-root`) | Has no direct access to WireGuard server host kernel; never touches or stores WireGuard private keys. |\n| **Privacy Engine** | Containerized Service | Normalizes diagnostic inputs, executes deterministic scoring algorithm, parses synthetic leak tokens, detects domain classification. | Unprivileged (`UID 10001`, `non-root`) | Cannot inspect user network packets; receives only structured diagnostic telemetry. |\n| **Real-Time Bus (Redis)** | Containerized Service | In-memory pub/sub fanout, stream-based queueing for peer handshakes, rate-limit buckets, and WebSocket session states. | Unprivileged (`UID 999`) | Volatile memory only; no unencrypted secrets persisted to disk. |\n| **PostgreSQL DB** | Managed / Containerized | ACID storage for users, sessions, devices, registered peer public keys, subscriptions, and immutable audit logs. | Database user with strict RLS | No plaintext storage of tokens or keys. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Revised Network Flow\n\n```\n                           [ CLIENT OS / APPLICATION TRAFFIC ]\n                                            |\n                         +------------------v-------------------+\n                         | Desktop Routing Engine / Killswitch  |\n                         | (Default Route: `wg0` Virtual TUN)   |\n                         +------------------+-------------------+\n                                            |\n                         +------------------+-------------------+\n                         |                                      |\n       [ DATA PLANE TRAFFIC ]                         [ CONTROL PLANE TRAFFIC ]\n                 |                                              |\n      Encrypted ChaCha20-Poly1305                     TLS 1.3 Encrypted HTTPS/WSS\n      UDP Port 51820 (WireGuard)                      TCP Port 443 (API Gateway)\n                 |                                              |\n                 v                                              v\n      +----------------------+                       +----------------------+\n      | Edge VPN Node Host   |                       | Central Ingress API  |\n      | (Public IP: 198.51..) |                       | (api.privacygw.net)  |\n      +----------+-----------+                       +----------+-----------+\n                 |                                              |\n     [ nftables Ingress Match ]                      [ Google OIDC / Token Validation ]\n                 |                                              |\n      +----------v-----------+                       +----------v-----------+\n      | wg0 Interface Decrypt|                       | Ingest Diagnostic    |\n      | Allocated VIP:       |                       | Telemetry Payload    |\n      | 10.66.1.42           |                       +----------+-----------+\n      +----------+-----------+                                  |\n                 |                                   [ Re-Evaluate 4-Pillar Score ]\n    +------------+------------+                                 |\n    |                         |                                 v\n[ DNS: 10.66.0.1:53 ]   [ NAT Forward eth0 ]         [ Publish Redis Event Bus ]\n    |                         |                                 |\n[ CoreDNS Sinkhole ]          |                                 v\n    |                         |                      [ Dispatch WSS to Clients ]\n    +------------+------------+\n                 |\n                 v\n        [ PUBLIC INTERNET ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Revised Data Flow\n\n```\n[ Browser / Desktop Prober ]       [ Central Core API ]       [ Privacy Engine ]       [ Redis Bus ]       [ WebSocket Client ]\n            |                               |                         |                      |                    |\n            | 1. Submit Diagnostic Payload  |                         |                      |                    |\n            |    POST /api/v1/telemetry     |                         |                      |                    |\n            |    { dns_probe_token,         |                         |                      |                    |\n            |      webrtc_exposed_ips,      |                         |                      |                    |\n            |      canvas_hash_entropy }    |                         |                      |                    |\n            |------------------------------>|                         |                      |                    |\n            |                               | 2. Validate Schema &    |                      |                    |\n            |                               |    Forward Job          |                      |                    |\n            |                               |------------------------>|                      |                    |\n            |                               |                         | 3. Query Synthetic   |                    |\n            |                               |                         |    DNS Probe Logger  |                    |\n            |                               |                         | 4. Compute 4-Pillar  |                    |\n            |                               |                         |    Deterministic Math|                    |\n            |                               |                         | 5. Generate Reasons  |                    |\n            |                               |                         | 6. Publish Event     |                    |\n            |                               |                         |--------------------->|                    |\n            |                               |                         |                      | 7. Deliver to      |\n            |                               |                         |                      |    User Channels   |\n            |                               |                         |                      |------------------->|\n            |                               |                         |                      |    { score: 84,    |\n            |                               |                         |                      |      deductions:[]}|\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Development Architecture vs. Production Architecture\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\nDEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT (Local Machine / Single Node)\n========================================================================================================================\n* Orchestration: Docker Compose (All services in one bridge network).\n* WireGuard Engine: Containerized using `wireguard-go` or host-mounted `/dev/net/tun` with mock rootless network bridge.\n* Database: Single PostgreSQL 16 container with pre-seeded test fixtures.\n* Cache/Bus: Single standalone Redis container.\n* Ingress: Local reverse proxy on `http://localhost:3000`.\n* DNS Prober: Mock DNS server returning pre-configured lookup responses.\n* Cryptographic Secrets: Static development `.env` credentials with explicit mock banners.\n* Status: **DEVELOPMENT IMPLEMENTATION \342\200\224 NOT PRODUCTION READY**.\n\n========================================================================================================================\nPRODUCTION ENVIRONMENT (High-Availability Cloud / Edge Compute)\n========================================================================================================================\n* Orchestration: Kubernetes / Managed Nomad for Control Plane; Immutable Bare-Metal / Edge VMs for VPN Nodes.\n* WireGuard Engine: Native Linux Kernel 6.x Module (`wireguard.ko`) running directly on edge OS.\n* Daemon: Isolated Go/Rust `pg-node-daemon` running under systemd with strict AppArmor/SELinux profile.\n* Database: Managed High-Availability PostgreSQL Cluster (Multi-AZ, read-replicas, encrypted at rest via AES-256).\n* Cache/Bus: Managed Redis Cluster with TLS encryption and automatic failover.\n* Ingress: Dual-redundant Cloudflare Magic Transit / Envoy API Gateway terminating TLS 1.3 with strict HSTS.\n* DNS Prober: Geographically distributed Authoritative DNS Name Servers (`ns1-ns4.leak.privacygateway.net`).\n* Secrets: HashiCorp Vault / AWS Secrets Manager with dynamic IAM short-lived credential rotation.\n* Status: **TARGET PRODUCTION ARCHITECTURE**.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Detailed Production vs. Development Comparison\n\n| Dimension | Development Environment | Production Architecture |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **WireGuard Execution** | Container with `/dev/net/tun` or userland `wireguard-go` | Native Host Kernel Module with hardware offloading |\n| **Privilege Model** | Docker `CAP_NET_ADMIN` | Bare-metal host systemd service; dropped capabilities |\n| **Server Nodes** | Single loopback server node (`127.0.0.1:51820`) | Multi-region nodes (US, EU, AP, LATAM) with healthchecks |\n| **Secrets Management** | `.env.local` file (Never committed) | HashiCorp Vault / Cloud Secret Manager with KMS encryption |\n| **TLS / Ingress** | Self-signed or local plaintext HTTP proxy | Strict TLS 1.3 (ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256), HSTS |\n| **DNS Resolution** | Containerized CoreDNS with static forwarders | Hardened Unbound + CoreDNS with DNSSEC validation |\n| **Backup & DR** | Ephemeral Docker volumes | Point-in-time recovery (PITR) WAL archiving + hourly encrypted backups |\n| **Monitoring** | Console output & basic Docker stats | Prometheus + Grafana + OpenTelemetry tracing (Zero PII) |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Revised Docker Architecture (Development & Service Containers)\n\nEvery container is explicitly configured according to the Principle of Least Privilege:\n\n```yaml\nversion: \'3.9\'\n\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16.2-alpine\n    container_name: pg_database\n    user: \"postgres\"\n    read_only: true\n    tmpfs:\n      - /tmp\n      - /var/run/postgresql\n    security_opt:\n      - no-new-privileges:true\n    cap_drop:\n      - ALL\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_DB: privacy_gateway\n      POSTGRES_USER_FILE: /run/secrets/db_user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data:rw\n      - ../infrastructure/migrations:/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d:ro\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U `cat /run/secrets/db_user` -d privacy_gateway\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n    deploy:\n      resources:\n        limits:\n          cpus: \'1.0\'\n          memory: 512M\n\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7.2-alpine\n    container_name: pg_redis\n    user: \"999:999\"\n    read_only: true\n    security_opt:\n      - no-new-privileges:true\n    cap_drop:\n      - ALL\n    command: [\"redis-server\", \"--appendonly\", \"yes\", \"--requirepass\", \"${REDIS_PASSWORD:-redissecret}\"]\n    volumes:\n      - redisdata:/data:rw\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"redis-cli\", \"ping\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  api:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/api/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_api\n    user: \"10001:10001\" # non-root unprivileged node user\n    read_only: true\n    tmpfs:\n      - /tmp\n    security_opt:\n      - no-new-privileges:true\n    cap_drop:\n      - ALL\n    depends_on:\n      postgres:\n        condition: service_healthy\n      redis:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    environment:\n      NODE_ENV: production\n      PORT: 3000\n    ports:\n      - \"3000:3000\"\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n      - pg-public-net\n\n  privacy-engine:\n    build:\n      context: .\n      dockerfile: docker/privacy-engine/Dockerfile\n    container_name: pg_privacy_engine\n    user: \"10001:10001\"\n    read_only: true\n    tmpfs:\n      - /tmp\n    security_opt:\n      - no-new-privileges:true\n    cap_drop:\n      - ALL\n    depends_on:\n      - redis\n      - postgres\n    networks:\n      - pg-internal-net\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\n  redisdata:\n\nnetworks:\n  pg-internal-net:\n    internal: true\n  pg-public-net:\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Revised Database Model (PostgreSQL 16)\n\n```sql\n-- Security Extensions\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS \"uuid-ossp\";\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS \"pgcrypto\";\n\n-- Enums\nCREATE TYPE user_role AS ENUM (\'user\', \'admin\', \'auditor\');\nCREATE TYPE auth_provider AS ENUM (\'google\', \'email_password\', \'github\', \'apple\');\nCREATE TYPE subscription_tier AS ENUM (\'free\', \'premium\', \'business\');\nCREATE TYPE subscription_status AS ENUM (\'active\', \'past_due\', \'canceled\', \'trialing\');\nCREATE TYPE peer_status AS ENUM (\'active\', \'revoked\', \'dormant\');\nCREATE TYPE leak_verdict AS ENUM (\'safe\', \'warning\', \'leak_detected\');\nCREATE TYPE risk_level AS ENUM (\'low\', \'medium\', \'high\');\n\n-- 1. Users Table\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    display_name VARCHAR(100),\n    avatar_url TEXT,\n    role user_role DEFAULT \'user\' NOT NULL,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE NOT NULL,\n    data_retention_days INTEGER DEFAULT 30 NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 2. Authentication Identities (OIDC / OAuth Accounts)\nCREATE TABLE authentication_accounts (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    provider auth_provider NOT NULL,\n    provider_subject_id VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    email_at_provider VARCHAR(255),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    UNIQUE(provider, provider_subject_id)\n);\n\n-- 3. User Sessions (Refresh Tokens)\nCREATE TABLE user_sessions (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    refresh_token_hash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    user_agent TEXT,\n    client_ip_anonymized INET, -- Truncated to /24 (IPv4) or /48 (IPv6)\n    expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    revoked_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 4. Registered Devices\nCREATE TABLE devices (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    device_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\n    client_version VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\n    is_revoked BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL,\n    last_active_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 5. VPN Server Nodes\nCREATE TABLE vpn_servers (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    node_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    country_code VARCHAR(2) NOT NULL,\n    city VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    public_endpoint VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    wireguard_public_key VARCHAR(44) NOT NULL,\n    ipv4_subnet CIDR NOT NULL,\n    ipv6_subnet CIDR,\n    capacity_peers INTEGER DEFAULT 250 NOT NULL,\n    current_active_peers INTEGER DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE NOT NULL,\n    is_maintenance BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL,\n    health_status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT \'healthy\' NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 6. WireGuard Peers (Public keys ONLY)\nCREATE TABLE wireguard_peers (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    vpn_server_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES vpn_servers(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,\n    public_key VARCHAR(44) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    allocated_ipv4 INET UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    allocated_ipv6 INET UNIQUE,\n    status peer_status DEFAULT \'active\' NOT NULL,\n    total_rx_bytes BIGINT DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    total_tx_bytes BIGINT DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    last_handshake_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 7. Subscriptions & Billing\nCREATE TABLE subscriptions (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    tier subscription_tier DEFAULT \'free\' NOT NULL,\n    status subscription_status DEFAULT \'active\' NOT NULL,\n    payment_provider VARCHAR(50),\n    external_subscription_id VARCHAR(255),\n    max_devices INTEGER DEFAULT 2 NOT NULL,\n    current_period_start TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    current_period_end TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n    canceled_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 8. Privacy Diagnostic Scans (Aggregated Snapshots)\nCREATE TABLE privacy_scans (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    overall_score INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (overall_score BETWEEN 0 AND 100),\n    network_privacy_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    tracking_exposure_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    fingerprint_risk_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    leak_protection_score INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    dns_leak_verdict leak_verdict NOT NULL,\n    webrtc_leak_verdict leak_verdict NOT NULL,\n    trackers_blocked_total INTEGER DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,\n    scan_explanation JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 9. Privacy Events Stream (Configurable User Retention)\nCREATE TABLE privacy_events (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    device_id UUID REFERENCES devices(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    event_type VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\n    severity VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,\n    description TEXT NOT NULL,\n    metadata JSONB DEFAULT \'{}\'::JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 10. Audit Logs (Immutable Security Log)\nCREATE TABLE audit_logs (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4(),\n    actor_user_id UUID REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    action VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    target_resource VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    target_id UUID,\n    ip_address_anonymized INET,\n    metadata JSONB DEFAULT \'{}\'::JSONB NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Optimization & Partition Indexes\nCREATE INDEX idx_user_sessions_lookup ON user_sessions(user_id, expires_at);\nCREATE INDEX idx_wg_peers_server_active ON wireguard_peers(vpn_server_id, status);\nCREATE INDEX idx_privacy_scans_user_time ON privacy_scans(user_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_privacy_events_user_time ON privacy_events(user_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_audit_logs_actor_time ON audit_logs(actor_user_id, created_at DESC);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Authentication Model & Separation (Google OAuth / OIDC PKCE)\n\n### A. Web Flow vs. Desktop Flow Separation\n\n```\n[ WEB AUTHENTICATION FLOW ]\n1. User clicks \"Sign in with Google\" on Web UI.\n2. Web App generates cryptographically random `state` + `code_verifier` (stored in secure `sessionStorage`).\n3. Derives `code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier))`.\n4. Browser redirects to:\n   https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?\n     client_id=...&response_type=code&scope=openid%20email%20profile&\n     redirect_uri=https://app.privacygateway.net/auth/callback&\n     state=...&code_challenge=...&code_challenge_method=S256\n5. Google returns `code` to Web UI callback.\n6. Web UI POSTs to backend `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` with `{ code, code_verifier, state }`.\n\n[ DESKTOP CLIENT AUTHENTICATION FLOW (RFC 8252 Loopback) ]\n1. Desktop client binds a temporary local HTTP loopback server on `http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>/callback`.\n2. Rust Core generates `code_verifier` and `code_challenge` (S256).\n3. Opens default system OS browser to Google Auth endpoint with `redirect_uri=http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>/callback`.\n4. Browser redirects to local loopback server after login.\n5. Desktop client loopback catches `code`, closes local socket, and POSTs `{ code, code_verifier }` to backend `/api/v1/auth/google/callback`.\n```\n\n### B. Backend Cryptographic Verification Checklist\nEvery OIDC assertion must pass all of the following validations:\n1. **Exchange Code**: Server exchanges code directly with `https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token` using TLS.\n2. **Signature Verification**: Validates JWT signature of `id_token` against Google\342\200\231s published JWKS (`https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs`).\n3. **Issuer Assertion**: `iss` must equal `https://accounts.google.com` or `accounts.google.com`.\n4. **Audience Assertion**: `aud` must match the application\'s registered `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`.\n5. **Freshness**: Current timestamp must be before `exp` and after `iat`.\n6. **Email Verification**: `email_verified` boolean must be explicitly `true`.\n7. **Zero Google Access Token Storage**: The Google access token is discarded immediately after identity validation; only the Privacy Gateway session JWT is issued.\n\n---\n\n## 10. WireGuard Security & Privilege Model: Container vs. Host Analysis\n\n### Architectural Trade-off Evaluation:\n\n| Criterion | Option A: Host-Level Kernel Execution with Daemon | Option B: Privileged Docker Container |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Privilege Scope** | **Narrowly Scoped**: Daemon runs with single `CAP_NET_ADMIN` capability via systemd. | **Broad/Dangerous**: Container requires `--privileged`, `--cap-add=NET_ADMIN`, `--cap-add=SYS_MODULE`, and `/lib/modules` host bind-mount. |\n| **Container Escape Risk** | **Zero**: No Docker daemon involved in kernel networking path. | **High**: A compromised container with `SYS_MODULE` can load arbitrary kernel rootkits onto the host. |\n| **Performance / Throughput** | **Native**: 10+ Gbps kernel packet processing with zero virtualization overhead. | **Degraded**: Virtual network bridge and namespace crossing introduce 15\342\200\22325% throughput penalty. |\n| **Blast Radius** | API application is on a separate VM/container with no access to the WireGuard host. | If API and WG container share a host, Docker socket compromise grants full host root. |\n\n### Production Recommendation: **Option A (Host-Level Kernel Module with Scoped Systemd Daemon)**\n- The WireGuard data plane runs in the host Linux kernel (`wg0`).\n- The `pg-node-daemon` is a standalone Go/Rust binary that listens **only on a local UNIX domain socket** (`/run/wireguard/daemon.sock`) with file permissions `0600` owned by `wgdaemon:wgdaemon`.\n- The daemon communicates with the central API over mTLS (Mutual TLS 1.3) using dedicated client certificates.\n- The Central API has **zero root access** and cannot execute arbitrary shell commands on edge nodes.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Browser Extension Capability Matrix\n\n| Feature / Surface | What Can Be Reliably Detected | What Is an Estimate / Heuristic | What Browser APIs Restrict | What CANNOT Be Detected |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Tracker Detection** | Known third-party request URLs matching EasyPrivacy / Disconnect lists via `declarativeNetRequest`. | Classification category (Advertising vs. Analytics vs. Fingerprinter). | Cannot inspect encrypted request payloads or WebSockets frames content in MV3. | First-party tracking scripts hosted on the same root domain (CNAME cloaking detection is heuristic only). |\n| **Canvas Fingerprinting** | Read-back API calls (`toDataURL`, `getImageData`) executed by background scripts without user action. | Uniqueness score of the generated Canvas hash against statistical cohorts. | Cannot determine if Canvas usage is benign (e.g. video rendering) vs. tracking without behavioral heuristics. | Server-side image generation or passive CSS canvas measurements. |\n| **WebGL Fingerprinting** | Access to unmasked GPU renderer strings (`UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL`). | Hardware entropy contribution to overall device uniqueness. | Chrome restricts blocking GPU queries without breaking 3D WebGL page rendering. | Underlying physical GPU clock drift or microarchitectural side-channels. |\n| **Audio Fingerprinting** | Calls to `OfflineAudioContext` and `AnalyserNode` frequency buffer rendering. | Audio rendering uniqueness estimation. | Cannot alter audio buffer values without causing audible artifacts in valid media. | Passive acoustic tracking via external device microphones. |\n| **WebRTC Exposure** | Local private IP addresses (e.g. `192.168.x.x`) and public STUN candidate IP reflection. | Whether candidate represents an active VPN bypass or valid local interface. | Chrome `webrtcIPHandlingPolicy` permits restricting candidates to default route. | Cannot inspect WebRTC end-to-end encrypted media payload contents. |\n\n*Disclaimer Labeling*: All extension fingerprint cards must explicitly display the label **\"HEURISTIC ESTIMATE \342\200\224 Does not identify individual human identity\"**.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Technical Privacy & Data-Retention Model\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                     TECHNICAL PRIVACY & RETENTION SCHEDULE\n========================================================================================================================\n\nCATEGORY 1: DATA WE INTENTIONALLY DO NOT COLLECT OR RECORD\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* User Browsing History: We do not log destination IP addresses, visited hostnames, or full URLs.\n* Traffic Payloads: WireGuard tunnel packets pass through Linux kernel memory and are immediately forwarded; no packet capture.\n* DNS Query Logs: CoreDNS runs with in-memory logging disabled; query history is never written to disk.\n* WireGuard Private Keys: Generated strictly on the client; the API accepts only 32-byte Base64 Public Keys.\n* Plaintext Passwords / Credentials: We use OIDC token verification; passwords never touch our servers.\n\nCATEGORY 2: OPERATIONAL DATA REQUIRED TO OPERATE THE SERVICE\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* Account Information (Email, Display Name, User ID):\n  - Storage: PostgreSQL `users` table.\n  - Retention: Maintained while account is active; purged within 72 hours of user account deletion.\n* WireGuard Public Key & Allocated VIP:\n  - Storage: PostgreSQL `wireguard_peers` table + Edge Node active peer table.\n  - Retention: Maintained while peer is registered; instantly purged upon peer revocation.\n* Aggregate Bandwidth (Total RX / TX Bytes):\n  - Storage: Monotonic byte counter in `wireguard_peers`.\n  - Retention: Aggregated for capacity planning; reset monthly.\n* Operational Node Health (CPU, RAM, Active Peer Count):\n  - Storage: Prometheus time-series metrics (Aggregated across node, zero user identifiers).\n  - Retention: 14 days rolling window.\n\nCATEGORY 3: PRIVACY INTELLIGENCE & DIAGNOSTIC DATA (USER CONFIGURABLE)\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* Tracker Block Events (Domain Hash, Category, Timestamp):\n  - Storage: PostgreSQL `privacy_events`.\n  - Retention: Configurable by user (24 Hours / 7 Days / 30 Days / Immediate Purge).\n* Diagnostic Test Snapshots (Score, DNS Leak Verdict, WebRTC Verdict):\n  - Storage: PostgreSQL `privacy_scans`.\n  - Retention: User-configurable; deleted on demand.\n\nTHIRD-PARTY VISIBILITY DISCLOSURE\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* Edge VPN Server Host: Sees incoming client IP and encrypted WireGuard UDP packets. Cannot see unencrypted contents.\n* Cloud Infrastructure Provider (AWS/Hetzner/GCP): Sees aggregate node ingress/egress network flows.\n* DNS Upstream Resolver: Sees recursive queries originating from the VPN Server IP (Not the client\'s residential IP).\n* Authentication Provider (Google): Knows you authenticated with Privacy Gateway; has no visibility into VPN usage.\n* Service Administrators: Can see active account emails, registered public keys, and aggregate server loads; CANNOT see browsing history.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13. Deterministic Privacy Score Methodology\n\nThe Privacy Score is an explainable 100-point integer computed from 4 weighted pillars:\n\n$$\\text{PrivacyScore} = (0.30 \\times S_{\\text{network}}) + (0.25 \\times S_{\\text{tracking}}) + (0.20 \\times S_{\\text{fingerprint}}) + (0.25 \\times S_{\\text{leak}})$$\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. NETWORK PRIVACY PILLAR (Weight: 30% | Base: 100 Points)                                                           |\n| Inputs:                                                                                                              |\n|   * WireGuard Tunnel State: Connected (+50), Disconnected (-50)                                                      |\n|   * DNS Encryption Status: Verified Internal DoT (+30), Plaintext / External (-30)                                   |\n|   * Server Node Trust & Attestation: Active Node in Good Health (+20), Unverified (-20)                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 2. TRACKING EXPOSURE PILLAR (Weight: 25% | Base: 100 Points)                                                         |\n| Inputs:                                                                                                              |\n|   * Tracker Blocking Efficiency: (Trackers Blocked / Total Trackers Encountered) * 100                               |\n|   * Deductions: High-risk CNAME-cloaked trackers detected (-15 per unblocked instance)                               |\n|   * Deductions: Unblocked third-party analytics scripts (-10 per script)                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 3. FINGERPRINT RISK PILLAR (Weight: 20% | Base: 100 Points)                                                          |\n| Inputs:                                                                                                              |\n|   * Canvas Hash Entropy: Unique hash in top 5% uniqueness (-30), Common cohort (+20)                                 |\n|   * WebGL Unmasked GPU Renderer exposed (-20)                                                                        |\n|   * AudioContext frequency buffer fingerprinting detected (-20)                                                      |\n|   * Standard User-Agent & Language headers matched (+30)                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 4. LEAK PROTECTION PILLAR (Weight: 25% | Base: 100 Points)                                                           |\n| Inputs:                                                                                                              |\n|   * DNS Leak Test: SAFE (+50), LEAK DETECTED (-50)                                                                   |\n|   * WebRTC Binding Test: SAFE (+50), EXPOSED Residential IP (-50)                                                    |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n*Explainability Guarantee*: Every score output returns a structured `explanation` array listing exact point deductions and remediation steps (e.g. `{\"pillar\": \"Leak Protection\", \"deduction\": -50, \"reason\": \"WebRTC STUN query exposed residential IP 203.0.113.4\"}`).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Real-Time Event Bus & WebSocket Architecture\n\n```\n                                      [ REAL-TIME EVENT PIPELINE ]\n\n+-----------------------+     +------------------------+     +------------------------+     +------------------------+\n|  Node Daemon Handshake|     | Extension Tracker Event|     | Desktop Prober Test    |     | API Billing Change     |\n+-----------+-----------+     +-----------+------------+     +-----------+------------+     +-----------+------------+\n            |                             |                              |                              |\n            v                             v                              v                              v\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                    REDIS STREAMS PRODUCER (`pg:stream:events`)                                     |\n|  * Message Schema: { id: \"1771720000-0\", user_id: \"uuid\", type: \"WG_HANDSHAKE\", payload: {...}, trace_id: \"...\" }   |\n+-------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                                  |\n                                                  v\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                REDIS CONSUMER GROUP (`pg-event-dispatchers`)                                       |\n|  * Acknowledges messages (`XACK`) to prevent lost events                                                           |\n|  * Dispatches to Redis Pub/Sub User Channel (`pg:channel:user:<user_id>`)                                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                                  |\n                                                  v\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                     WEBSOCKET SERVER (Node.js Cluster)                                             |\n|                                                                                                                    |\n|  * Authenticates WebSocket handshake via Bearer JWT query parameter or Authorization header                        |\n|  * Subscribes socket to Redis channel `pg:channel:user:<user_id>`                                                  |\n|  * Heartbeat: Ping/Pong frame sent every 25 seconds; terminates dead sockets after 60s timeout                     |\n|  * Backpressure: High-watermark buffer dropped if client socket is stalled (>64KB buffer)                          |\n|  * Reconnect Handling: Client includes `last_event_id`; server replays missed events from Redis Stream              |\n+-------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                                  |\n                         +------------------------+------------------------+\n                         |                                                 |\n                         v                                                 v\n             [ Web Dashboard React UI ]                         [ Desktop Client GUI ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 15. Multi-Server Node Lifecycle & Geographic Topology\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                           SERVER NODE LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT                                         |\n|                                                                                                                    |\n|  1. PROVISIONING           2. ACTIVE SERVING            3. DRAINING / MAINTENANCE        4. DECOMMISSIONING        |\n|  +--------------------+    +--------------------+       +--------------------+           +--------------------+    |\n|  | * Terraform spins  |    | * Heartbeat healthy|       | * `is_maintenance` |           | * Revoke all peer  |    |\n|  |   up node instance |    | * Low latency      |       |   set to TRUE      |           |   public keys      |    |\n|  | * mTLS certs issued|--->| * Accepts new peer |--->   | * No new peers     |---------->| * Zero disk wipe   |    |\n|  | * WireGuard pubkey |    |   allocations      |       |   allocated        |           | * Destroy cloud    |    |\n|  |   registered to DB |    | * Netlink sync     |       | * WS notifies peers|           |   compute instance |    |\n|  +--------------------+    +--------------------+       |   to switch node   |           +--------------------+    |\n|                                                         +--------------------+                                     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n### Geographic Node Distribution Plan:\n1. **US-East** (Ashburn, VA) \342\200\224 `us-east.privacygateway.net` (CIDR: `10.66.1.0/24`)\n2. **EU-Central** (Frankfurt, DE) \342\200\224 `eu-central.privacygateway.net` (CIDR: `10.66.2.0/24`)\n3. **AP-South** (Singapore, SG) \342\200\224 `ap-south.privacygateway.net` (CIDR: `10.66.3.0/24`)\n4. **IN-West** (Mumbai, IN) \342\200\224 `in-west.privacygateway.net` (CIDR: `10.66.4.0/24`)\n\n---\n\n## 16. Production Infrastructure Architecture\n\n```\n[ Edge Cloud / AWS / Hetzner ]\n  \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 VPC 1: Control Plane (Private Subnets)\n  \342\224\202     \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 EKS / Managed Nomad Cluster (API, Privacy Engine, Worker pods)\n  \342\224\202     \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Managed PostgreSQL (Multi-AZ, Encrypted EBS volumes)\n  \342\224\202     \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Managed Redis Cluster (In-memory, Auth enabled)\n  \342\224\202     \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Ingress Envoy Gateway (Public Subnet, Port 443 only)\n  \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 VPC 2..N: Geographic Edge Nodes (Isolated VPCs per region)\n        \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Linux Kernel 6.x Bare-Metal Instances (Port 51820 UDP Ingress)\n        \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Isolated CoreDNS Resolver Container (Port 53 local UDP only)\n        \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Scoped `pg-node-daemon` connecting back to Control Plane via mTLS\n```\n\n---\n\n## 17. Expanded Threat Model (STRIDE Matrix)\n\n| Threat & Vector | Target Component | Attack Description | Impact | Technical Mitigation | Residual Risk |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Compromised Desktop Client** | Control Plane API | Malicious user reverse-engineers desktop app and sends malformed telemetry/peer requests. | DoS on API or attempts to register invalid public keys. | Strict JWT Bearer validation, rate-limiting per User ID (Redis token bucket), and strict schema validation with Zod. | User can always modify local client UI state on their own machine. |\n| **Malicious Browser Extension** | Privacy Engine | Malicious extension injects fabricated tracker events. | Distorted privacy scores. | Extension signs telemetry with temporary device-bound session token; anomaly detection on event frequencies. | Compromised browser can report false local DOM heuristics. |\n| **Compromised VPN Edge Node** | WireGuard Node | Attacker gains root on an edge node. | Can monitor transit metadata for connected peers on that specific node. | Node has zero user accounts or DB passwords. All storage is ephemeral memory. Other nodes in different regions are isolated. | Traffic traversing the compromised node during the intrusion window could be metadata-profiled. |\n| **Compromised Database** | PostgreSQL DB | SQL injection or unauthorized DB access. | Data breach of account records and peer public keys. | Parametrized SQL queries, zero plaintext passwords, zero private keys stored, AES-256 encrypted at rest. | Stored email addresses and device names exposed. |\n| **Malicious Administrator** | Control Plane | Rogue employee accesses customer data. | Attempts to inspect user activity. | Audit logs are write-only and shipped to immutable append-only storage (WORM bucket); zero browsing logs exist to inspect. | Admin can revoke accounts or force subscription tier updates. |\n| **Stolen WireGuard Private Key** | Client Device | Attacker extracts private key from user laptop. | Attacker can connect to VPN as that peer until revoked. | Keys stored in OS secure enclave (Keychain/DPAPI). User can click \"Revoke Device\" from web dashboard instantly. | Attacker has VPN access until revocation timestamp. |\n| **DDoS on VPN UDP Port** | Edge Node | High-bandwidth UDP flood against port 51820. | Service disruption on single node. | WireGuard built-in BLAKE2s cookie protocol for cryptographic handshake rate-limiting; BGP Anycast scrubbing. | Extreme volumetric attacks can saturate uplink bandwidth. |\n| **VPN Service Abuse (Spam/Botnet)** | Exit Traffic | Malicious user uses VPN for cyberattacks or spam. | Server IP blocklisted by external CDNs/services. | Outbound SMTP port 25 blocked by default; connection-rate anomalies trigger automated peer suspension. | Potential blacklisting of specific exit IPs by aggressive third-party firewalls. |\n\n---\n\n## 18. Security Boundaries & Enclaves\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| TRUST BOUNDARY 1: BROWSER / CLIENT USER-SPACE                                                     |\n| * Untrusted inputs: DOM events, WebExtension scripts, local user filesystem.                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                      | HTTPS / WSS / gRPC (Strict Schema Validation)\n                                      v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| TRUST BOUNDARY 2: CENTRAL API & CONTROL PLANE                                                      |\n| * Authenticated zone: Validated JWT Bearers, RBAC enforcement, parameterized SQL operations.      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                      | Mutual TLS 1.3 (Client Certificate Authentication)\n                                      v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| TRUST BOUNDARY 3: EDGE NODE DAEMON & WIREGUARD KERNEL                                              |\n| * Cryptographic zone: Curve25519 authentication, netlink isolation, zero database connection.       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 19. Known Technical Limitations\n\n1. **Browser Extension Manifest V3 Limitations**: Extension cannot inspect WebSocket frames or dynamically rewritten WebRTC streams inside WebWorkers on certain Chromium builds.\n2. **Kernel WireGuard Driver Installation**: On corporate or restricted Windows workstations, installing the `wintun` virtual adapter driver requires initial Administrator approval.\n3. **Synthetic Leak Detection Resolution**: Synthetic DNS subdomain testing requires public DNS resolution latency (~100\342\200\223300ms) before the verdict is marked verified.\n4. **Residential IP Spoofing Prevention**: While WireGuard prevents IP spoofing inside the tunnel (`AllowedIPs`), an ISP can always observe that an encrypted UDP connection is occurring between the user and the VPN server IP.\n\n---\n\n## 20. What Is Actually Implementable Now (Phase 1\342\200\2234)\n\n1. **Complete Monorepo & TypeScript Ecosystem** (API, Shared Types, React Web Dashboard).\n2. **PostgreSQL Relational Schema & Migration Pipeline** with Row-Level Security.\n3. **Google OAuth 2.0 / OIDC with PKCE Verification** for both Web and Desktop flows.\n4. **WireGuard Peer Lifecycle Management Engine** (Key generation, Subnet IP allocation, configuration formatting).\n5. **Deterministic 4-Pillar Privacy Scoring & Diagnostics Evaluator**.\n6. **Real-Time WebSocket Ingestion Bus & Event Broadcaster** (Redis Streams + Socket.IO).\n7. **Local Docker Environment** running with non-root security profiles.\n\n---\n\n## 21. What Requires Future Infrastructure (Phase 12+)\n\n1. **Multi-Region Bare-Metal Edge Node Deployments** (Requires dedicated cloud provider accounts in US, EU, AP).\n2. **Production Authoritative DNS Server Fleet** (`ns1.leak.privacygateway.net` binding for synthetic leak tests).\n3. **Stripe / Payment Gateway Live Merchant Integration** (Requires active commercial business credentials).\n4. **Code-Signing Certificates** (Apple Developer ID & Microsoft Authenticode for seamless desktop client installation).\n\n---\n\n## 22. Revised Incremental Development Roadmap\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 0.1: REVISED MASTER SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & SECURITY SPECIFICATION (CURRENT)                  |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 1: Monorepo Setup, Docker Orchestration, and PostgreSQL Database Migrations                 |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 2: OpenID Connect & Google OAuth 2.0 PKCE Authentication (Web + Desktop Handshakes)         |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 3: WireGuard Server Node Controller & Netlink Peer Management Module                         |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 4: Core REST API & Real-Time WebSocket Event Bus (Redis Streams Ingestion)                  |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 5: Desktop Client Core (Tauri / Rust Engine + Virtual Adapter & Killswitch)                 |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 6: Browser Privacy Extension (Manifest V3 Tracker Blocking & Heuristic Engine)              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 7: Deterministic Privacy Scoring Engine & Synthetic Leak Prober Engine                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 8: Production Web Management Dashboard & Admin Operations Center                            |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |\n  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PHASE 9: End-to-End Integration, Automated Test Suites & Security Hardening Audits                |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## Changes From Phase 0\n\n1. **WireGuard Privilege Architecture Overhaul**: Replaced the initial privileged Docker container concept with a **Host-Level Linux Kernel Module + Scoped Systemd Daemon** communicating over a protected local UNIX socket with mTLS, removing `SYS_MODULE` and host-root escape risks.\n2. **Technically Precise Privacy & Logging Specification**: Replaced the informal \"zero logs guarantee\" with an exhaustive, 3-category technical disclosure clarifying exactly what is never logged, what is required for operations, and what third parties (ISPs, Google, Cloud providers) can observe.\n3. **Deterministic & Explainable 4-Pillar Privacy Scoring Formula**: Formulated explicit mathematical weighting, normalization inputs, penalty deductions, and mandatory explanation payloads, explicitly distinguishing heuristic exposure from identity confirmation.\n4. **Browser Extension Realism & Limitation Matrix**: Explicitly defined the boundaries of WebExtension MV3 APIs, clarifying that Canvas/WebGL/Audio measurements are statistical heuristics and forbidding any claims of 100% fingerprint attribution.\n5. **Hardened Google OIDC / PKCE Dual-Flow Architecture**: Separated standard Web redirects from Desktop RFC 8252 local loopback authentication, ensuring strict ID token signature verification against Google JWKS with zero storage of Google access tokens.\n6. **Robust Real-Time Redis Streams & WebSocket Delivery**: Added consumer groups (`XACK`), message sequence ordering, heartbeat timeouts, and backpressure policies to prevent lost or fake real-time events.\n7. **Strict Non-Root Docker & Production Hardening**: Pinned all container versions, enforced `read_only` filesystems, added `cap_drop: ALL`, dropped all `latest` tags, and isolated internal networks from public ingress.\n8. **Commercial Multi-Server Lifecycle Design**: Added node draining, maintenance transitions, peer capacity counters, and regional failover specifications.\n\n---\n\n### Architectural Review Confirmation\n\nThis revised **Phase 0.1 Architecture Specification** resolves all privilege boundaries, mathematical scoring ambiguities, privacy disclosures, and operational constraints.\n\nPlease review and provide your approval so we may proceed to **Phase 1: Monorepo Setup, Docker Orchestration, and PostgreSQL Database Migrations**."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
