  user_input {
    user_response: "Phase 1 is approved as the development foundation.\n\nThe available verification is accepted, but do NOT claim that PostgreSQL migration runtime or Docker Compose runtime was fully verified, because the AI Studio sandbox does not provide a Docker daemon or running PostgreSQL service.\n\nNow proceed to Phase 2: Google OAuth/OIDC Authentication.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo NOT implement Phase 3 or any later phase.\n\nBefore writing implementation code, first provide the Phase 2 implementation plan and authentication architecture.\n\nPhase 2 must implement REAL authentication, not simulated login.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Google Authentication\n- Google OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect\n- Authorization Code Flow\n- PKCE where appropriate\n- Proper state validation\n- Nonce validation where applicable\n- Validate issuer\n- Validate audience\n- Validate token signature\n- Validate expiration\n- Validate email_verified\n- Secure redirect URI handling\n\n2. Web Login\nImplement:\n\nBrowser\n\342\206\222 Privacy Gateway\n\342\206\222 Google\n\342\206\222 OAuth callback\n\342\206\222 Backend validation\n\342\206\222 User creation/linking\n\342\206\222 Secure application session\n\nDo not trust identity information supplied directly by the frontend.\n\n3. Desktop Authentication Preparation\n\nThe future Tauri desktop application must be able to authenticate securely.\n\nFollow the native-app OAuth security model appropriate for desktop applications.\n\nDo not implement the Tauri client yet.\n\nDesign the authentication API so the future desktop client can use it without redesigning the authentication system.\n\n4. Database\n\nUse the existing:\n\nusers\nauthentication_accounts\nuser_sessions\n\ntables.\n\nDo not create duplicate identity systems.\n\nSupport:\n\n- first-time Google user registration\n- returning Google user login\n- account linking\n- logout\n- session revocation\n- multiple sessions/devices\n\nDo not store Google access/refresh tokens unless there is a clearly justified requirement.\n\n5. Sessions\n\nImplement secure application sessions.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- cryptographically secure session/refresh tokens\n- store only appropriate token hashes server-side\n- expiration\n- rotation where appropriate\n- revocation\n- logout\n- protection against replay\n- secure cookie configuration for web sessions\n- CSRF protection where applicable\n\nNever store plaintext session secrets unnecessarily.\n\n6. Authorization\n\nPrepare the existing roles:\n\nuser\nadmin\nauditor\n\nImplement proper authorization boundaries.\n\nA normal user must never be able to access administrative functionality simply by modifying an API request.\n\n7. Security\n\nProtect against:\n\n- OAuth state attacks\n- CSRF\n- authorization-code injection\n- token replay\n- session fixation\n- session theft\n- account takeover\n- open redirects\n- confused-deputy attacks\n- improper account linking\n\nUse secure defaults.\n\n8. Privacy\n\nAuthentication must collect the minimum information required.\n\nDo not collect browsing history.\n\nDo not collect URLs.\n\nDo not collect DNS history.\n\nDo not introduce VPN traffic logging.\n\n9. API\n\nCreate clearly defined authentication endpoints, for example:\n\nGET /api/v1/auth/google/start\nGET /api/v1/auth/google/callback\nPOST /api/v1/auth/logout\nPOST /api/v1/auth/refresh\nGET /api/v1/auth/me\n\nUse appropriate HTTP methods and security controls.\n\n10. Frontend\n\nUpdate the existing React dashboard to support:\n\n- Sign in with Google\n- authenticated state\n- current user information\n- logout\n- authentication errors\n- loading states\n\nDo not redesign the entire dashboard.\n\n11. Testing\n\nCreate real tests for:\n\n- OAuth state validation\n- invalid authorization codes\n- invalid ID tokens\n- wrong issuer\n- wrong audience\n- expired tokens\n- unverified email\n- account creation\n- returning-user login\n- account linking\n- logout\n- session expiration\n- session revocation\n- unauthorized admin access\n- CSRF/session protections\n\nDo not use fake \"Google login successful\" logic.\n\n12. Environment Variables\n\nAdd only the required Google OAuth development variables to .env.example.\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\ndevelopment credentials\nfrom\nproduction credentials.\n\nNever commit real OAuth secrets.\n\n13. Docker\n\nPhase 2 must continue to work with the existing Docker architecture.\n\nDo not weaken the Phase 1 security configuration.\n\n14. No Future Features\n\nDo NOT implement:\n\n- WireGuard\n- VPN peer management\n- VPN server provisioning\n- Tauri desktop client\n- browser extension\n- tracker detection\n- fingerprint analysis\n- DNS leak detection\n- WebRTC detection\n- privacy scoring\n- billing\n- multi-region infrastructure\n\nThose remain future phases.\n\n15. Important Roadmap Change\n\nUpdate the roadmap so the next stages are:\n\nPhase 1 \342\200\224 Foundation \342\234\205\nPhase 2 \342\200\224 Authentication / Google OAuth\nPhase 3 \342\200\224 Real WireGuard VPN infrastructure\nPhase 4 \342\200\224 Real-time event infrastructure\nPhase 5 \342\200\224 Tauri desktop client\nPhase 6 \342\200\224 Browser extension\nPhase 7 \342\200\224 Privacy intelligence\nPhase 8 \342\200\224 Production hardening / multi-server expansion\n\nDo not implement these future phases now.\n\nFIRST RESPONSE:\n\nBefore changing code, provide:\n\n1. Phase 2 architecture\n2. Google OAuth flow diagram\n3. Web authentication flow\n4. Future desktop authentication flow\n5. Session architecture\n6. Database changes, if any\n7. API endpoints\n8. Security model\n9. Threat model\n10. Testing strategy\n11. Environment variables required\n12. Files that will be created/modified\n\nThen WAIT for my approval.\n\nDo not write implementation code until I approve the Phase 2 plan."
    items {
      text: "Phase 1 is approved as the development foundation.\n\nThe available verification is accepted, but do NOT claim that PostgreSQL migration runtime or Docker Compose runtime was fully verified, because the AI Studio sandbox does not provide a Docker daemon or running PostgreSQL service.\n\nNow proceed to Phase 2: Google OAuth/OIDC Authentication.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo NOT implement Phase 3 or any later phase.\n\nBefore writing implementation code, first provide the Phase 2 implementation plan and authentication architecture.\n\nPhase 2 must implement REAL authentication, not simulated login.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Google Authentication\n- Google OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect\n- Authorization Code Flow\n- PKCE where appropriate\n- Proper state validation\n- Nonce validation where applicable\n- Validate issuer\n- Validate audience\n- Validate token signature\n- Validate expiration\n- Validate email_verified\n- Secure redirect URI handling\n\n2. Web Login\nImplement:\n\nBrowser\n\342\206\222 Privacy Gateway\n\342\206\222 Google\n\342\206\222 OAuth callback\n\342\206\222 Backend validation\n\342\206\222 User creation/linking\n\342\206\222 Secure application session\n\nDo not trust identity information supplied directly by the frontend.\n\n3. Desktop Authentication Preparation\n\nThe future Tauri desktop application must be able to authenticate securely.\n\nFollow the native-app OAuth security model appropriate for desktop applications.\n\nDo not implement the Tauri client yet.\n\nDesign the authentication API so the future desktop client can use it without redesigning the authentication system.\n\n4. Database\n\nUse the existing:\n\nusers\nauthentication_accounts\nuser_sessions\n\ntables.\n\nDo not create duplicate identity systems.\n\nSupport:\n\n- first-time Google user registration\n- returning Google user login\n- account linking\n- logout\n- session revocation\n- multiple sessions/devices\n\nDo not store Google access/refresh tokens unless there is a clearly justified requirement.\n\n5. Sessions\n\nImplement secure application sessions.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- cryptographically secure session/refresh tokens\n- store only appropriate token hashes server-side\n- expiration\n- rotation where appropriate\n- revocation\n- logout\n- protection against replay\n- secure cookie configuration for web sessions\n- CSRF protection where applicable\n\nNever store plaintext session secrets unnecessarily.\n\n6. Authorization\n\nPrepare the existing roles:\n\nuser\nadmin\nauditor\n\nImplement proper authorization boundaries.\n\nA normal user must never be able to access administrative functionality simply by modifying an API request.\n\n7. Security\n\nProtect against:\n\n- OAuth state attacks\n- CSRF\n- authorization-code injection\n- token replay\n- session fixation\n- session theft\n- account takeover\n- open redirects\n- confused-deputy attacks\n- improper account linking\n\nUse secure defaults.\n\n8. Privacy\n\nAuthentication must collect the minimum information required.\n\nDo not collect browsing history.\n\nDo not collect URLs.\n\nDo not collect DNS history.\n\nDo not introduce VPN traffic logging.\n\n9. API\n\nCreate clearly defined authentication endpoints, for example:\n\nGET /api/v1/auth/google/start\nGET /api/v1/auth/google/callback\nPOST /api/v1/auth/logout\nPOST /api/v1/auth/refresh\nGET /api/v1/auth/me\n\nUse appropriate HTTP methods and security controls.\n\n10. Frontend\n\nUpdate the existing React dashboard to support:\n\n- Sign in with Google\n- authenticated state\n- current user information\n- logout\n- authentication errors\n- loading states\n\nDo not redesign the entire dashboard.\n\n11. Testing\n\nCreate real tests for:\n\n- OAuth state validation\n- invalid authorization codes\n- invalid ID tokens\n- wrong issuer\n- wrong audience\n- expired tokens\n- unverified email\n- account creation\n- returning-user login\n- account linking\n- logout\n- session expiration\n- session revocation\n- unauthorized admin access\n- CSRF/session protections\n\nDo not use fake \"Google login successful\" logic.\n\n12. Environment Variables\n\nAdd only the required Google OAuth development variables to .env.example.\n\nClearly distinguish:\n\ndevelopment credentials\nfrom\nproduction credentials.\n\nNever commit real OAuth secrets.\n\n13. Docker\n\nPhase 2 must continue to work with the existing Docker architecture.\n\nDo not weaken the Phase 1 security configuration.\n\n14. No Future Features\n\nDo NOT implement:\n\n- WireGuard\n- VPN peer management\n- VPN server provisioning\n- Tauri desktop client\n- browser extension\n- tracker detection\n- fingerprint analysis\n- DNS leak detection\n- WebRTC detection\n- privacy scoring\n- billing\n- multi-region infrastructure\n\nThose remain future phases.\n\n15. Important Roadmap Change\n\nUpdate the roadmap so the next stages are:\n\nPhase 1 \342\200\224 Foundation \342\234\205\nPhase 2 \342\200\224 Authentication / Google OAuth\nPhase 3 \342\200\224 Real WireGuard VPN infrastructure\nPhase 4 \342\200\224 Real-time event infrastructure\nPhase 5 \342\200\224 Tauri desktop client\nPhase 6 \342\200\224 Browser extension\nPhase 7 \342\200\224 Privacy intelligence\nPhase 8 \342\200\224 Production hardening / multi-server expansion\n\nDo not implement these future phases now.\n\nFIRST RESPONSE:\n\nBefore changing code, provide:\n\n1. Phase 2 architecture\n2. Google OAuth flow diagram\n3. Web authentication flow\n4. Future desktop authentication flow\n5. Session architecture\n6. Database changes, if any\n7. API endpoints\n8. Security model\n9. Threat model\n10. Testing strategy\n11. Environment variables required\n12. Files that will be created/modified\n\nThen WAIT for my approval.\n\nDo not write implementation code until I approve the Phase 2 plan."
