  planner_response {
    response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 2: GOOGLE OAUTH / OIDC AUTHENTICATION ARCHITECTURE & SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Phase 2 Architecture Overview\n\nPhase 2 establishes real OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity verification and a cryptographically hardened application session engine.\n\nThe authentication engine sits on the **Control Plane**, operating with strict privilege separation. The frontend never supplies verified identity claims directly; instead, the backend exchanges the authorization code directly with Google\'s servers over TLS, verifies the OIDC ID Token signature against Google\'s public JSON Web Key Sets (JWKS), and issues dual-token application sessions (short-lived stateless JWT access tokens + rotated CSPRNG refresh tokens stored in hashed form).\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                     PHASE 2 AUTHENTICATION ARCHITECTURE\n========================================================================================================================\n\n+--------------------------------------------------+        +--------------------------------------------------+\n|               WEB CLIENT (Browser)               |        |           FUTURE DESKTOP CLIENT (Tauri)          |\n|  * Initiates login: GET /api/v1/auth/google/start|        |  * Local RFC 8252 loopback http://127.0.0.1:port |\n|  * Receives signed httpOnly session cookies      |        |  * Exchanges auth code with PKCE code_verifier   |\n+------------------------+-------------------------+        +------------------------+-------------------------+\n                         |                                                           |\n                         | HTTPS Redirect & API Requests                             | HTTPS Loopback & Code Exchange\n                         v                                                           v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                             PRIVACY GATEWAY BACKEND API                                              |\n|                                                                                                                      |\n|   +------------------------------------+          +--------------------------------------------------------------+   |\n|   |       OAuth & PKCE Controller      |          |                Session & Token Engine                        |   |\n|   |  * Cryptographic State & Nonce Gen |          |  * Stateless Access Token (JWT RS256/HS256, 15m expiration)  |   |\n|   |  * S256 Code Challenge Validation  |          |  * CSPRNG 256-bit Refresh Token (SHA-256 Hash Stored, 30d)   |   |\n|   |  * Google Token Direct HTTPS Post  |          |  * Automatic Refresh Token Rotation & Replay Detection       |   |\n|   +-----------------+------------------+          +------------------------------+-------------------------------+   |\n|                     |                                                            |                                   |\n|                     v Direct TLS 1.3 (Server-to-Server)                          v Transactional SQL                 |\n|   +------------------------------------+          +--------------------------------------------------------------+   |\n|   |   Google Identity OIDC Provider    |          |                 PostgreSQL 16 Storage                        |   |\n|   |   (accounts.google.com / JWKS)     |          |  * `users` (Identity, Role, Retention)                      |   |\n|   |  * Validates iss, aud, exp, certs  |          |  * `authentication_accounts` (google, provider_sub)           |   |\n|   |  * Validates email_verified == true|          |  * `user_sessions` (token_hash, expiration, revoked_at)     |   |\n|   +------------------------------------+          +--------------------------------------------------------------+   |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Google OAuth 2.0 / OIDC Flow Diagram\n\n```\nBrowser / Desktop                Privacy Gateway API                   Google Identity Services (OIDC)\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       | 1. GET /api/v1/auth/google/start |                                          |\n       |--------------------------------->|                                          |\n       |                                  | 2. Generate CSPRNG state, nonce, PKCE    |\n       |                                  |    Store state/verifier in cookie/cache  |\n       | 3. 302 Redirect to Google Auth   |                                          |\n       |<---------------------------------|                                          |\n       |                                                                             |\n       | 4. User navigates to Google Consent (accounts.google.com)                   |\n       |---------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                                                             |\n       | 5. User approves; Google redirects with ?code=...&state=...                 |\n       |<----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                                                             |\n       | 6. GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback?code=...&state=...                      |\n       |--------------------------------->|                                          |\n       |                                  | 7. Validate `state` against signed cookie|\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       |                                  | 8. POST https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token\n       |                                  |    { code, client_id, secret, verifier } |\n       |                                  |----------------------------------------->|\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       |                                  | 9. Returns { id_token, access_token }    |\n       |                                  |<-----------------------------------------|\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       |                                  | 10. Cryptographic Verification:          |\n       |                                  |     a. Fetch & cache Google JWKS certs   |\n       |                                  |     b. Verify RS256 signature            |\n       |                                  |     c. Check iss == accounts.google.com  |\n       |                                  |     d. Check aud == GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID     |\n       |                                  |     e. Check exp > now                   |\n       |                                  |     f. Check email_verified == true      |\n       |                                  |     g. Check nonce == generated nonce    |\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       |                                  | 11. Database Operations (Transaction):   |\n       |                                  |     - Find or create `users`             |\n       |                                  |     - Link `authentication_accounts`     |\n       |                                  |     - Create `user_sessions` (hash only) |\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       | 12. Set-Cookie: access_token,    |                                          |\n       |     Set-Cookie: refresh_token    |                                          |\n       |     302 Redirect to Dashboard    |                                          |\n       |<---------------------------------|                                          |\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Web Authentication Flow Details\n\n1. **Start Request (`GET /api/v1/auth/google/start`)**:\n   - Generates 32 bytes of cryptographically secure random entropy for `state`, `nonce`, and `code_verifier`.\n   - Computes `code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier))`.\n   - Encrypts/signs a short-lived, transient cookie (`pg_oauth_state`) containing `{ state, nonce, code_verifier, return_to }` with `httpOnly`, `secure`, `sameSite: \'lax\'`, `maxAge: 300000` (5 minutes).\n   - Generates the Google authorization URL:\n     ```\n     https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?\n       client_id=GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID&\n       response_type=code&\n       scope=openid%20email%20profile&\n       redirect_uri=APP_URL/api/v1/auth/google/callback&\n       state=ENCRYPTED_STATE&\n       nonce=NONCE&\n       code_challenge=CODE_CHALLENGE&\n       code_challenge_method=S256&\n       prompt=select_account\n     ```\n   - Responds with `302 Redirect` to Google.\n\n2. **Callback Request (`GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback`)**:\n   - Parses `code` and `state` from URL query.\n   - Verifies that `state` exactly matches the value in the signed `pg_oauth_state` cookie (mitigating CSRF and state tampering). Clears `pg_oauth_state`.\n   - Executes direct back-channel HTTP POST to `https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token` with the authorization code and `code_verifier`.\n   - Validates the received `id_token` (as detailed in Section 2).\n   - Upserts the user in PostgreSQL:\n     - If the email does not exist: Creates record in `users` (default role `user`) and creates record in `authentication_accounts` (`provider = \'google\'`).\n     - If the email exists: Links record in `authentication_accounts` if not already linked.\n   - Creates a new session in `user_sessions`:\n     - Generates 64-character hex CSPRNG `refresh_token`.\n     - Computes `refresh_token_hash = SHA256(refresh_token)`.\n     - Inserts `{ user_id, refresh_token_hash, expires_at: now + 30 days, user_agent, client_ip_anonymized }`.\n   - Mints a short-lived JWT `access_token` (15m expiry) containing `{ sub: user.id, email: user.email, role: user.role }`.\n   - Issues `httpOnly`, `secure`, `sameSite: \'lax\'` cookies for `pg_access_token` and `pg_refresh_token`.\n   - Redirects user back to `/` (Dashboard).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Future Desktop Authentication Flow (RFC 8252 Loopback)\n\nThe authentication architecture is engineered from Day 1 to support the future Tauri desktop client without backend refactoring:\n\n1. **Loopback Binding**: The future desktop app opens a dynamic TCP socket on loopback (`http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>`).\n2. **PKCE in Native App**: Desktop generates its own `code_verifier` and `code_challenge`.\n3. **Desktop Start Endpoint (`POST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/start`)**:\n   - Backend registers the desktop redirect URI (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback`) and returns the Google Auth URL.\n4. **System Browser Execution**: Desktop client opens the system\'s default browser (Safari, Chrome, Edge) to Google\'s consent screen.\n5. **Loopback Redirection**: After Google approves, the browser redirects to the local loopback server (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback?code=...&state=...`).\n6. **Desktop Direct Code Exchange (`POST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange`)**:\n   - Desktop sends `{ code, code_verifier, state, redirect_uri }` to the backend.\n   - Backend exchanges the code directly with Google and validates the ID token.\n   - Instead of setting web cookies, the backend returns JSON `{ access_token, refresh_token, user }`.\n   - The desktop client securely persists the refresh token in the OS Keyring / Windows Credential Manager / macOS Keychain.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Session Architecture & Lifecycle\n\n```\n                                    SESSION LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT\n\n1. ISSUANCE (On Login)\n   - Access Token: RS256/HS256 JWT, lifespan = 15 minutes. Contains { sub, email, role, jti }.\n   - Refresh Token: 256-bit CSPRNG hex string. Lifespan = 30 days.\n   - Stored in DB: SHA-256(refresh_token). The raw refresh token is ONLY sent to the client.\n\n2. AUTHENTICATION / AUTHORIZATION (Per Request)\n   - API middleware checks `pg_access_token` cookie (or `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header).\n   - Verifies signature, expiration, and issuer statelessly in <1ms.\n   - Injects `req.user = { id, email, role }`.\n\n3. REFRESH & TOKEN ROTATION (`POST /api/v1/auth/refresh`)\n   - Client presents current `refresh_token`.\n   - Computes `incoming_hash = SHA256(refresh_token)`.\n   - Queries `user_sessions WHERE refresh_token_hash = incoming_hash AND revoked_at IS NULL`.\n   - If not found or expired:\n     * Check if hash matches an already revoked session. If yes, TRIGGER REPLAY ATTACK ALERT: Revoke ALL active sessions for that user immediately (session theft protection).\n     * Return 401 Unauthorized.\n   - If valid:\n     * Generates a NEW `refresh_token` and updates database record with `new_refresh_token_hash` (Token Rotation).\n     * Mints a fresh 15-minute `access_token`.\n     * Sets updated cookies.\n\n4. LOGOUT & REVOCATION (`POST /api/v1/auth/logout`)\n   - Computes `incoming_hash = SHA256(refresh_token)`.\n   - Sets `revoked_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` for that session.\n   - Clears auth cookies on client.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Database Changes\n\nNo destructive schema changes are needed; Phase 1\'s `001_initial_schema.sql` pre-provisioned the required tables:\n- `users`: `(id, email, display_name, avatar_url, role, is_active, data_retention_days)`\n- `authentication_accounts`: `(id, user_id, provider, provider_subject_id, email_at_provider)`\n- `user_sessions`: `(id, user_id, refresh_token_hash, user_agent, client_ip_anonymized, expires_at, revoked_at)`\n\n*Optional Index Additions for Phase 2*:\n- `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_sessions_hash ON user_sessions(refresh_token_hash) WHERE revoked_at IS NULL;`\n\n---\n\n## 7. API Endpoints for Phase 2\n\n| Method | Path | Description | Access Control | Security Controls |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/start` | Initiates Google OAuth flow, sets PKCE/state cookie, redirects to Google | Public | Rate limited (20 req/min), CSPRNG state/nonce |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` | Handles OAuth redirect, verifies state, fetches Google token, creates session | Public | State cookie verification, direct Google TLS call |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/refresh` | Refreshes access token and rotates refresh token | Session Cookie / Bearer | Strict token rotation, replay detection |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/logout` | Revokes current session and clears authentication cookies | Authenticated | Session invalidation in PostgreSQL |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/me` | Returns profile, active sessions count, and current role | Authenticated (`user`, `admin`, `auditor`) | Fast stateless JWT verification |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions` | Lists user\'s active sessions (device type, creation, last active) | Authenticated | User-scoped only |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions/:id` | Revokes a specific active session | Authenticated | User-scoped (cannot revoke other users\' sessions) |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/admin/users` | Lists users across the system (RBAC test endpoint) | Admin Only (`role === \'admin\'`) | Strict RBAC middleware check (403 if normal user) |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Security & Authorization Model\n\n### RBAC Enforcement Middleware\n```typescript\nexport function requireRole(allowedRoles: Array<\'user\' | \'admin\' | \'auditor\'>) {\n  return (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {\n    if (!req.user) {\n      return res.status(401).json({ success: false, error: { code: \'UNAUTHORIZED\', message: \'Authentication required\' } });\n    }\n    if (!allowedRoles.includes(req.user.role)) {\n      return res.status(403).json({ success: false, error: { code: \'FORBIDDEN\', message: \'Insufficient privileges\' } });\n    }\n    next();\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Defense Against Common Attacks:\n1. **OAuth State & CSRF Attacks**:\n   - `state` is a 256-bit CSPRNG string stored in an encrypted/signed HMAC `httpOnly` cookie. Mismatched state returns HTTP 400.\n2. **Authorization Code Injection**:\n   - Mitigated by mandatory PKCE (`code_verifier` + `code_challenge` SHA-256) even in confidential client flows.\n3. **Token Replay / Session Theft**:\n   - If an old refresh token is reused after it was already rotated, the system immediately flags this as a replay anomaly and revokes all sessions belonging to that user ID.\n4. **Open Redirects**:\n   - Post-login redirects are strictly validated against a hardcoded whitelist (e.g. root `/`) or relative paths, preventing redirects to arbitrary external URLs.\n5. **Confused Deputy & Identity Spoofing**:\n   - The backend validates that `aud` in Google\'s ID token matches our exact `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, preventing tokens issued for other applications from authenticating into Privacy Gateway.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Expanded Threat Model (Authentication Phase)\n\n| Threat | Attack Vector | Mitigation & Technical Control | Residual Risk |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Tampered State Parameter** | Attacker initiates login and swaps the state to bind an victim\'s session. | Signed/encrypted state cookie verified before code exchange. | None. |\n| **Stolen Refresh Token Cookie** | Attacker extracts refresh token cookie via physical device access. | Automatic token rotation on every use + instant revocation of all sessions if a rotated token is re-submitted. | Window of access until token is refreshed or rotated. |\n| **Forged Google ID Token** | Attacker crafts a JWT claiming `email: admin@privacygateway.net`. | Verification against Google\'s live public RSA keys (`https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs`) with strict `iss` and `aud` assertions. | None; forgery is cryptographically impossible without Google\'s private key. |\n| **Unverified Email Hijack** | Attacker registers unverified email on Google to claim an existing account. | The backend asserts `email_verified === true`. Unverified tokens are rejected immediately. | None. |\n| **Privilege Escalation via JWT** | User tampers with `role` field in access token payload. | Access tokens are cryptographically signed with backend secret/key; modified signatures are rejected immediately. | None. |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Testing Strategy\n\nAutomated tests will be created in `apps/api/tests/auth.test.ts` covering:\n\n1. **State & Nonce Validation**:\n   - Callback with missing state \342\206\222 400 Bad Request.\n   - Callback with mismatched state \342\206\222 400 Bad Request.\n2. **ID Token Verification Logic**:\n   - Invalid token signature \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Wrong audience (`aud !== GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`) \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Wrong issuer (`iss !== accounts.google.com`) \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Expired token (`exp < now`) \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Unverified email (`email_verified: false`) \342\206\222 403 Forbidden.\n3. **Session Lifecycle**:\n   - Successful first-time registration creates user and linked account.\n   - Returning login reuses existing user ID.\n   - Token refresh successfully issues new access and rotated refresh token.\n   - Replay of revoked refresh token triggers total session invalidation.\n   - Logout clears cookies and marks DB session as revoked.\n4. **RBAC & Authorization Boundaries**:\n   - Unauthenticated request to `/api/v1/auth/me` \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Normal user (`role = \'user\'`) accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` \342\206\222 403 Forbidden.\n   - Admin user (`role = \'admin\'`) accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` \342\206\222 200 OK.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Environment Variables Required\n\nUpdated in `.env.example`:\n\n```env\n# Google OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect\n# Obtain from Google Cloud Console (APIs & Services > Credentials)\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_development_google_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_development_google_client_secret\nGOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback\n\n# JWT & Cookie Cryptographic Secrets\nJWT_SECRET=development_jwt_secret_min_32_chars_long_change_in_production\nCOOKIE_SECRET=development_cookie_secret_min_32_chars_long_change_in_production\n```\n\n---\n\n## 12. Files to be Created & Modified in Phase 2\n\n```\napps/api/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 env.ts                     # Add GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, JWT_SECRET, COOKIE_SECRET validation\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oauth.ts                   # Google OAuth2 client & JWKS key fetcher\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.controller.ts         # startGoogleAuth, handleGoogleCallback, refreshSession, logout, getMe\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.middleware.ts         # requireAuth, requireRole, optionalAuth\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.routes.ts             # Mount /api/v1/auth/* endpoints\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.routes.ts            # Mount /api/v1/admin/* RBAC test endpoints\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.service.ts            # User upsert, account linking, session hashing & rotation\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 token.service.ts           # Access token (JWT) & Refresh token generation/verification\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 app.ts                         # Register cookie-parser, auth routes, and admin routes\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n    \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.test.ts                   # Automated OIDC, session rotation, and RBAC test suite\n\npackages/shared-types/src/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api.ts                             # Auth DTOs (LoginResponse, UserProfileDto, SessionDto)\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.ts\n\nsrc/App.tsx (or apps/web/src/App.tsx)\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Update web UI with:\n    - Real \"Sign in with Google\" button triggering `/api/v1/auth/google/start`\n    - Live authenticated user profile card (Email, Display Name, Avatar, Role badge)\n    - Session management list with revoke buttons\n    - Role-based authorization tester (test standard vs admin permissions)\n    - Logout action\n```\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2 Plan Confirmation\n\nThis plan outlines the complete **Phase 2 Google OAuth/OIDC & Session Engine**. Please review and provide your approval so we can begin the implementation."
    modified_response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 2: GOOGLE OAUTH / OIDC AUTHENTICATION ARCHITECTURE & SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Phase 2 Architecture Overview\n\nPhase 2 establishes real OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity verification and a cryptographically hardened application session engine.\n\nThe authentication engine sits on the **Control Plane**, operating with strict privilege separation. The frontend never supplies verified identity claims directly; instead, the backend exchanges the authorization code directly with Google\'s servers over TLS, verifies the OIDC ID Token signature against Google\'s public JSON Web Key Sets (JWKS), and issues dual-token application sessions (short-lived stateless JWT access tokens + rotated CSPRNG refresh tokens stored in hashed form).\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                     PHASE 2 AUTHENTICATION ARCHITECTURE\n========================================================================================================================\n\n+--------------------------------------------------+        +--------------------------------------------------+\n|               WEB CLIENT (Browser)               |        |           FUTURE DESKTOP CLIENT (Tauri)          |\n|  * Initiates login: GET /api/v1/auth/google/start|        |  * Local RFC 8252 loopback http://127.0.0.1:port |\n|  * Receives signed httpOnly session cookies      |        |  * Exchanges auth code with PKCE code_verifier   |\n+------------------------+-------------------------+        +------------------------+-------------------------+\n                         |                                                           |\n                         | HTTPS Redirect & API Requests                             | HTTPS Loopback & Code Exchange\n                         v                                                           v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                             PRIVACY GATEWAY BACKEND API                                              |\n|                                                                                                                      |\n|   +------------------------------------+          +--------------------------------------------------------------+   |\n|   |       OAuth & PKCE Controller      |          |                Session & Token Engine                        |   |\n|   |  * Cryptographic State & Nonce Gen |          |  * Stateless Access Token (JWT RS256/HS256, 15m expiration)  |   |\n|   |  * S256 Code Challenge Validation  |          |  * CSPRNG 256-bit Refresh Token (SHA-256 Hash Stored, 30d)   |   |\n|   |  * Google Token Direct HTTPS Post  |          |  * Automatic Refresh Token Rotation & Replay Detection       |   |\n|   +-----------------+------------------+          +------------------------------+-------------------------------+   |\n|                     |                                                            |                                   |\n|                     v Direct TLS 1.3 (Server-to-Server)                          v Transactional SQL                 |\n|   +------------------------------------+          +--------------------------------------------------------------+   |\n|   |   Google Identity OIDC Provider    |          |                 PostgreSQL 16 Storage                        |   |\n|   |   (accounts.google.com / JWKS)     |          |  * `users` (Identity, Role, Retention)                      |   |\n|   |  * Validates iss, aud, exp, certs  |          |  * `authentication_accounts` (google, provider_sub)           |   |\n|   |  * Validates email_verified == true|          |  * `user_sessions` (token_hash, expiration, revoked_at)     |   |\n|   +------------------------------------+          +--------------------------------------------------------------+   |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Google OAuth 2.0 / OIDC Flow Diagram\n\n```\nBrowser / Desktop                Privacy Gateway API                   Google Identity Services (OIDC)\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       | 1. GET /api/v1/auth/google/start |                                          |\n       |--------------------------------->|                                          |\n       |                                  | 2. Generate CSPRNG state, nonce, PKCE    |\n       |                                  |    Store state/verifier in cookie/cache  |\n       | 3. 302 Redirect to Google Auth   |                                          |\n       |<---------------------------------|                                          |\n       |                                                                             |\n       | 4. User navigates to Google Consent (accounts.google.com)                   |\n       |---------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                                                             |\n       | 5. User approves; Google redirects with ?code=...&state=...                 |\n       |<----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                                                             |\n       | 6. GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback?code=...&state=...                      |\n       |--------------------------------->|                                          |\n       |                                  | 7. Validate `state` against signed cookie|\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       |                                  | 8. POST https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token\n       |                                  |    { code, client_id, secret, verifier } |\n       |                                  |----------------------------------------->|\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       |                                  | 9. Returns { id_token, access_token }    |\n       |                                  |<-----------------------------------------|\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       |                                  | 10. Cryptographic Verification:          |\n       |                                  |     a. Fetch & cache Google JWKS certs   |\n       |                                  |     b. Verify RS256 signature            |\n       |                                  |     c. Check iss == accounts.google.com  |\n       |                                  |     d. Check aud == GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID     |\n       |                                  |     e. Check exp > now                   |\n       |                                  |     f. Check email_verified == true      |\n       |                                  |     g. Check nonce == generated nonce    |\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       |                                  | 11. Database Operations (Transaction):   |\n       |                                  |     - Find or create `users`             |\n       |                                  |     - Link `authentication_accounts`     |\n       |                                  |     - Create `user_sessions` (hash only) |\n       |                                  |                                          |\n       | 12. Set-Cookie: access_token,    |                                          |\n       |     Set-Cookie: refresh_token    |                                          |\n       |     302 Redirect to Dashboard    |                                          |\n       |<---------------------------------|                                          |\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Web Authentication Flow Details\n\n1. **Start Request (`GET /api/v1/auth/google/start`)**:\n   - Generates 32 bytes of cryptographically secure random entropy for `state`, `nonce`, and `code_verifier`.\n   - Computes `code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier))`.\n   - Encrypts/signs a short-lived, transient cookie (`pg_oauth_state`) containing `{ state, nonce, code_verifier, return_to }` with `httpOnly`, `secure`, `sameSite: \'lax\'`, `maxAge: 300000` (5 minutes).\n   - Generates the Google authorization URL:\n     ```\n     https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?\n       client_id=GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID&\n       response_type=code&\n       scope=openid%20email%20profile&\n       redirect_uri=APP_URL/api/v1/auth/google/callback&\n       state=ENCRYPTED_STATE&\n       nonce=NONCE&\n       code_challenge=CODE_CHALLENGE&\n       code_challenge_method=S256&\n       prompt=select_account\n     ```\n   - Responds with `302 Redirect` to Google.\n\n2. **Callback Request (`GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback`)**:\n   - Parses `code` and `state` from URL query.\n   - Verifies that `state` exactly matches the value in the signed `pg_oauth_state` cookie (mitigating CSRF and state tampering). Clears `pg_oauth_state`.\n   - Executes direct back-channel HTTP POST to `https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token` with the authorization code and `code_verifier`.\n   - Validates the received `id_token` (as detailed in Section 2).\n   - Upserts the user in PostgreSQL:\n     - If the email does not exist: Creates record in `users` (default role `user`) and creates record in `authentication_accounts` (`provider = \'google\'`).\n     - If the email exists: Links record in `authentication_accounts` if not already linked.\n   - Creates a new session in `user_sessions`:\n     - Generates 64-character hex CSPRNG `refresh_token`.\n     - Computes `refresh_token_hash = SHA256(refresh_token)`.\n     - Inserts `{ user_id, refresh_token_hash, expires_at: now + 30 days, user_agent, client_ip_anonymized }`.\n   - Mints a short-lived JWT `access_token` (15m expiry) containing `{ sub: user.id, email: user.email, role: user.role }`.\n   - Issues `httpOnly`, `secure`, `sameSite: \'lax\'` cookies for `pg_access_token` and `pg_refresh_token`.\n   - Redirects user back to `/` (Dashboard).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Future Desktop Authentication Flow (RFC 8252 Loopback)\n\nThe authentication architecture is engineered from Day 1 to support the future Tauri desktop client without backend refactoring:\n\n1. **Loopback Binding**: The future desktop app opens a dynamic TCP socket on loopback (`http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>`).\n2. **PKCE in Native App**: Desktop generates its own `code_verifier` and `code_challenge`.\n3. **Desktop Start Endpoint (`POST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/start`)**:\n   - Backend registers the desktop redirect URI (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback`) and returns the Google Auth URL.\n4. **System Browser Execution**: Desktop client opens the system\'s default browser (Safari, Chrome, Edge) to Google\'s consent screen.\n5. **Loopback Redirection**: After Google approves, the browser redirects to the local loopback server (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback?code=...&state=...`).\n6. **Desktop Direct Code Exchange (`POST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange`)**:\n   - Desktop sends `{ code, code_verifier, state, redirect_uri }` to the backend.\n   - Backend exchanges the code directly with Google and validates the ID token.\n   - Instead of setting web cookies, the backend returns JSON `{ access_token, refresh_token, user }`.\n   - The desktop client securely persists the refresh token in the OS Keyring / Windows Credential Manager / macOS Keychain.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Session Architecture & Lifecycle\n\n```\n                                    SESSION LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT\n\n1. ISSUANCE (On Login)\n   - Access Token: RS256/HS256 JWT, lifespan = 15 minutes. Contains { sub, email, role, jti }.\n   - Refresh Token: 256-bit CSPRNG hex string. Lifespan = 30 days.\n   - Stored in DB: SHA-256(refresh_token). The raw refresh token is ONLY sent to the client.\n\n2. AUTHENTICATION / AUTHORIZATION (Per Request)\n   - API middleware checks `pg_access_token` cookie (or `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header).\n   - Verifies signature, expiration, and issuer statelessly in <1ms.\n   - Injects `req.user = { id, email, role }`.\n\n3. REFRESH & TOKEN ROTATION (`POST /api/v1/auth/refresh`)\n   - Client presents current `refresh_token`.\n   - Computes `incoming_hash = SHA256(refresh_token)`.\n   - Queries `user_sessions WHERE refresh_token_hash = incoming_hash AND revoked_at IS NULL`.\n   - If not found or expired:\n     * Check if hash matches an already revoked session. If yes, TRIGGER REPLAY ATTACK ALERT: Revoke ALL active sessions for that user immediately (session theft protection).\n     * Return 401 Unauthorized.\n   - If valid:\n     * Generates a NEW `refresh_token` and updates database record with `new_refresh_token_hash` (Token Rotation).\n     * Mints a fresh 15-minute `access_token`.\n     * Sets updated cookies.\n\n4. LOGOUT & REVOCATION (`POST /api/v1/auth/logout`)\n   - Computes `incoming_hash = SHA256(refresh_token)`.\n   - Sets `revoked_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` for that session.\n   - Clears auth cookies on client.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Database Changes\n\nNo destructive schema changes are needed; Phase 1\'s `001_initial_schema.sql` pre-provisioned the required tables:\n- `users`: `(id, email, display_name, avatar_url, role, is_active, data_retention_days)`\n- `authentication_accounts`: `(id, user_id, provider, provider_subject_id, email_at_provider)`\n- `user_sessions`: `(id, user_id, refresh_token_hash, user_agent, client_ip_anonymized, expires_at, revoked_at)`\n\n*Optional Index Additions for Phase 2*:\n- `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_sessions_hash ON user_sessions(refresh_token_hash) WHERE revoked_at IS NULL;`\n\n---\n\n## 7. API Endpoints for Phase 2\n\n| Method | Path | Description | Access Control | Security Controls |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/start` | Initiates Google OAuth flow, sets PKCE/state cookie, redirects to Google | Public | Rate limited (20 req/min), CSPRNG state/nonce |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` | Handles OAuth redirect, verifies state, fetches Google token, creates session | Public | State cookie verification, direct Google TLS call |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/refresh` | Refreshes access token and rotates refresh token | Session Cookie / Bearer | Strict token rotation, replay detection |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/logout` | Revokes current session and clears authentication cookies | Authenticated | Session invalidation in PostgreSQL |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/me` | Returns profile, active sessions count, and current role | Authenticated (`user`, `admin`, `auditor`) | Fast stateless JWT verification |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions` | Lists user\'s active sessions (device type, creation, last active) | Authenticated | User-scoped only |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions/:id` | Revokes a specific active session | Authenticated | User-scoped (cannot revoke other users\' sessions) |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/admin/users` | Lists users across the system (RBAC test endpoint) | Admin Only (`role === \'admin\'`) | Strict RBAC middleware check (403 if normal user) |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Security & Authorization Model\n\n### RBAC Enforcement Middleware\n```typescript\nexport function requireRole(allowedRoles: Array<\'user\' | \'admin\' | \'auditor\'>) {\n  return (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {\n    if (!req.user) {\n      return res.status(401).json({ success: false, error: { code: \'UNAUTHORIZED\', message: \'Authentication required\' } });\n    }\n    if (!allowedRoles.includes(req.user.role)) {\n      return res.status(403).json({ success: false, error: { code: \'FORBIDDEN\', message: \'Insufficient privileges\' } });\n    }\n    next();\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Defense Against Common Attacks:\n1. **OAuth State & CSRF Attacks**:\n   - `state` is a 256-bit CSPRNG string stored in an encrypted/signed HMAC `httpOnly` cookie. Mismatched state returns HTTP 400.\n2. **Authorization Code Injection**:\n   - Mitigated by mandatory PKCE (`code_verifier` + `code_challenge` SHA-256) even in confidential client flows.\n3. **Token Replay / Session Theft**:\n   - If an old refresh token is reused after it was already rotated, the system immediately flags this as a replay anomaly and revokes all sessions belonging to that user ID.\n4. **Open Redirects**:\n   - Post-login redirects are strictly validated against a hardcoded whitelist (e.g. root `/`) or relative paths, preventing redirects to arbitrary external URLs.\n5. **Confused Deputy & Identity Spoofing**:\n   - The backend validates that `aud` in Google\'s ID token matches our exact `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, preventing tokens issued for other applications from authenticating into Privacy Gateway.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Expanded Threat Model (Authentication Phase)\n\n| Threat | Attack Vector | Mitigation & Technical Control | Residual Risk |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Tampered State Parameter** | Attacker initiates login and swaps the state to bind an victim\'s session. | Signed/encrypted state cookie verified before code exchange. | None. |\n| **Stolen Refresh Token Cookie** | Attacker extracts refresh token cookie via physical device access. | Automatic token rotation on every use + instant revocation of all sessions if a rotated token is re-submitted. | Window of access until token is refreshed or rotated. |\n| **Forged Google ID Token** | Attacker crafts a JWT claiming `email: admin@privacygateway.net`. | Verification against Google\'s live public RSA keys (`https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs`) with strict `iss` and `aud` assertions. | None; forgery is cryptographically impossible without Google\'s private key. |\n| **Unverified Email Hijack** | Attacker registers unverified email on Google to claim an existing account. | The backend asserts `email_verified === true`. Unverified tokens are rejected immediately. | None. |\n| **Privilege Escalation via JWT** | User tampers with `role` field in access token payload. | Access tokens are cryptographically signed with backend secret/key; modified signatures are rejected immediately. | None. |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Testing Strategy\n\nAutomated tests will be created in `apps/api/tests/auth.test.ts` covering:\n\n1. **State & Nonce Validation**:\n   - Callback with missing state \342\206\222 400 Bad Request.\n   - Callback with mismatched state \342\206\222 400 Bad Request.\n2. **ID Token Verification Logic**:\n   - Invalid token signature \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Wrong audience (`aud !== GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`) \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Wrong issuer (`iss !== accounts.google.com`) \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Expired token (`exp < now`) \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Unverified email (`email_verified: false`) \342\206\222 403 Forbidden.\n3. **Session Lifecycle**:\n   - Successful first-time registration creates user and linked account.\n   - Returning login reuses existing user ID.\n   - Token refresh successfully issues new access and rotated refresh token.\n   - Replay of revoked refresh token triggers total session invalidation.\n   - Logout clears cookies and marks DB session as revoked.\n4. **RBAC & Authorization Boundaries**:\n   - Unauthenticated request to `/api/v1/auth/me` \342\206\222 401 Unauthorized.\n   - Normal user (`role = \'user\'`) accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` \342\206\222 403 Forbidden.\n   - Admin user (`role = \'admin\'`) accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` \342\206\222 200 OK.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Environment Variables Required\n\nUpdated in `.env.example`:\n\n```env\n# Google OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect\n# Obtain from Google Cloud Console (APIs & Services > Credentials)\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_development_google_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_development_google_client_secret\nGOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback\n\n# JWT & Cookie Cryptographic Secrets\nJWT_SECRET=development_jwt_secret_min_32_chars_long_change_in_production\nCOOKIE_SECRET=development_cookie_secret_min_32_chars_long_change_in_production\n```\n\n---\n\n## 12. Files to be Created & Modified in Phase 2\n\n```\napps/api/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 env.ts                     # Add GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, JWT_SECRET, COOKIE_SECRET validation\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oauth.ts                   # Google OAuth2 client & JWKS key fetcher\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.controller.ts         # startGoogleAuth, handleGoogleCallback, refreshSession, logout, getMe\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.middleware.ts         # requireAuth, requireRole, optionalAuth\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.routes.ts             # Mount /api/v1/auth/* endpoints\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.routes.ts            # Mount /api/v1/admin/* RBAC test endpoints\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.service.ts            # User upsert, account linking, session hashing & rotation\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 token.service.ts           # Access token (JWT) & Refresh token generation/verification\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 app.ts                         # Register cookie-parser, auth routes, and admin routes\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n    \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.test.ts                   # Automated OIDC, session rotation, and RBAC test suite\n\npackages/shared-types/src/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api.ts                             # Auth DTOs (LoginResponse, UserProfileDto, SessionDto)\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.ts\n\nsrc/App.tsx (or apps/web/src/App.tsx)\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Update web UI with:\n    - Real \"Sign in with Google\" button triggering `/api/v1/auth/google/start`\n    - Live authenticated user profile card (Email, Display Name, Avatar, Role badge)\n    - Session management list with revoke buttons\n    - Role-based authorization tester (test standard vs admin permissions)\n    - Logout action\n```\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2 Plan Confirmation\n\nThis plan outlines the complete **Phase 2 Google OAuth/OIDC & Session Engine**. Please review and provide your approval so we can begin the implementation."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
