  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787382682
      nanos: 996772081
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Phase 2 architecture is approved in principle, but make the following security corrections BEFORE implementation.\n\nDo not write implementation code yet.\n\n1. OAuth State / PKCE Storage\n\nDo not store the OAuth state, nonce, and PKCE code_verifier directly in a browser cookie if Redis can be used.\n\nUse Redis for short-lived OAuth transaction state:\n\nstate\n\342\206\222 nonce\n\342\206\222 PKCE code_verifier\n\342\206\222 return_to\n\342\206\222 created_at / expiry\n\nUse a short TTL (approximately 5 minutes).\n\nThe browser should receive only the opaque state value required to correlate the OAuth transaction.\n\nEnsure the state is single-use and deleted after successful or failed callback processing.\n\nDo not log OAuth state, nonce, code_verifier, authorization codes, access tokens, or ID tokens.\n\n2. JWT Algorithm\n\nDo NOT leave the JWT algorithm as \"RS256/HS256\".\n\nUse RS256.\n\nUse asymmetric signing:\n\n- private signing key is held only by the token-issuing backend\n- public key is used for verification\n- JWT must contain issuer, audience, subject, expiration, issued-at and jti\n- reject unexpected algorithms\n- reject invalid issuer\n- reject invalid audience\n- reject expired tokens\n\nDo not put the JWT private signing key in source code.\n\n3. Refresh Token Security\n\nImplement refresh-token rotation transactionally.\n\nAdd a token-family concept.\n\nEvery login creates a unique session/token family.\n\nEvery successful refresh:\n\n- invalidate the previous refresh token\n- create the replacement token\n- store only the replacement token hash\n- keep the same token family\n- perform the operation atomically\n\nIf an already-rotated refresh token is presented again:\n\n- treat it as token reuse/replay\n- revoke the affected token family\n- optionally revoke all sessions for the user according to the documented security policy\n- create a security/audit event\n\nAvoid false-positive mass revocation caused by legitimate concurrent refresh requests.\n\nAdd tests for concurrent refresh attempts.\n\n4. Google Identity Linking\n\nDo NOT automatically link a Google account to an existing Privacy Gateway account solely because the email addresses match.\n\nUse Google\'s stable OIDC `sub` claim as the primary provider identity:\n\n(provider, provider_subject_id)\n\nIf an account already exists:\n\n- authenticate the existing Google identity if the provider subject matches\n- otherwise require an explicit authenticated account-linking flow\n\nDo not allow an attacker who controls an email address to automatically attach a new identity provider to another account.\n\n5. Google OIDC Validation\n\nValidate all required claims:\n\n- signature\n- algorithm\n- issuer\n- audience\n- expiration\n- issued-at where appropriate\n- nonce\n- email_verified\n- subject\n\nUse Google\'s official discovery/JWKS mechanism.\n\nCache JWKS keys appropriately and handle key rotation.\n\nDo not hardcode certificate material.\n\n6. Desktop OAuth Architecture\n\nKeep the future Tauri authentication architecture compatible with RFC 8252 loopback authentication.\n\nHowever, Phase 2 must NOT implement the Tauri client.\n\nDo not claim that the backend can dynamically register arbitrary loopback ports with Google.\n\nDocument the exact Google OAuth client configuration required for the future native desktop application and clearly separate:\n\nWEB OAUTH\nfrom\nNATIVE DESKTOP OAUTH\n\n7. Session Cookies\n\nFor web sessions:\n\n- HttpOnly\n- Secure in production\n- SameSite=Lax or stricter where compatible\n- appropriate Path\n- explicit expiration/max-age\n\nDocument CSRF protections for every cookie-authenticated state-changing endpoint.\n\n8. Account Sessions / IP Privacy\n\nThe current schema contains client IP prefix information.\n\nBefore implementation, evaluate whether Phase 2 actually needs this.\n\nPrefer not storing client IP information unless there is a concrete security requirement.\n\nIf retained:\n\n- document exactly what is stored\n- minimize precision\n- define retention\n- never log the full IP\n\n9. OAuth Callback Security\n\nThe callback must:\n\n- accept only expected parameters\n- reject missing/invalid state\n- reject reused state\n- reject unexpected redirect behavior\n- exchange the authorization code only once\n- clear OAuth transaction state after completion\n- never expose tokens in URLs after authentication\n\n10. Tests\n\nAdd tests for:\n\n- state generation\n- state mismatch\n- state reuse\n- expired state\n- nonce mismatch\n- invalid issuer\n- invalid audience\n- invalid signature\n- wrong JWT algorithm\n- expired JWT\n- account creation\n- existing Google identity login\n- account-linking protection\n- refresh-token rotation\n- refresh-token replay\n- concurrent refresh\n- logout\n- RBAC\n- CSRF protections\n\n11. No Future Implementation\n\nDo not implement:\n\n- WireGuard\n- VPN peer management\n- Tauri\n- browser extension\n- tracker detection\n- fingerprint analysis\n- DNS/WebRTC detection\n- privacy scoring\n- billing\n- multi-region infrastructure\n\nThose remain future phases.\n\n12. Before Coding\n\nProduce the revised Phase 2.1 specification containing:\n\n- revised OAuth flow\n- Redis OAuth transaction model\n- revised JWT model\n- refresh-token family model\n- account-linking model\n- web session model\n- future desktop authentication model\n- API endpoints\n- database changes\n- security boundaries\n- threat model\n- test plan\n- environment variables\n- exact files to modify/create\n\nThen STOP and wait for approval.\n\nDo not implement code until I approve Phase 2.1."
    items {
      text: "Phase 2 architecture is approved in principle, but make the following security corrections BEFORE implementation.\n\nDo not write implementation code yet.\n\n1. OAuth State / PKCE Storage\n\nDo not store the OAuth state, nonce, and PKCE code_verifier directly in a browser cookie if Redis can be used.\n\nUse Redis for short-lived OAuth transaction state:\n\nstate\n\342\206\222 nonce\n\342\206\222 PKCE code_verifier\n\342\206\222 return_to\n\342\206\222 created_at / expiry\n\nUse a short TTL (approximately 5 minutes).\n\nThe browser should receive only the opaque state value required to correlate the OAuth transaction.\n\nEnsure the state is single-use and deleted after successful or failed callback processing.\n\nDo not log OAuth state, nonce, code_verifier, authorization codes, access tokens, or ID tokens.\n\n2. JWT Algorithm\n\nDo NOT leave the JWT algorithm as \"RS256/HS256\".\n\nUse RS256.\n\nUse asymmetric signing:\n\n- private signing key is held only by the token-issuing backend\n- public key is used for verification\n- JWT must contain issuer, audience, subject, expiration, issued-at and jti\n- reject unexpected algorithms\n- reject invalid issuer\n- reject invalid audience\n- reject expired tokens\n\nDo not put the JWT private signing key in source code.\n\n3. Refresh Token Security\n\nImplement refresh-token rotation transactionally.\n\nAdd a token-family concept.\n\nEvery login creates a unique session/token family.\n\nEvery successful refresh:\n\n- invalidate the previous refresh token\n- create the replacement token\n- store only the replacement token hash\n- keep the same token family\n- perform the operation atomically\n\nIf an already-rotated refresh token is presented again:\n\n- treat it as token reuse/replay\n- revoke the affected token family\n- optionally revoke all sessions for the user according to the documented security policy\n- create a security/audit event\n\nAvoid false-positive mass revocation caused by legitimate concurrent refresh requests.\n\nAdd tests for concurrent refresh attempts.\n\n4. Google Identity Linking\n\nDo NOT automatically link a Google account to an existing Privacy Gateway account solely because the email addresses match.\n\nUse Google\'s stable OIDC `sub` claim as the primary provider identity:\n\n(provider, provider_subject_id)\n\nIf an account already exists:\n\n- authenticate the existing Google identity if the provider subject matches\n- otherwise require an explicit authenticated account-linking flow\n\nDo not allow an attacker who controls an email address to automatically attach a new identity provider to another account.\n\n5. Google OIDC Validation\n\nValidate all required claims:\n\n- signature\n- algorithm\n- issuer\n- audience\n- expiration\n- issued-at where appropriate\n- nonce\n- email_verified\n- subject\n\nUse Google\'s official discovery/JWKS mechanism.\n\nCache JWKS keys appropriately and handle key rotation.\n\nDo not hardcode certificate material.\n\n6. Desktop OAuth Architecture\n\nKeep the future Tauri authentication architecture compatible with RFC 8252 loopback authentication.\n\nHowever, Phase 2 must NOT implement the Tauri client.\n\nDo not claim that the backend can dynamically register arbitrary loopback ports with Google.\n\nDocument the exact Google OAuth client configuration required for the future native desktop application and clearly separate:\n\nWEB OAUTH\nfrom\nNATIVE DESKTOP OAUTH\n\n7. Session Cookies\n\nFor web sessions:\n\n- HttpOnly\n- Secure in production\n- SameSite=Lax or stricter where compatible\n- appropriate Path\n- explicit expiration/max-age\n\nDocument CSRF protections for every cookie-authenticated state-changing endpoint.\n\n8. Account Sessions / IP Privacy\n\nThe current schema contains client IP prefix information.\n\nBefore implementation, evaluate whether Phase 2 actually needs this.\n\nPrefer not storing client IP information unless there is a concrete security requirement.\n\nIf retained:\n\n- document exactly what is stored\n- minimize precision\n- define retention\n- never log the full IP\n\n9. OAuth Callback Security\n\nThe callback must:\n\n- accept only expected parameters\n- reject missing/invalid state\n- reject reused state\n- reject unexpected redirect behavior\n- exchange the authorization code only once\n- clear OAuth transaction state after completion\n- never expose tokens in URLs after authentication\n\n10. Tests\n\nAdd tests for:\n\n- state generation\n- state mismatch\n- state reuse\n- expired state\n- nonce mismatch\n- invalid issuer\n- invalid audience\n- invalid signature\n- wrong JWT algorithm\n- expired JWT\n- account creation\n- existing Google identity login\n- account-linking protection\n- refresh-token rotation\n- refresh-token replay\n- concurrent refresh\n- logout\n- RBAC\n- CSRF protections\n\n11. No Future Implementation\n\nDo not implement:\n\n- WireGuard\n- VPN peer management\n- Tauri\n- browser extension\n- tracker detection\n- fingerprint analysis\n- DNS/WebRTC detection\n- privacy scoring\n- billing\n- multi-region infrastructure\n\nThose remain future phases.\n\n12. Before Coding\n\nProduce the revised Phase 2.1 specification containing:\n\n- revised OAuth flow\n- Redis OAuth transaction model\n- revised JWT model\n- refresh-token family model\n- account-linking model\n- web session model\n- future desktop authentication model\n- API endpoints\n- database changes\n- security boundaries\n- threat model\n- test plan\n- environment variables\n- exact files to modify/create\n\nThen STOP and wait for approval.\n\nDo not implement code until I approve Phase 2.1."
    }
  }
