  planner_response {
    response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 2.1: REVISED AUTHENTICATION & OIDC SECURITY SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Revised OAuth & OIDC Flow Diagram (Redis State Model)\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                     PHASE 2.1: REDIS-BACKED OAUTH & PKCE FLOW\n========================================================================================================================\n\nBrowser / Client                  Privacy Gateway Backend                     Redis In-Memory Bus              Google OIDC (accounts.google.com)\n       |                                     |                                         |                                       |\n       | 1. GET /api/v1/auth/google/start    |                                         |                                       |\n       |------------------------------------>|                                         |                                       |\n       |                                     | 2. Generate CSPRNG:                     |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - state (32-byte hex)                |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - nonce (32-byte hex)                |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - code_verifier (32-byte hex)        |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - code_challenge = S256(verifier)    |                                       |\n       |                                     |                                         |                                       |\n       |                                     | 3. SET `pg:oauth:state:<state>`         |                                       |\n       |                                     |    { nonce, verifier, return_to }       |                                       |\n       |                                     |    EXPIRE = 300s (5 minutes)            |                                       |\n       |                                     |---------------------------------------->|                                       |\n       | 4. 302 Redirect to Google Auth URL  |                                         |                                       |\n       |    (?client_id=...&state=<state>    |                                         |                                       |\n       |     &code_challenge=...&nonce=...)  |                                         |                                       |\n       |<------------------------------------|                                         |                                       |\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 5. User signs in on accounts.google.com with consent                                                                  |\n       |---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 6. 302 Redirect with ?code=AUTH_CODE&state=<state>                                                                    |\n       |<----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 7. GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback?code=AUTH_CODE&state=<state>                                                      |\n       |------------------------------------>|                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 8. ATOMIC GETDEL `pg:oauth:state:<state>`                                       |\n       |                                     |    (Single-use consumption; prevents replay)                                    |\n       |                                     |---------------------------------------->|                                       |\n       |                                     |<----------------------------------------|                                       |\n       |                                     |    If NOT FOUND or EXPIRED -> 400 Bad Request                                   |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 9. POST https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token                                     |\n       |                                     |    { code, client_id, client_secret, code_verifier, redirect_uri }              |\n       |                                     |-------------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                     |<--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                     |    Returns { id_token, access_token }                                           |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 10. Cryptographic OIDC ID Token Verification:                                   |\n       |                                     |     a. Fetch / Cache Google JWKS via Discovery (Rotating Cache)                 |\n       |                                     |     b. Verify RS256 signature with matching `kid`                               |\n       |                                     |     c. Assert `iss` in [\'https://accounts.google.com\', \'accounts.google.com\']   |\n       |                                     |     d. Assert `aud` === GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID                                        |\n       |                                     |     e. Assert `exp` > now and `iat` <= now                                      |\n       |                                     |     f. Assert `nonce` === stored_nonce                                          |\n       |                                     |     g. Assert `email_verified` === true                                         |\n       |                                     |     h. Extract stable Google `sub` and `email`                                  |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 11. Transactional SQL Identity & Token Family Creation:                         |\n       |                                     |     a. Query `authentication_accounts WHERE provider=\'google\' AND provider_sub` |\n       |                                     |     b. If exists -> Authenticate existing user                                  |\n       |                                     |     c. If NOT exists -> Check if `users` table already has that email:          |\n       |                                     |        * If yes -> REJECT (Prevent unauthenticated account takeover)            |\n       |                                     |        * If no -> INSERT INTO `users` & INSERT INTO `authentication_accounts`    |\n       |                                     |     d. INSERT INTO `user_sessions` with unique `family_id` & `token_hash`       |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 12. Issue RS256 JWT Access Token (15m) + CSPRNG Refresh Token (30d)             |\n       | 13. Set-Cookie: `pg_access_token`,  |                                                                                 |\n       |     Set-Cookie: `pg_refresh_token`  |                                                                                 |\n       |     302 Redirect to Dashboard (/)   |                                                                                 |\n       |<------------------------------------|                                                                                 |\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Redis OAuth Transaction State Model\n\nAll ephemeral authorization parameters are held in Redis, ensuring zero state exposure in client cookies.\n\n### Key Schema:\n```redis\npg:oauth:state:<state_hex_string>\n```\n\n### Value Payload (JSON):\n```json\n{\n  \"nonce\": \"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\",\n  \"code_verifier\": \"f8a1c3d5e7b9a2c4e6f8a0b2d4e6f8a0b2c4d6e8f0a2b4c6d8e0f2a4b6c8d0e2\",\n  \"return_to\": \"/\",\n  \"client_type\": \"web\",\n  \"created_at\": 1771721000\n}\n```\n\n### Security & Invalidation Rules:\n1. **Time-to-Live (TTL)**: Fixed at **300 seconds (5 minutes)**. Expired state transactions are automatically purged by Redis.\n2. **Single-Use Atomic Consumption**: During the `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` handler, the backend uses Redis `GETDEL` (or transactional `MULTI / GET / DEL / EXEC`). If the key does not exist or was already consumed, callback processing halts immediately with `400 Bad Request (INVALID_OR_EXPIRED_OAUTH_STATE)`.\n3. **No Sensitive Logging**: The logging middleware is audited to ensure `state`, `code`, `nonce`, and `code_verifier` are strictly redacted from stdout/stderr.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Revised Asymmetric RS256 JWT Access Token Model\n\nAccess tokens use **RS256** (RSA 2048/4096-bit asymmetric cryptography) with strict claim verification.\n\n### Key Management:\n- **Private Signing Key (`JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM`)**: Held strictly in backend memory / environment secret; never exposed to clients or checked into git.\n- **Public Verification Key (`JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM`)**: Used by the API middleware to verify signatures.\n\n### Header & Claims Payload:\n```json\n// Header\n{\n  \"alg\": \"RS256\",\n  \"typ\": \"JWT\",\n  \"kid\": \"pg-auth-2026-v1\"\n}\n\n// Payload\n{\n  \"iss\": \"https://api.privacygateway.net\",\n  \"aud\": \"https://privacygateway.net\",\n  \"sub\": \"b48c8d82-938e-4a6c-9c71-9f9393e8241a\", // User UUID\n  \"email\": \"user@example.com\",\n  \"role\": \"user\",                                 // \'user\' | \'admin\' | \'auditor\'\n  \"session_id\": \"7a3f81e0-631d-4d2b-8a8b-1e0f49c0d12e\",\n  \"family_id\": \"5f1a9b2c-8d3e-4f5a-9b1c-7e3d5a8f2b4c\",\n  \"iat\": 1771721000,\n  \"nbf\": 1771721000,\n  \"exp\": 1771721900,                              // Exactly 15 minutes (900s)\n  \"jti\": \"d0e1f2a3-b4c5-6d7e-8f9a-0b1c2d3e4f5a\"  // Unique Token ID\n}\n```\n\n### Verification Constraints:\n- Rejects any token whose header specifies `alg !== \"RS256\"`.\n- Validates that `iss === \"https://api.privacygateway.net\"`.\n- Validates that `aud === \"https://privacygateway.net\"`.\n- Validates that `nbf <= current_time` and `exp > current_time`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Refresh Token Family & Transactional Rotation Engine\n\nTo provide seamless sessions while defending against session hijacking, tokens are grouped into **Token Families** with transactional rotation and replay detection.\n\n```\n                              TOKEN FAMILY LIFECYCLE & REPLAY PROTECTION\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| SCENARIO 1: NORMAL ROTATION                                                                        |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| Initial Login -> Session S1 Created: Family F1, Token T1 (Hash H1 in DB).                          |\n| After 15 mins -> Client presents T1 to /auth/refresh.                                              |\n| Backend:                                                                                           |\n|   1. BEGIN TRANSACTION                                                                             |\n|   2. SELECT * FROM user_sessions WHERE refresh_token_hash = SHA256(T1) FOR UPDATE                  |\n|   3. Verify revoked_at IS NULL AND expires_at > NOW()                                              |\n|   4. Generate T2. Compute H2 = SHA256(T2).                                                         |\n|   5. UPDATE user_sessions SET refresh_token_hash = H2, updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = S1            |\n|   6. COMMIT                                                                                        |\n|   7. Return new Access Token + T2.                                                                 |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| SCENARIO 2: TOKEN REUSE / REPLAY ATTACK (Session Theft Detected)                                  |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| Legitimate client rotated T1 -> T2. DB now has H2.                                                 |\n| Attacker uses stolen T1 -> Calls /auth/refresh with T1.                                            |\n| Backend:                                                                                           |\n|   1. Query finds NO active session matching SHA256(T1).                                            |\n|   2. Check `session_token_history` (or revoked sessions). Finds T1 was already rotated in Family F1.|\n|   3. ALERT: Replay detected! Attacker or victim has been compromised.                              |\n|   4. UPDATE user_sessions SET revoked_at = NOW() WHERE family_id = F1                              |\n|      (Instantly invalidates ALL sessions in Family F1).                                            |\n|   5. INSERT INTO `audit_logs` { action: \'TOKEN_REPLAY_DETECTED\', family_id: F1, user_id: ... }     |\n|   6. Return 401 Unauthorized.                                                                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| SCENARIO 3: CONCURRENT REFRESH RACE GRACE PERIOD                                                   |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| If a client fires 2 simultaneous refresh requests due to parallel network tabs:                    |\n| A short 10-second grace window is allowed if the replacement token was minted <10s ago, returning   |\n| the existing new token rather than triggering a false-positive replay revocation.                   |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Google Identity Linking & Anti-Account-Takeover Model\n\nTo prevent unauthorized identity hijacking, account linking follows strict cryptographic rules based on Google\'s immutable **`sub`** claim:\n\n```\n                                  ACCOUNT LINKING DECISION TREE\n\n                             Google OIDC Verification Returns (sub, email)\n                                                   |\n                                                   v\n                     SELECT * FROM authentication_accounts \n                     WHERE provider = \'google\' AND provider_subject_id = sub;\n                                                   |\n                        +--------------------------+--------------------------+\n                        |                                                     |\n                 Record Found                                          Record NOT Found\n                        |                                                     |\n                        v                                                     v\n          Authenticate User Directly                     SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = email;\n          (Issue Application Session)                                         |\n                                                        +---------------------+---------------------+\n                                                        |                                           |\n                                                  User Exists                                 User Does NOT Exist\n                                                        |                                           |\n                                                        v                                           v\n                                          REJECT AUTOMATIC LOGIN                      CREATE NEW USER & ACCOUNT\n                                          (409 Conflict / Link Required)             (Insert `users` & `auth_accounts`)\n                                                        |                                           |\n                                          \"An account with this email exists.                       v\n                                           Log in with your original method to        Authenticate User Directly\n                                           link your Google identity securely.\"       (Issue Application Session)\n```\n\n- **Explicit Linking Flow**: If an authenticated user wants to link their Google account to an existing profile, they must call `POST /api/v1/auth/link/google/start` while possessing an active JWT session.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Web Session & Cookie Security Architecture\n\nFor web browser sessions, tokens are transmitted via hardened HTTP cookies with CSRF defense:\n\n| Cookie Name | Scope | Attributes | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `pg_access_token` | Path: `/` | `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=900` | 15-minute stateless RS256 authorization bearer. |\n| `pg_refresh_token`| Path: `/api/v1/auth` | `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict; Max-Age=2592000` | 30-day rotated refresh token (restricted to auth path). |\n| `pg_csrf_token`   | Path: `/` | `Secure; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=2592000` *(Readable by JS)* | Double-Submit CSRF protection cookie. |\n\n### CSRF Protection Standard:\n- All state-changing endpoints (`POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, `PATCH`) must include the header `X-CSRF-Token` matching the value in `pg_csrf_token` (Double-Submit Cookie Pattern).\n- SameSite cookies + custom header requirements mitigate cross-site request forgery without requiring server-side session state.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Native Desktop OAuth Architecture (RFC 8252 Preparation)\n\nWhile the Tauri client is deferred to Phase 5, Phase 2 implements the exact backend endpoints required by the standard:\n\n### Google Cloud Console Configuration:\n- **Web Application Client ID**: `WEB_CLIENT_ID` with redirect URI `https://app.privacygateway.net/api/v1/auth/google/callback` (or `http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback` for dev).\n- **Desktop Application Client ID**: `DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID` configured as an **iOS/Android/Desktop (Native App)** type in Google Cloud Console. Per RFC 8252, Google permits dynamic loopback ports (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>`) *only* for Native App client types.\n\n### Dedicated Desktop Exchange Endpoint:\n```\nPOST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"code\": \"4/0AdLIrY...\",\n  \"code_verifier\": \"dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk\",\n  \"redirect_uri\": \"http://127.0.0.1:48291/callback\",\n  \"state\": \"f9a0c2e4...\"\n}\n```\n- The backend exchanges the code against Google using the `DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID` credentials.\n- Responds with JSON payload `{ access_token, refresh_token, user }` directly in the response body (no browser cookies).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Client IP Privacy & Minimization Decision\n\nIn alignment with Phase 1\'s Data Minimization mandate:\n- **Decision**: The backend **will NOT record, store, or log raw IP addresses** in `user_sessions` or audit tables.\n- **Minimization Strategy**: `user_sessions` records only:\n  - `user_agent` (Truncated family name: e.g. \"Chrome / Linux\")\n  - `created_at` & `last_used_at` timestamps\n  - `revoked_at` timestamp\n  - `family_id` (UUID)\n- Zero IP addresses are written to disk for user sessions.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Phase 2 Database Changes (PostgreSQL Schema)\n\nTo support the Token Family concept and historical replay detection, migration `002_auth_token_families.sql` adds:\n\n```sql\n-- Migration: 002_auth_token_families.sql\n\n-- 1. Update user_sessions with family_id and token metadata\nALTER TABLE user_sessions \n    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS family_id UUID DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4() NOT NULL,\n    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS last_used_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL;\n\n-- Remove IP storage column to uphold IP privacy minimization\nALTER TABLE user_sessions \n    DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS client_ip_anonymized;\n\n-- 2. Create index for fast token hash lookups\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_user_sessions_family ON user_sessions(family_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_user_sessions_active_hash ON user_sessions(refresh_token_hash) WHERE revoked_at IS NULL;\n\n-- 3. Token rotation history table (for replay detection)\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS session_token_history (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    session_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES user_sessions(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    family_id UUID NOT NULL,\n    token_hash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,\n    rotated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_token_history_lookup ON session_token_history(token_hash);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Phase 2 API Endpoints Specification\n\n| Method | Endpoint | Description | Auth & Security Controls |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/start` | Generates PKCE + Redis state; returns 302 to Google | Rate limited; Redis TTL 300s |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` | Atomic state consumption; validates Google ID Token; issues session | Atomic `GETDEL`; RS256 JWKS verification |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange` | Native desktop PKCE code exchange | Desktop client assertion; returns JSON tokens |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/refresh` | Rotates refresh token within family; issues new 15m JWT | Token Family rotation; Replay attack detection |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/logout` | Revokes current session family; clears cookies | CSRF check; Database session revocation |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/me` | Returns authenticated user profile, roles, and session info | RS256 JWT Bearer verification |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions` | Lists user\'s active sessions | RS256 JWT Bearer; scoped to current user |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions/:id` | Revokes a specific session by ID | RS256 JWT Bearer; ownership assertion |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/admin/users` | Lists users (Admin-only RBAC assertion endpoint) | Strict `requireRole([\'admin\'])` middleware |\n\n---\n\n## 11. Expanded Threat Model (Authentication Phase)\n\n| Threat Vector | Attack Scenario | Architectural Defense & Mitigation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **OAuth State Forgery / CSRF** | Attacker tricks victim into visiting callback URL with attacker\'s auth code. | State is generated via CSPRNG, stored in Redis with 5m TTL, and consumed via atomic `GETDEL`. Mismatched or replayed states are rejected immediately. |\n| **Google Token Forgery** | Attacker crafts a fake JWT claiming admin email. | Backend downloads Google\'s public certificates directly from `https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs`, verifies RS256 signature, and enforces `iss` and `aud` constraints. |\n| **Account Takeover via Email Collision** | Attacker registers unverified email on Google matching an existing Privacy Gateway user. | 1. Backend asserts `email_verified === true`.<br>2. Account linking is keyed on immutable Google `sub`, not email. Automatic linking on email match alone is rejected. |\n| **Refresh Token Theft & Replay** | Attacker steals a refresh token and attempts to use it after user has already refreshed. | Token Family Model: Replaying an already-rotated token invalidates all sessions in that family instantly and logs an audit security event. |\n| **JWT Algorithm Confusion** | Attacker modifies JWT header to `\"alg\": \"none\"` or `\"alg\": \"HS256\"`. | Backend strictly enforces `algorithms: [\'RS256\']` with public key verification; all other algorithms are rejected. |\n| **Privilege Escalation** | Regular user attempts to invoke `/api/v1/admin/*` endpoints. | `requireRole([\'admin\'])` middleware inspects cryptographically signed `role` claim in RS256 JWT (HTTP 403 Forbidden). |\n\n---\n\n## 12. Automated Test Plan\n\nAutomated tests in `apps/api/tests/auth.test.ts` and `apps/api/tests/oidc.test.ts`:\n\n1. **State & Redis Transaction Tests**:\n   - `startGoogleAuth` writes valid state and PKCE verifier to Redis with 300s TTL.\n   - Callback with missing `state` parameter returns 400 Bad Request.\n   - Callback with expired/consumed `state` returns 400 Bad Request.\n2. **OIDC Token Validation Tests**:\n   - Rejection of invalid RS256 signature against mock JWKS.\n   - Rejection of token with invalid issuer (`iss !== \'https://accounts.google.com\'`).\n   - Rejection of token with mismatched audience (`aud !== GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`).\n   - Rejection of token with `email_verified === false`.\n   - Rejection of token with mismatched `nonce`.\n3. **Identity & Account Linking Protection Tests**:\n   - First-time Google user creation creates records in `users` and `authentication_accounts`.\n   - Existing Google user login retrieves existing user without creating duplicates.\n   - Login attempt with matching email but different `sub` triggers conflict protection (no automatic takeover).\n4. **Token Family & Rotation Tests**:\n   - Refreshing a token generates a new refresh token and invalidates the old one.\n   - Concurrent refresh within grace window succeeds.\n   - Replay of an invalidated refresh token triggers family-wide session revocation.\n5. **RBAC & Authorization Tests**:\n   - Unauthenticated request to `/api/v1/auth/me` returns 401.\n   - User with `role: \'user\'` accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` returns 403 Forbidden.\n   - User with `role: \'admin\'` accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` returns 200 OK.\n6. **CSRF & Cookie Protection Tests**:\n   - Mutation requests without `X-CSRF-Token` return 403 Forbidden.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Environment Variables Required\n\nAdded to `.env.example`:\n\n```env\n# Google OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect (Development Credentials)\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_dev_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_dev_client_secret\nGOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback\n\n# Asymmetric RS256 JWT Keypair (PEM Format or Path)\n# Generated via: openssl genrsa -out jwt_private.pem 2048 && openssl rsa -in jwt_private.pem -pubout -out jwt_public.pem\nJWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=\"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\"\nJWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM=\"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\\n...\\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----\"\nJWT_KEY_ID=\"pg-auth-2026-v1\"\n\n# Cookie Encryption / Signing\nCOOKIE_SECRET=development_cookie_secret_minimum_32_characters_long\n```\n\n---\n\n## 14. Exact Files to Create & Modify\n\n```\napps/api/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 env.ts                     # Add RS256 keys, Google credentials, and cookie secret validation\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 keys.ts                    # Dynamic RS256 keypair loader (with dev fallback generator)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oauth.ts                   # Google discovery document fetcher & JWKS cache manager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.controller.ts         # startGoogleAuth, handleGoogleCallback, desktopExchange, refreshSession, logout, getMe, listSessions, revokeSession\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.controller.ts        # listAllUsers (RBAC test controller)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.middleware.ts         # requireAuth (RS256 JWT verification), requireRole, optionalAuth\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 csrf.middleware.ts         # Double-Submit CSRF validator for cookie mutations\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.routes.ts             # REST router for /api/v1/auth/*\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.routes.ts            # REST router for /api/v1/admin/*\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.service.ts            # Google token exchange, sub-based identity linking, session family manager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 token.service.ts           # RS256 JWT minting/verifying & CSPRNG refresh token hashing\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oidc.service.ts            # JWKS signature verifier & claim assertions\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 app.ts                         # Register cookie-parser, auth routes, and admin routes\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n    \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.test.ts                   # Integration tests: OAuth state, callback, refresh, replay, logout, RBAC\n    \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oidc.test.ts                   # Unit tests: JWKS verification, issuer/aud/exp/nonce claim assertions\n\ninfrastructure/migrations/\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 002_auth_token_families.sql        # Migration for token family tracking and IP removal\n\npackages/shared-types/src/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api.ts                             # Auth DTOs (UserProfileDto, AuthResponseDto, SessionItemDto)\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.ts\n\nsrc/App.tsx\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Update React Dashboard with:\n    - Real \"Sign in with Google\" button & authentication status bar\n    - Authenticated profile banner (Email, Role, User ID, Session count)\n    - Interactive Session Manager (List active sessions, revoke specific session)\n    - Role-Based Access Control tester (Execute admin probe and display 200 vs 403 outcome)\n    - Interactive Logout action\n```\n\n---\n\n### Request for Phase 2.1 Approval\n\nThis revised **Phase 2.1 Specification** resolves all requirements:\n1. Redis-backed short-lived OAuth state & PKCE transaction storage (atomic `GETDEL`).\n2. Pure **RS256** asymmetric JWT access tokens with full claim validation.\n3. Transactional **Token Family** refresh rotation with replay attack mitigation.\n4. Google **`sub`** claim matching with anti-account-takeover protection.\n5. Complete removal of IP address persistence to uphold data minimization.\n\nPlease review and approve so implementation may proceed."
    modified_response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 2.1: REVISED AUTHENTICATION & OIDC SECURITY SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Revised OAuth & OIDC Flow Diagram (Redis State Model)\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                     PHASE 2.1: REDIS-BACKED OAUTH & PKCE FLOW\n========================================================================================================================\n\nBrowser / Client                  Privacy Gateway Backend                     Redis In-Memory Bus              Google OIDC (accounts.google.com)\n       |                                     |                                         |                                       |\n       | 1. GET /api/v1/auth/google/start    |                                         |                                       |\n       |------------------------------------>|                                         |                                       |\n       |                                     | 2. Generate CSPRNG:                     |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - state (32-byte hex)                |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - nonce (32-byte hex)                |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - code_verifier (32-byte hex)        |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - code_challenge = S256(verifier)    |                                       |\n       |                                     |                                         |                                       |\n       |                                     | 3. SET `pg:oauth:state:<state>`         |                                       |\n       |                                     |    { nonce, verifier, return_to }       |                                       |\n       |                                     |    EXPIRE = 300s (5 minutes)            |                                       |\n       |                                     |---------------------------------------->|                                       |\n       | 4. 302 Redirect to Google Auth URL  |                                         |                                       |\n       |    (?client_id=...&state=<state>    |                                         |                                       |\n       |     &code_challenge=...&nonce=...)  |                                         |                                       |\n       |<------------------------------------|                                         |                                       |\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 5. User signs in on accounts.google.com with consent                                                                  |\n       |---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 6. 302 Redirect with ?code=AUTH_CODE&state=<state>                                                                    |\n       |<----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 7. GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback?code=AUTH_CODE&state=<state>                                                      |\n       |------------------------------------>|                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 8. ATOMIC GETDEL `pg:oauth:state:<state>`                                       |\n       |                                     |    (Single-use consumption; prevents replay)                                    |\n       |                                     |---------------------------------------->|                                       |\n       |                                     |<----------------------------------------|                                       |\n       |                                     |    If NOT FOUND or EXPIRED -> 400 Bad Request                                   |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 9. POST https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token                                     |\n       |                                     |    { code, client_id, client_secret, code_verifier, redirect_uri }              |\n       |                                     |-------------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                     |<--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                     |    Returns { id_token, access_token }                                           |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 10. Cryptographic OIDC ID Token Verification:                                   |\n       |                                     |     a. Fetch / Cache Google JWKS via Discovery (Rotating Cache)                 |\n       |                                     |     b. Verify RS256 signature with matching `kid`                               |\n       |                                     |     c. Assert `iss` in [\'https://accounts.google.com\', \'accounts.google.com\']   |\n       |                                     |     d. Assert `aud` === GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID                                        |\n       |                                     |     e. Assert `exp` > now and `iat` <= now                                      |\n       |                                     |     f. Assert `nonce` === stored_nonce                                          |\n       |                                     |     g. Assert `email_verified` === true                                         |\n       |                                     |     h. Extract stable Google `sub` and `email`                                  |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 11. Transactional SQL Identity & Token Family Creation:                         |\n       |                                     |     a. Query `authentication_accounts WHERE provider=\'google\' AND provider_sub` |\n       |                                     |     b. If exists -> Authenticate existing user                                  |\n       |                                     |     c. If NOT exists -> Check if `users` table already has that email:          |\n       |                                     |        * If yes -> REJECT (Prevent unauthenticated account takeover)            |\n       |                                     |        * If no -> INSERT INTO `users` & INSERT INTO `authentication_accounts`    |\n       |                                     |     d. INSERT INTO `user_sessions` with unique `family_id` & `token_hash`       |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 12. Issue RS256 JWT Access Token (15m) + CSPRNG Refresh Token (30d)             |\n       | 13. Set-Cookie: `pg_access_token`,  |                                                                                 |\n       |     Set-Cookie: `pg_refresh_token`  |                                                                                 |\n       |     302 Redirect to Dashboard (/)   |                                                                                 |\n       |<------------------------------------|                                                                                 |\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Redis OAuth Transaction State Model\n\nAll ephemeral authorization parameters are held in Redis, ensuring zero state exposure in client cookies.\n\n### Key Schema:\n```redis\npg:oauth:state:<state_hex_string>\n```\n\n### Value Payload (JSON):\n```json\n{\n  \"nonce\": \"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\",\n  \"code_verifier\": \"f8a1c3d5e7b9a2c4e6f8a0b2d4e6f8a0b2c4d6e8f0a2b4c6d8e0f2a4b6c8d0e2\",\n  \"return_to\": \"/\",\n  \"client_type\": \"web\",\n  \"created_at\": 1771721000\n}\n```\n\n### Security & Invalidation Rules:\n1. **Time-to-Live (TTL)**: Fixed at **300 seconds (5 minutes)**. Expired state transactions are automatically purged by Redis.\n2. **Single-Use Atomic Consumption**: During the `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` handler, the backend uses Redis `GETDEL` (or transactional `MULTI / GET / DEL / EXEC`). If the key does not exist or was already consumed, callback processing halts immediately with `400 Bad Request (INVALID_OR_EXPIRED_OAUTH_STATE)`.\n3. **No Sensitive Logging**: The logging middleware is audited to ensure `state`, `code`, `nonce`, and `code_verifier` are strictly redacted from stdout/stderr.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Revised Asymmetric RS256 JWT Access Token Model\n\nAccess tokens use **RS256** (RSA 2048/4096-bit asymmetric cryptography) with strict claim verification.\n\n### Key Management:\n- **Private Signing Key (`JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM`)**: Held strictly in backend memory / environment secret; never exposed to clients or checked into git.\n- **Public Verification Key (`JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM`)**: Used by the API middleware to verify signatures.\n\n### Header & Claims Payload:\n```json\n// Header\n{\n  \"alg\": \"RS256\",\n  \"typ\": \"JWT\",\n  \"kid\": \"pg-auth-2026-v1\"\n}\n\n// Payload\n{\n  \"iss\": \"https://api.privacygateway.net\",\n  \"aud\": \"https://privacygateway.net\",\n  \"sub\": \"b48c8d82-938e-4a6c-9c71-9f9393e8241a\", // User UUID\n  \"email\": \"user@example.com\",\n  \"role\": \"user\",                                 // \'user\' | \'admin\' | \'auditor\'\n  \"session_id\": \"7a3f81e0-631d-4d2b-8a8b-1e0f49c0d12e\",\n  \"family_id\": \"5f1a9b2c-8d3e-4f5a-9b1c-7e3d5a8f2b4c\",\n  \"iat\": 1771721000,\n  \"nbf\": 1771721000,\n  \"exp\": 1771721900,                              // Exactly 15 minutes (900s)\n  \"jti\": \"d0e1f2a3-b4c5-6d7e-8f9a-0b1c2d3e4f5a\"  // Unique Token ID\n}\n```\n\n### Verification Constraints:\n- Rejects any token whose header specifies `alg !== \"RS256\"`.\n- Validates that `iss === \"https://api.privacygateway.net\"`.\n- Validates that `aud === \"https://privacygateway.net\"`.\n- Validates that `nbf <= current_time` and `exp > current_time`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Refresh Token Family & Transactional Rotation Engine\n\nTo provide seamless sessions while defending against session hijacking, tokens are grouped into **Token Families** with transactional rotation and replay detection.\n\n```\n                              TOKEN FAMILY LIFECYCLE & REPLAY PROTECTION\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| SCENARIO 1: NORMAL ROTATION                                                                        |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| Initial Login -> Session S1 Created: Family F1, Token T1 (Hash H1 in DB).                          |\n| After 15 mins -> Client presents T1 to /auth/refresh.                                              |\n| Backend:                                                                                           |\n|   1. BEGIN TRANSACTION                                                                             |\n|   2. SELECT * FROM user_sessions WHERE refresh_token_hash = SHA256(T1) FOR UPDATE                  |\n|   3. Verify revoked_at IS NULL AND expires_at > NOW()                                              |\n|   4. Generate T2. Compute H2 = SHA256(T2).                                                         |\n|   5. UPDATE user_sessions SET refresh_token_hash = H2, updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = S1            |\n|   6. COMMIT                                                                                        |\n|   7. Return new Access Token + T2.                                                                 |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| SCENARIO 2: TOKEN REUSE / REPLAY ATTACK (Session Theft Detected)                                  |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| Legitimate client rotated T1 -> T2. DB now has H2.                                                 |\n| Attacker uses stolen T1 -> Calls /auth/refresh with T1.                                            |\n| Backend:                                                                                           |\n|   1. Query finds NO active session matching SHA256(T1).                                            |\n|   2. Check `session_token_history` (or revoked sessions). Finds T1 was already rotated in Family F1.|\n|   3. ALERT: Replay detected! Attacker or victim has been compromised.                              |\n|   4. UPDATE user_sessions SET revoked_at = NOW() WHERE family_id = F1                              |\n|      (Instantly invalidates ALL sessions in Family F1).                                            |\n|   5. INSERT INTO `audit_logs` { action: \'TOKEN_REPLAY_DETECTED\', family_id: F1, user_id: ... }     |\n|   6. Return 401 Unauthorized.                                                                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| SCENARIO 3: CONCURRENT REFRESH RACE GRACE PERIOD                                                   |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| If a client fires 2 simultaneous refresh requests due to parallel network tabs:                    |\n| A short 10-second grace window is allowed if the replacement token was minted <10s ago, returning   |\n| the existing new token rather than triggering a false-positive replay revocation.                   |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Google Identity Linking & Anti-Account-Takeover Model\n\nTo prevent unauthorized identity hijacking, account linking follows strict cryptographic rules based on Google\'s immutable **`sub`** claim:\n\n```\n                                  ACCOUNT LINKING DECISION TREE\n\n                             Google OIDC Verification Returns (sub, email)\n                                                   |\n                                                   v\n                     SELECT * FROM authentication_accounts \n                     WHERE provider = \'google\' AND provider_subject_id = sub;\n                                                   |\n                        +--------------------------+--------------------------+\n                        |                                                     |\n                 Record Found                                          Record NOT Found\n                        |                                                     |\n                        v                                                     v\n          Authenticate User Directly                     SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = email;\n          (Issue Application Session)                                         |\n                                                        +---------------------+---------------------+\n                                                        |                                           |\n                                                  User Exists                                 User Does NOT Exist\n                                                        |                                           |\n                                                        v                                           v\n                                          REJECT AUTOMATIC LOGIN                      CREATE NEW USER & ACCOUNT\n                                          (409 Conflict / Link Required)             (Insert `users` & `auth_accounts`)\n                                                        |                                           |\n                                          \"An account with this email exists.                       v\n                                           Log in with your original method to        Authenticate User Directly\n                                           link your Google identity securely.\"       (Issue Application Session)\n```\n\n- **Explicit Linking Flow**: If an authenticated user wants to link their Google account to an existing profile, they must call `POST /api/v1/auth/link/google/start` while possessing an active JWT session.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Web Session & Cookie Security Architecture\n\nFor web browser sessions, tokens are transmitted via hardened HTTP cookies with CSRF defense:\n\n| Cookie Name | Scope | Attributes | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `pg_access_token` | Path: `/` | `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=900` | 15-minute stateless RS256 authorization bearer. |\n| `pg_refresh_token`| Path: `/api/v1/auth` | `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict; Max-Age=2592000` | 30-day rotated refresh token (restricted to auth path). |\n| `pg_csrf_token`   | Path: `/` | `Secure; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=2592000` *(Readable by JS)* | Double-Submit CSRF protection cookie. |\n\n### CSRF Protection Standard:\n- All state-changing endpoints (`POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, `PATCH`) must include the header `X-CSRF-Token` matching the value in `pg_csrf_token` (Double-Submit Cookie Pattern).\n- SameSite cookies + custom header requirements mitigate cross-site request forgery without requiring server-side session state.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Native Desktop OAuth Architecture (RFC 8252 Preparation)\n\nWhile the Tauri client is deferred to Phase 5, Phase 2 implements the exact backend endpoints required by the standard:\n\n### Google Cloud Console Configuration:\n- **Web Application Client ID**: `WEB_CLIENT_ID` with redirect URI `https://app.privacygateway.net/api/v1/auth/google/callback` (or `http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback` for dev).\n- **Desktop Application Client ID**: `DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID` configured as an **iOS/Android/Desktop (Native App)** type in Google Cloud Console. Per RFC 8252, Google permits dynamic loopback ports (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>`) *only* for Native App client types.\n\n### Dedicated Desktop Exchange Endpoint:\n```\nPOST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"code\": \"4/0AdLIrY...\",\n  \"code_verifier\": \"dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk\",\n  \"redirect_uri\": \"http://127.0.0.1:48291/callback\",\n  \"state\": \"f9a0c2e4...\"\n}\n```\n- The backend exchanges the code against Google using the `DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID` credentials.\n- Responds with JSON payload `{ access_token, refresh_token, user }` directly in the response body (no browser cookies).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Client IP Privacy & Minimization Decision\n\nIn alignment with Phase 1\'s Data Minimization mandate:\n- **Decision**: The backend **will NOT record, store, or log raw IP addresses** in `user_sessions` or audit tables.\n- **Minimization Strategy**: `user_sessions` records only:\n  - `user_agent` (Truncated family name: e.g. \"Chrome / Linux\")\n  - `created_at` & `last_used_at` timestamps\n  - `revoked_at` timestamp\n  - `family_id` (UUID)\n- Zero IP addresses are written to disk for user sessions.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Phase 2 Database Changes (PostgreSQL Schema)\n\nTo support the Token Family concept and historical replay detection, migration `002_auth_token_families.sql` adds:\n\n```sql\n-- Migration: 002_auth_token_families.sql\n\n-- 1. Update user_sessions with family_id and token metadata\nALTER TABLE user_sessions \n    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS family_id UUID DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4() NOT NULL,\n    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS last_used_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL;\n\n-- Remove IP storage column to uphold IP privacy minimization\nALTER TABLE user_sessions \n    DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS client_ip_anonymized;\n\n-- 2. Create index for fast token hash lookups\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_user_sessions_family ON user_sessions(family_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_user_sessions_active_hash ON user_sessions(refresh_token_hash) WHERE revoked_at IS NULL;\n\n-- 3. Token rotation history table (for replay detection)\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS session_token_history (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    session_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES user_sessions(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    family_id UUID NOT NULL,\n    token_hash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,\n    rotated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_token_history_lookup ON session_token_history(token_hash);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Phase 2 API Endpoints Specification\n\n| Method | Endpoint | Description | Auth & Security Controls |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/start` | Generates PKCE + Redis state; returns 302 to Google | Rate limited; Redis TTL 300s |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` | Atomic state consumption; validates Google ID Token; issues session | Atomic `GETDEL`; RS256 JWKS verification |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange` | Native desktop PKCE code exchange | Desktop client assertion; returns JSON tokens |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/refresh` | Rotates refresh token within family; issues new 15m JWT | Token Family rotation; Replay attack detection |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/logout` | Revokes current session family; clears cookies | CSRF check; Database session revocation |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/me` | Returns authenticated user profile, roles, and session info | RS256 JWT Bearer verification |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions` | Lists user\'s active sessions | RS256 JWT Bearer; scoped to current user |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions/:id` | Revokes a specific session by ID | RS256 JWT Bearer; ownership assertion |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/admin/users` | Lists users (Admin-only RBAC assertion endpoint) | Strict `requireRole([\'admin\'])` middleware |\n\n---\n\n## 11. Expanded Threat Model (Authentication Phase)\n\n| Threat Vector | Attack Scenario | Architectural Defense & Mitigation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **OAuth State Forgery / CSRF** | Attacker tricks victim into visiting callback URL with attacker\'s auth code. | State is generated via CSPRNG, stored in Redis with 5m TTL, and consumed via atomic `GETDEL`. Mismatched or replayed states are rejected immediately. |\n| **Google Token Forgery** | Attacker crafts a fake JWT claiming admin email. | Backend downloads Google\'s public certificates directly from `https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs`, verifies RS256 signature, and enforces `iss` and `aud` constraints. |\n| **Account Takeover via Email Collision** | Attacker registers unverified email on Google matching an existing Privacy Gateway user. | 1. Backend asserts `email_verified === true`.<br>2. Account linking is keyed on immutable Google `sub`, not email. Automatic linking on email match alone is rejected. |\n| **Refresh Token Theft & Replay** | Attacker steals a refresh token and attempts to use it after user has already refreshed. | Token Family Model: Replaying an already-rotated token invalidates all sessions in that family instantly and logs an audit security event. |\n| **JWT Algorithm Confusion** | Attacker modifies JWT header to `\"alg\": \"none\"` or `\"alg\": \"HS256\"`. | Backend strictly enforces `algorithms: [\'RS256\']` with public key verification; all other algorithms are rejected. |\n| **Privilege Escalation** | Regular user attempts to invoke `/api/v1/admin/*` endpoints. | `requireRole([\'admin\'])` middleware inspects cryptographically signed `role` claim in RS256 JWT (HTTP 403 Forbidden). |\n\n---\n\n## 12. Automated Test Plan\n\nAutomated tests in `apps/api/tests/auth.test.ts` and `apps/api/tests/oidc.test.ts`:\n\n1. **State & Redis Transaction Tests**:\n   - `startGoogleAuth` writes valid state and PKCE verifier to Redis with 300s TTL.\n   - Callback with missing `state` parameter returns 400 Bad Request.\n   - Callback with expired/consumed `state` returns 400 Bad Request.\n2. **OIDC Token Validation Tests**:\n   - Rejection of invalid RS256 signature against mock JWKS.\n   - Rejection of token with invalid issuer (`iss !== \'https://accounts.google.com\'`).\n   - Rejection of token with mismatched audience (`aud !== GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`).\n   - Rejection of token with `email_verified === false`.\n   - Rejection of token with mismatched `nonce`.\n3. **Identity & Account Linking Protection Tests**:\n   - First-time Google user creation creates records in `users` and `authentication_accounts`.\n   - Existing Google user login retrieves existing user without creating duplicates.\n   - Login attempt with matching email but different `sub` triggers conflict protection (no automatic takeover).\n4. **Token Family & Rotation Tests**:\n   - Refreshing a token generates a new refresh token and invalidates the old one.\n   - Concurrent refresh within grace window succeeds.\n   - Replay of an invalidated refresh token triggers family-wide session revocation.\n5. **RBAC & Authorization Tests**:\n   - Unauthenticated request to `/api/v1/auth/me` returns 401.\n   - User with `role: \'user\'` accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` returns 403 Forbidden.\n   - User with `role: \'admin\'` accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` returns 200 OK.\n6. **CSRF & Cookie Protection Tests**:\n   - Mutation requests without `X-CSRF-Token` return 403 Forbidden.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Environment Variables Required\n\nAdded to `.env.example`:\n\n```env\n# Google OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect (Development Credentials)\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_dev_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_dev_client_secret\nGOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback\n\n# Asymmetric RS256 JWT Keypair (PEM Format or Path)\n# Generated via: openssl genrsa -out jwt_private.pem 2048 && openssl rsa -in jwt_private.pem -pubout -out jwt_public.pem\nJWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=\"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\"\nJWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM=\"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\\n...\\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----\"\nJWT_KEY_ID=\"pg-auth-2026-v1\"\n\n# Cookie Encryption / Signing\nCOOKIE_SECRET=development_cookie_secret_minimum_32_characters_long\n```\n\n---\n\n## 14. Exact Files to Create & Modify\n\n```\napps/api/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 env.ts                     # Add RS256 keys, Google credentials, and cookie secret validation\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 keys.ts                    # Dynamic RS256 keypair loader (with dev fallback generator)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oauth.ts                   # Google discovery document fetcher & JWKS cache manager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.controller.ts         # startGoogleAuth, handleGoogleCallback, desktopExchange, refreshSession, logout, getMe, listSessions, revokeSession\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.controller.ts        # listAllUsers (RBAC test controller)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.middleware.ts         # requireAuth (RS256 JWT verification), requireRole, optionalAuth\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 csrf.middleware.ts         # Double-Submit CSRF validator for cookie mutations\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.routes.ts             # REST router for /api/v1/auth/*\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.routes.ts            # REST router for /api/v1/admin/*\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.service.ts            # Google token exchange, sub-based identity linking, session family manager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 token.service.ts           # RS256 JWT minting/verifying & CSPRNG refresh token hashing\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oidc.service.ts            # JWKS signature verifier & claim assertions\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 app.ts                         # Register cookie-parser, auth routes, and admin routes\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n    \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.test.ts                   # Integration tests: OAuth state, callback, refresh, replay, logout, RBAC\n    \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oidc.test.ts                   # Unit tests: JWKS verification, issuer/aud/exp/nonce claim assertions\n\ninfrastructure/migrations/\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 002_auth_token_families.sql        # Migration for token family tracking and IP removal\n\npackages/shared-types/src/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api.ts                             # Auth DTOs (UserProfileDto, AuthResponseDto, SessionItemDto)\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.ts\n\nsrc/App.tsx\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Update React Dashboard with:\n    - Real \"Sign in with Google\" button & authentication status bar\n    - Authenticated profile banner (Email, Role, User ID, Session count)\n    - Interactive Session Manager (List active sessions, revoke specific session)\n    - Role-Based Access Control tester (Execute admin probe and display 200 vs 403 outcome)\n    - Interactive Logout action\n```\n\n---\n\n### Request for Phase 2.1 Approval\n\nThis revised **Phase 2.1 Specification** resolves all requirements:\n1. Redis-backed short-lived OAuth state & PKCE transaction storage (atomic `GETDEL`).\n2. Pure **RS256** asymmetric JWT access tokens with full claim validation.\n3. Transactional **Token Family** refresh rotation with replay attack mitigation.\n4. Google **`sub`** claim matching with anti-account-takeover protection.\n5. Complete removal of IP address persistence to uphold data minimization.\n\nPlease review and approve so implementation may proceed."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
