  user_input {
    user_response: "Phase 2.1 is almost approved, but make these final corrections before implementation.\n\n1. REFRESH TOKEN CONCURRENCY\n\nThe current 10-second \"grace window\" says the server can return the existing replacement refresh token.\n\nThat is not valid if the replacement refresh token is only stored as a SHA-256 hash, because the backend cannot reconstruct the plaintext token.\n\nDo NOT store plaintext refresh tokens simply to implement the grace period.\n\nChoose one of these designs:\n\nA) Remove the grace window and implement strict atomic rotation, with the client expected to handle a concurrent-refresh 401 safely.\n\nOR\n\nB) Design a formally specified server-side idempotency mechanism that does not persist plaintext refresh tokens.\n\nDocument the selected approach and add concurrent-refresh tests.\n\nDo not claim a grace-window implementation that cannot actually return the replacement token.\n\n2. COOKIE_SECRET\n\nReview whether COOKIE_SECRET is actually required.\n\nOAuth transaction state is stored entirely in Redis and must not be stored in client cookies.\n\nIf COOKIE_SECRET is not required by the final cookie/CSRF implementation, REMOVE it from:\n\n- environment configuration\n- .env.example\n- source code\n- documentation\n\nDo not maintain unnecessary cryptographic secrets.\n\nThe CSRF double-submit cookie should use a cryptographically random value and the server should validate the submitted header against the cookie according to the documented design.\n\n3. DESKTOP AUTHENTICATION\n\nDo not implement a vague \"desktop client assertion.\"\n\nTauri remains deferred to Phase 5.\n\nPhase 2 should only prepare a clearly documented native OAuth interface.\n\nThe future desktop flow must use:\n\n- Google Native/Desktop OAuth client\n- Authorization Code Flow\n- PKCE S256\n- state validation\n- exact redirect URI validation\n- one-time authorization-code exchange\n- secure loopback redirect handling\n- no client secret embedded in the desktop application\n\nDo not invent a custom desktop authentication secret or assertion.\n\n4. JWT KEY MANAGEMENT\n\nKeep RS256-only.\n\nHowever, make development key generation explicit:\n\n- Production requires externally supplied persistent private/public keys.\n- Development may generate an ephemeral key only when explicitly configured.\n- Never silently generate a production key.\n- Never commit generated private keys.\n- JWT key ID must support future key rotation.\n\nDocument the behavior when the server restarts with an ephemeral development key.\n\n5. SESSION REVOCATION\n\nDocument the distinction between:\n\n- refresh-token/session revocation\n- access-token expiration\n\nA revoked session must immediately prevent refresh-token use.\n\nAn already-issued 15-minute access JWT may remain cryptographically valid until expiration unless an explicit access-token revocation mechanism is introduced.\n\nDo not claim instantaneous access-token revocation unless implemented.\n\n6. LOGGING\n\nEnsure the following are NEVER logged:\n\n- OAuth authorization codes\n- state\n- nonce\n- PKCE verifier\n- Google ID tokens\n- Google access tokens\n- refresh tokens\n- JWTs\n- private keys\n- CSRF tokens\n\n7. FINAL REVIEW\n\nUpdate the Phase 2.1 specification with these corrections.\n\nThen provide:\n\n- final OAuth flow\n- final refresh-token lifecycle\n- final cookie/CSRF model\n- final desktop preparation model\n- final environment variables\n- final database migration\n- final API endpoints\n- final threat model\n- final test plan\n\nDo NOT modify the code yet.\n\nWait for my final approval before implementation."
    items {
      text: "Phase 2.1 is almost approved, but make these final corrections before implementation.\n\n1. REFRESH TOKEN CONCURRENCY\n\nThe current 10-second \"grace window\" says the server can return the existing replacement refresh token.\n\nThat is not valid if the replacement refresh token is only stored as a SHA-256 hash, because the backend cannot reconstruct the plaintext token.\n\nDo NOT store plaintext refresh tokens simply to implement the grace period.\n\nChoose one of these designs:\n\nA) Remove the grace window and implement strict atomic rotation, with the client expected to handle a concurrent-refresh 401 safely.\n\nOR\n\nB) Design a formally specified server-side idempotency mechanism that does not persist plaintext refresh tokens.\n\nDocument the selected approach and add concurrent-refresh tests.\n\nDo not claim a grace-window implementation that cannot actually return the replacement token.\n\n2. COOKIE_SECRET\n\nReview whether COOKIE_SECRET is actually required.\n\nOAuth transaction state is stored entirely in Redis and must not be stored in client cookies.\n\nIf COOKIE_SECRET is not required by the final cookie/CSRF implementation, REMOVE it from:\n\n- environment configuration\n- .env.example\n- source code\n- documentation\n\nDo not maintain unnecessary cryptographic secrets.\n\nThe CSRF double-submit cookie should use a cryptographically random value and the server should validate the submitted header against the cookie according to the documented design.\n\n3. DESKTOP AUTHENTICATION\n\nDo not implement a vague \"desktop client assertion.\"\n\nTauri remains deferred to Phase 5.\n\nPhase 2 should only prepare a clearly documented native OAuth interface.\n\nThe future desktop flow must use:\n\n- Google Native/Desktop OAuth client\n- Authorization Code Flow\n- PKCE S256\n- state validation\n- exact redirect URI validation\n- one-time authorization-code exchange\n- secure loopback redirect handling\n- no client secret embedded in the desktop application\n\nDo not invent a custom desktop authentication secret or assertion.\n\n4. JWT KEY MANAGEMENT\n\nKeep RS256-only.\n\nHowever, make development key generation explicit:\n\n- Production requires externally supplied persistent private/public keys.\n- Development may generate an ephemeral key only when explicitly configured.\n- Never silently generate a production key.\n- Never commit generated private keys.\n- JWT key ID must support future key rotation.\n\nDocument the behavior when the server restarts with an ephemeral development key.\n\n5. SESSION REVOCATION\n\nDocument the distinction between:\n\n- refresh-token/session revocation\n- access-token expiration\n\nA revoked session must immediately prevent refresh-token use.\n\nAn already-issued 15-minute access JWT may remain cryptographically valid until expiration unless an explicit access-token revocation mechanism is introduced.\n\nDo not claim instantaneous access-token revocation unless implemented.\n\n6. LOGGING\n\nEnsure the following are NEVER logged:\n\n- OAuth authorization codes\n- state\n- nonce\n- PKCE verifier\n- Google ID tokens\n- Google access tokens\n- refresh tokens\n- JWTs\n- private keys\n- CSRF tokens\n\n7. FINAL REVIEW\n\nUpdate the Phase 2.1 specification with these corrections.\n\nThen provide:\n\n- final OAuth flow\n- final refresh-token lifecycle\n- final cookie/CSRF model\n- final desktop preparation model\n- final environment variables\n- final database migration\n- final API endpoints\n- final threat model\n- final test plan\n\nDo NOT modify the code yet.\n\nWait for my final approval before implementation."
