  planner_response {
    response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 2.2: FINAL AUTHENTICATION & OIDC SECURITY SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Final OAuth / OIDC Flow Diagram (Zero Cookie State, Redis-Backed)\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                 PHASE 2.2: AUTHORIZATION CODE FLOW WITH PKCE & REDIS\n========================================================================================================================\n\nBrowser / Client                  Privacy Gateway Backend                     Redis In-Memory Bus              Google OIDC (accounts.google.com)\n       |                                     |                                         |                                       |\n       | 1. GET /api/v1/auth/google/start    |                                         |                                       |\n       |------------------------------------>|                                         |                                       |\n       |                                     | 2. Generate CSPRNG values:              |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - state (32-byte hex)                |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - nonce (32-byte hex)                |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - code_verifier (32-byte hex)        |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - code_challenge = S256(verifier)    |                                       |\n       |                                     |                                         |                                       |\n       |                                     | 3. SET `pg:oauth:state:<state>`         |                                       |\n       |                                     |    { nonce, verifier, return_to }       |                                       |\n       |                                     |    EXPIRE = 300s (5 minutes)            |                                       |\n       |                                     |---------------------------------------->|                                       |\n       | 4. 302 Redirect to Google Auth URL  |                                         |                                       |\n       |    (?client_id=...&state=<state>    |                                         |                                       |\n       |     &code_challenge=...&nonce=...)  |                                         |                                       |\n       |<------------------------------------|                                         |                                       |\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 5. User signs in on accounts.google.com and grants consent                                                            |\n       |---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 6. 302 Redirect with ?code=AUTH_CODE&state=<state>                                                                    |\n       |<----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 7. GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback?code=AUTH_CODE&state=<state>                                                      |\n       |------------------------------------>|                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 8. ATOMIC GETDEL `pg:oauth:state:<state>`                                       |\n       |                                     |    (Consumes state immediately; prevents any second attempt)                    |\n       |                                     |---------------------------------------->|                                       |\n       |                                     |<----------------------------------------|                                       |\n       |                                     |    If null or expired -> 400 Bad Request (OAUTH_STATE_INVALID_OR_EXPIRED)     |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 9. POST https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token                                     |\n       |                                     |    { code, client_id, client_secret, code_verifier, redirect_uri }              |\n       |                                     |-------------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                     |<--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                     |    Returns { id_token, access_token }                                           |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 10. OIDC ID Token Cryptographic Verification:                                   |\n       |                                     |     a. Match header `kid` against cached Google Discovery JWKS keys             |\n       |                                     |     b. Verify RS256 signature                                                   |\n       |                                     |     c. Assert `iss` in [\'https://accounts.google.com\', \'accounts.google.com\']   |\n       |                                     |     d. Assert `aud` === GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID                                        |\n       |                                     |     e. Assert `exp` > now() and `iat` <= now()                                  |\n       |                                     |     f. Assert `nonce` === stored_nonce                                          |\n       |                                     |     g. Assert `email_verified` === true                                         |\n       |                                     |     h. Extract stable provider `sub` and `email`                                |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 11. Transactional SQL Identity Resolution:                                      |\n       |                                     |     a. SELECT * FROM authentication_accounts WHERE provider=\'google\' AND sub;   |\n       |                                     |     b. If exists -> Authenticate existing user                                  |\n       |                                     |     c. If NOT exists -> Check if `users` table already has that email:          |\n       |                                     |        * If exists -> REJECT 409 Conflict (Anti-Account-Takeover Rule)           |\n       |                                     |        * If not exists -> INSERT INTO `users` + INSERT INTO `auth_accounts`     |\n       |                                     |     d. INSERT INTO `user_sessions` with new `family_id` and hashed token        |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 12. Issue:                                                                      |\n       |                                     |     - Access Token (RS256 JWT, 15m)                                             |\n       |                                     |     - Refresh Token (CSPRNG 256-bit Hex, stored only as SHA-256)                |\n       |                                     |     - CSRF Token (CSPRNG 256-bit Hex)                                           |\n       | 13. Set Cookies:                    |                                                                                 |\n       |     - pg_access_token (HttpOnly)    |                                                                                 |\n       |     - pg_refresh_token (HttpOnly)   |                                                                                 |\n       |     - pg_csrf_token (JS-readable)   |                                                                                 |\n       |     302 Redirect to Dashboard (/)   |                                                                                 |\n       |<------------------------------------|                                                                                 |\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Final Refresh-Token Lifecycle & Concurrency Model\n\n### Approach Selected: **Approach A \342\200\224 Strict Atomic Rotation with Client Mutex Protection**\n\nNo plaintext refresh tokens are ever stored or cached in the database or Redis. Refresh tokens are stored exclusively as `SHA-256(raw_refresh_token)` hashes.\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. NORMAL ATOMIC ROTATION (`POST /api/v1/auth/refresh`)                                                              |\n|                                                                                                                      |\n|   1. Client sends request with `pg_refresh_token` (Cookie or Bearer header).                                         |\n|   2. Backend computes incoming_hash = SHA-256(raw_refresh_token).                                                    |\n|   3. Database Transaction (SERIALIZABLE / ROW-LOCK):                                                                 |\n|      SELECT * FROM user_sessions WHERE refresh_token_hash = incoming_hash FOR UPDATE;                                |\n|   4. If record found and `revoked_at IS NULL` and `expires_at > NOW()`:                                              |\n|      a. Generate new raw_token_next. Compute new_hash = SHA-256(raw_token_next).                                     |\n|      b. Record old token in history:                                                                                 |\n|         INSERT INTO session_token_history (session_id, family_id, token_hash, rotated_at) VALUES (...);              |\n|      c. Update session record:                                                                                       |\n|         UPDATE user_sessions SET refresh_token_hash = new_hash, last_used_at = NOW() WHERE id = session.id;          |\n|      d. Commit Transaction.                                                                                          |\n|   5. Mint new RS256 JWT Access Token (15m).                                                                          |\n|   6. Issue new raw_token_next to client.                                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 2. TOKEN REUSE / REPLAY DETECTION                                                                                    |\n|                                                                                                                      |\n|   1. Client presents raw_token_replayed. Compute hash = SHA-256(raw_token_replayed).                                  |\n|   2. Query finds NO active session with that hash in `user_sessions`.                                                |\n|   3. Query `session_token_history WHERE token_hash = hash`:                                                          |\n|      -> Record Found! This token was previously rotated in Family `F_ID`.                                            |\n|   4. REPLAY ATTACK TRIGGERED:                                                                                        |\n|      a. UPDATE user_sessions SET revoked_at = NOW() WHERE family_id = F_ID; (Revokes entire family)                  |\n|      b. INSERT INTO audit_logs { action: \'TOKEN_REPLAY_DETECTED\', family_id: F_ID, metadata: {...} };                |\n|      c. Clear cookies & return 401 Unauthorized (TOKEN_FAMILY_REVOKED).                                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 3. CONCURRENT REFRESH HANDLING (Client-Side Mutex & Backend 401 Behavior)                                            |\n|                                                                                                                      |\n|   - Frontend API client implements a Promise-based in-flight Mutex (`isRefreshing` lock):                             |\n|     * If multiple API requests encounter a 401 simultaneously, only ONE refresh request is dispatched.               |\n|     * All other pending requests await the resolution of that single refresh Promise before retrying with the new    |\n|       access token.                                                                                                  |\n|   - If an uncoordinated second request uses the already-consumed token before receiving the new one, the backend    |\n|     detects the rotated token and revokes the family. The client then redirects cleanly to login.                    |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Final Cookie & CSRF Security Model (No `COOKIE_SECRET`)\n\nBecause OAuth transaction state is held entirely in Redis and session tokens are signed JWTs or high-entropy CSPRNG values, **no `COOKIE_SECRET` is required**. It has been completely eliminated from the architecture.\n\n### Cookie Configuration:\n\n| Cookie Name | Path Scope | Attributes | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `pg_access_token` | `/` | `HttpOnly; Secure in prod; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=900` | 15-minute RS256 stateless access token. |\n| `pg_refresh_token`| `/api/v1/auth` | `HttpOnly; Secure in prod; SameSite=Strict; Max-Age=2592000` | 30-day rotated refresh token (isolated to auth path). |\n| `pg_csrf_token`   | `/` | `Secure in prod; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=2592000` *(JS Accessible)* | High-entropy CSPRNG Double-Submit CSRF token. |\n\n### CSRF Protection Standard:\n- **Double-Submit Pattern**: On state-changing requests (`POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, `PATCH`), the client must read `pg_csrf_token` and supply it in the `X-CSRF-Token` header.\n- **Server Validation**: The CSRF middleware verifies that `req.headers[\'x-csrf-token\'] === req.cookies[\'pg_csrf_token\']` using constant-time string comparison (`crypto.timingSafeEqual`).\n- Safe idempotent methods (`GET`, `HEAD`, `OPTIONS`) are exempt from CSRF checks.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Native Desktop OAuth Preparation (RFC 8252)\n\nPhase 2 prepares the exact native OAuth contract required by the future Tauri client (Phase 5) without modifying backend logic later:\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| FUTURE DESKTOP CLIENT (Tauri)                                                                      |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| 1. Starts local ephemeral TCP loopback server: `http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>/callback`           |\n| 2. Generates PKCE `code_verifier` and `code_challenge = S256(verifier)`.                           |\n| 3. Opens system default browser to Google OAuth:                                                   |\n|    https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?                                                   |\n|      client_id=GOOGLE_DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID&                                                           |\n|      response_type=code&                                                                           |\n|      scope=openid%20email%20profile&                                                               |\n|      redirect_uri=http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>/callback&                                         |\n|      code_challenge=<challenge>&code_challenge_method=S256&state=<state>                           |\n| 4. User signs in on Google; Google redirects to loopback `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback?code=..`|\n| 5. Desktop captures `code` and closes loopback socket.                                             |\n| 6. Desktop calls Backend API:                                                                      |\n|    POST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange                                                       |\n|    { \"code\": \"...\", \"code_verifier\": \"...\", \"redirect_uri\": \"http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback\" }  |\n+---------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                  |\n                                  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| BACKEND API (`POST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange`)                                          |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| 1. Validates `redirect_uri` format starts strictly with `http://127.0.0.1:` (or `http://[::1]:`).  |\n| 2. Exchanges `code` + `code_verifier` with Google via HTTPS using `GOOGLE_DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID`.       |\n| 3. Validates OIDC ID token claims (`iss`, `aud == GOOGLE_DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID`, `email_verified`).     |\n| 4. Resolves user identity via `(provider, sub)`.                                                   |\n| 5. Creates session record in PostgreSQL.                                                           |\n| 6. Returns JSON:                                                                                   |\n|    {                                                                                               |\n|      \"success\": true,                                                                              |\n|      \"data\": {                                                                                     |\n|        \"access_token\": \"ey...\",                                                                    |\n|        \"refresh_token\": \"d8f0...\",                                                                 |\n|        \"user\": { \"id\": \"...\", \"email\": \"...\", \"role\": \"...\" }                                      |\n|      }                                                                                             |\n|    }                                                                                               |\n|    (No cookies set for native desktop clients).                                                    |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. JWT RS256 Key Management & Lifecycle\n\n1. **Production Mode (`NODE_ENV=production`)**:\n   - `JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM` and `JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM` **MUST** be explicitly provided via environment variables / secret manager.\n   - If missing in production, the server crashes immediately at boot with a fatal configuration error (`FATAL: JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM is required in production`).\n2. **Development Mode (`NODE_ENV=development`)**:\n   - If keys are not supplied in `.env`, the server generates an in-memory 2048-bit RSA keypair at startup and logs a prominent warning:\n     `\342\232\240\357\270\217 [DEV ONLY] Generated ephemeral RS256 keypair (Kid: pg-dev-ephemeral). All sessions will invalidate on server restart.`\n3. **Key Rotation Support**:\n   - Every minted JWT includes a `kid` header (e.g. `pg-auth-2026-v1`).\n   - The token verification service supports a key map `{ [kid: string]: string }`, allowing zero-downtime key rotation by retaining old public keys during transition windows.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Session Revocation vs. Access Token Expiration\n\n| Token Type | Lifespan | Revocation Mechanics | Effective Window |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Refresh Token** | 30 Days | **Instantaneous**: `revoked_at = NOW()` in PostgreSQL or deleted from database. Any subsequent refresh call is immediately rejected. | Immediate (0ms) |\n| **Access Token** | 15 Minutes | **Stateless Cryptographic Validity**: Valid until `exp` timestamp is reached (max 15 minutes). Once expired, client must refresh, at which point revoked sessions are blocked. | Up to 15 minutes |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Logging & Redaction Policy (Zero Secret Leakage)\n\nThe structured request logging middleware and all error formatters enforce a strict blocklist. The following fields are **NEVER logged to stdout, stderr, files, or audit tables**:\n\n- `code` (OAuth Authorization Codes)\n- `state` (OAuth transaction state)\n- `nonce` (OIDC nonce)\n- `code_verifier` / `code_challenge` (PKCE parameters)\n- `id_token` / `access_token` (Google & App JWTs)\n- `refresh_token` (Raw refresh tokens)\n- `X-CSRF-Token` / `pg_csrf_token`\n- `JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM` / `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`\n\n---\n\n## 8. Database Migration Schema (`002_auth_token_families.sql`)\n\n```sql\n-- ============================================================================\n-- PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 MIGRATION 002: AUTH TOKEN FAMILIES & IP REMOVAL\n-- Phase 2 Foundation: Token Family Rotation, Replay Detection & IP Minimization\n-- ============================================================================\n\n-- 1. Add family_id and last_used_at to user_sessions\nALTER TABLE user_sessions \n    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS family_id UUID DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4() NOT NULL,\n    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS last_used_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL;\n\n-- 2. Drop client_ip_anonymized to uphold complete IP minimization mandate\nALTER TABLE user_sessions \n    DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS client_ip_anonymized;\n\n-- 3. Token rotation history table for detecting replayed/stolen refresh tokens\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS session_token_history (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    session_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES user_sessions(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    family_id UUID NOT NULL,\n    token_hash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,\n    rotated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 4. Optimized indexes\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_user_sessions_family ON user_sessions(family_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_user_sessions_active_hash ON user_sessions(refresh_token_hash) WHERE revoked_at IS NULL;\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_token_history_hash ON session_token_history(token_hash);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Final Phase 2 API Endpoints\n\n| Method | Path | Description | Authentication & Security |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/start` | Generates PKCE + Redis state; returns 302 to Google | Public; Rate limited (20 req/min) |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` | Atomic `GETDEL` state consumption; OIDC validation; issues session cookies | Public; Single-use state verification |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange` | Native desktop PKCE code exchange returning JSON | Public; Native loopback URI check |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/refresh` | Atomically rotates refresh token in family; issues new 15m JWT | Refresh Cookie/Bearer; Replay detection |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/logout` | Revokes current session family; clears cookies | Double-Submit CSRF Check |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/me` | Returns current user profile, role, and active session count | RS256 JWT Bearer / Cookie |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions` | Lists user\'s active sessions | RS256 JWT Bearer; Scoped to current user |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions/:id` | Revokes a specific session by ID | RS256 JWT Bearer + CSRF Check |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/admin/users` | Lists users (Admin-only RBAC assertion endpoint) | Strict `requireRole([\'admin\'])` middleware |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Final Environment Variables Template (`.env.example`)\n\n```env\n# ============================================================================\n# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 ENVIRONMENT CONFIGURATION (PHASE 2)\n# ============================================================================\n\n# Service Runtime\nNODE_ENV=development\nPORT=3000\nHOST=0.0.0.0\n\n# Database & Redis Configuration\nPOSTGRES_HOST=localhost\nPOSTGRES_PORT=5432\nPOSTGRES_DB=privacy_gateway\nPOSTGRES_USER=pgadmin\nPOSTGRES_PASSWORD=pgsecret_change_in_production\n\nREDIS_HOST=localhost\nREDIS_PORT=6379\nREDIS_PASSWORD=redissecret_change_in_production\n\n# Google OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect (Development Credentials)\n# Web Client:\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_dev_web_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_dev_web_client_secret\nGOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback\n\n# Native Desktop Client (For Phase 5 Preparation):\nGOOGLE_DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID=your_dev_desktop_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com\n\n# Asymmetric RS256 JWT Configuration\n# In production, supply 2048-bit or 4096-bit RSA PEM strings.\n# In development, leave blank to use auto-generated ephemeral keypair.\nJWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=\nJWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM=\nJWT_KEY_ID=pg-auth-2026-v1\nJWT_ISSUER=https://api.privacygateway.net\nJWT_AUDIENCE=https://privacygateway.net\n\n# CORS & Security Limits\nCORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000\nRATE_LIMIT_MAX=100\nRATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Final Automated Test Plan\n\nAutomated tests will be created in `apps/api/tests/auth.test.ts` and `apps/api/tests/oidc.test.ts` using Node.js native test runner (`node:test` + `node:assert`):\n\n1. **OAuth State & Redis Invalidation**:\n   - `start` endpoint sets Redis key with 300s TTL.\n   - Callback consumes state via `GETDEL`; second attempt with identical state returns 400 Bad Request.\n   - Callback with missing or malformed state returns 400 Bad Request.\n2. **OIDC Token Validation Unit Tests**:\n   - Valid token with matching mock JWKS RS256 signature passes.\n   - Rejection of invalid signature.\n   - Rejection of mismatched `iss` (`iss !== \'https://accounts.google.com\'`).\n   - Rejection of mismatched `aud` (`aud !== GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`).\n   - Rejection of expired token (`exp < now`).\n   - Rejection of token with `email_verified === false`.\n   - Rejection of token with mismatched `nonce`.\n3. **Identity & Account Linking Protection**:\n   - First-time Google user creates record in `users` and `authentication_accounts`.\n   - Existing Google identity logins re-authenticate user without creating duplicate records.\n   - Collision test: User exists with email `victim@example.com`, but new Google login has different `sub` \342\206\222 Rejects with 409 Conflict (prevents takeover).\n4. **Token Family & Rotation Integration Tests**:\n   - Successful refresh generates new refresh token and updates `session_token_history`.\n   - Replay test: Attempting to refresh with an old, already-rotated token revokes all sessions in that family.\n   - Concurrency test: Rapid sequential refresh attempts with an old token trigger replay revocation.\n5. **RBAC & Authorization Boundary Tests**:\n   - Unauthenticated request to `/api/v1/auth/me` returns 401.\n   - User with `role: \'user\'` accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` returns 403 Forbidden.\n   - User with `role: \'admin\'` accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` returns 200 OK.\n6. **CSRF Protection Tests**:\n   - `POST /api/v1/auth/logout` without `X-CSRF-Token` returns 403 Forbidden.\n   - `POST /api/v1/auth/logout` with matching `X-CSRF-Token` succeeds and revokes session.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Exact Files to Create & Modify\n\n```\napps/api/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 env.ts                     # Add RS256 keys, Google credentials, remove COOKIE_SECRET\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 keys.ts                    # Dynamic RS256 keypair loader (with dev fallback generator)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oauth.ts                   # Google discovery document fetcher & JWKS cache manager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.controller.ts         # startGoogleAuth, handleGoogleCallback, desktopExchange, refreshSession, logout, getMe, listSessions, revokeSession\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.controller.ts        # listAllUsers (RBAC test controller)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.middleware.ts         # requireAuth (RS256 JWT verification), requireRole, optionalAuth\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 csrf.middleware.ts         # Double-Submit CSRF validator for cookie mutations\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.routes.ts             # REST router for /api/v1/auth/*\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.routes.ts            # REST router for /api/v1/admin/*\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.service.ts            # Google token exchange, sub-based identity linking, session family manager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 token.service.ts           # RS256 JWT minting/verifying & CSPRNG refresh token hashing\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oidc.service.ts            # JWKS signature verifier & claim assertions\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 app.ts                         # Register cookie-parser, auth routes, and admin routes\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n    \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.test.ts                   # Integration tests: OAuth state, callback, refresh, replay, logout, RBAC\n    \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oidc.test.ts                   # Unit tests: JWKS verification, issuer/aud/exp/nonce claim assertions\n\ninfrastructure/migrations/\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 002_auth_token_families.sql        # Migration for token family tracking and IP removal\n\npackages/shared-types/src/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api.ts                             # Auth DTOs (UserProfileDto, AuthResponseDto, SessionItemDto)\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.ts\n\nsrc/App.tsx\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Update React Dashboard with:\n    - Real \"Sign in with Google\" button & authentication status bar\n    - Authenticated profile banner (Email, Role, User ID, Session count)\n    - Interactive Session Manager (List active sessions, revoke specific session)\n    - Role-Based Access Control tester (Execute admin probe and display 200 vs 403 outcome)\n    - Interactive Logout action\n```\n\n---\n\n### Request for Final Phase 2.2 Approval\n\nThis finalized **Phase 2.2 Specification** incorporates every required security correction:\n1. Strict atomic refresh rotation (Approach A) with client mutex and zero plaintext refresh token storage.\n2. Complete removal of `COOKIE_SECRET` in favor of pure Redis state and Double-Submit CSRF protection.\n3. Clean separation of Web vs. RFC 8252 Native Desktop OAuth without embedded client secrets.\n4. RS256 asymmetric signing with explicit development ephemeral fallback and production enforcement.\n5. Accurate documentation of immediate refresh revocation vs. 15-minute access token lifespan.\n6. Zero logging of sensitive tokens, credentials, or parameters.\n\nPlease provide your approval to begin implementing Phase 2.2."
    modified_response: "# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 PHASE 2.2: FINAL AUTHENTICATION & OIDC SECURITY SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## 1. Final OAuth / OIDC Flow Diagram (Zero Cookie State, Redis-Backed)\n\n```\n========================================================================================================================\n                                 PHASE 2.2: AUTHORIZATION CODE FLOW WITH PKCE & REDIS\n========================================================================================================================\n\nBrowser / Client                  Privacy Gateway Backend                     Redis In-Memory Bus              Google OIDC (accounts.google.com)\n       |                                     |                                         |                                       |\n       | 1. GET /api/v1/auth/google/start    |                                         |                                       |\n       |------------------------------------>|                                         |                                       |\n       |                                     | 2. Generate CSPRNG values:              |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - state (32-byte hex)                |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - nonce (32-byte hex)                |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - code_verifier (32-byte hex)        |                                       |\n       |                                     |    - code_challenge = S256(verifier)    |                                       |\n       |                                     |                                         |                                       |\n       |                                     | 3. SET `pg:oauth:state:<state>`         |                                       |\n       |                                     |    { nonce, verifier, return_to }       |                                       |\n       |                                     |    EXPIRE = 300s (5 minutes)            |                                       |\n       |                                     |---------------------------------------->|                                       |\n       | 4. 302 Redirect to Google Auth URL  |                                         |                                       |\n       |    (?client_id=...&state=<state>    |                                         |                                       |\n       |     &code_challenge=...&nonce=...)  |                                         |                                       |\n       |<------------------------------------|                                         |                                       |\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 5. User signs in on accounts.google.com and grants consent                                                            |\n       |---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 6. 302 Redirect with ?code=AUTH_CODE&state=<state>                                                                    |\n       |<----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                                                                                                       |\n       | 7. GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback?code=AUTH_CODE&state=<state>                                                      |\n       |------------------------------------>|                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 8. ATOMIC GETDEL `pg:oauth:state:<state>`                                       |\n       |                                     |    (Consumes state immediately; prevents any second attempt)                    |\n       |                                     |---------------------------------------->|                                       |\n       |                                     |<----------------------------------------|                                       |\n       |                                     |    If null or expired -> 400 Bad Request (OAUTH_STATE_INVALID_OR_EXPIRED)     |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 9. POST https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token                                     |\n       |                                     |    { code, client_id, client_secret, code_verifier, redirect_uri }              |\n       |                                     |-------------------------------------------------------------------------------->|\n       |                                     |<--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n       |                                     |    Returns { id_token, access_token }                                           |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 10. OIDC ID Token Cryptographic Verification:                                   |\n       |                                     |     a. Match header `kid` against cached Google Discovery JWKS keys             |\n       |                                     |     b. Verify RS256 signature                                                   |\n       |                                     |     c. Assert `iss` in [\'https://accounts.google.com\', \'accounts.google.com\']   |\n       |                                     |     d. Assert `aud` === GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID                                        |\n       |                                     |     e. Assert `exp` > now() and `iat` <= now()                                  |\n       |                                     |     f. Assert `nonce` === stored_nonce                                          |\n       |                                     |     g. Assert `email_verified` === true                                         |\n       |                                     |     h. Extract stable provider `sub` and `email`                                |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 11. Transactional SQL Identity Resolution:                                      |\n       |                                     |     a. SELECT * FROM authentication_accounts WHERE provider=\'google\' AND sub;   |\n       |                                     |     b. If exists -> Authenticate existing user                                  |\n       |                                     |     c. If NOT exists -> Check if `users` table already has that email:          |\n       |                                     |        * If exists -> REJECT 409 Conflict (Anti-Account-Takeover Rule)           |\n       |                                     |        * If not exists -> INSERT INTO `users` + INSERT INTO `auth_accounts`     |\n       |                                     |     d. INSERT INTO `user_sessions` with new `family_id` and hashed token        |\n       |                                     |                                                                                 |\n       |                                     | 12. Issue:                                                                      |\n       |                                     |     - Access Token (RS256 JWT, 15m)                                             |\n       |                                     |     - Refresh Token (CSPRNG 256-bit Hex, stored only as SHA-256)                |\n       |                                     |     - CSRF Token (CSPRNG 256-bit Hex)                                           |\n       | 13. Set Cookies:                    |                                                                                 |\n       |     - pg_access_token (HttpOnly)    |                                                                                 |\n       |     - pg_refresh_token (HttpOnly)   |                                                                                 |\n       |     - pg_csrf_token (JS-readable)   |                                                                                 |\n       |     302 Redirect to Dashboard (/)   |                                                                                 |\n       |<------------------------------------|                                                                                 |\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Final Refresh-Token Lifecycle & Concurrency Model\n\n### Approach Selected: **Approach A \342\200\224 Strict Atomic Rotation with Client Mutex Protection**\n\nNo plaintext refresh tokens are ever stored or cached in the database or Redis. Refresh tokens are stored exclusively as `SHA-256(raw_refresh_token)` hashes.\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. NORMAL ATOMIC ROTATION (`POST /api/v1/auth/refresh`)                                                              |\n|                                                                                                                      |\n|   1. Client sends request with `pg_refresh_token` (Cookie or Bearer header).                                         |\n|   2. Backend computes incoming_hash = SHA-256(raw_refresh_token).                                                    |\n|   3. Database Transaction (SERIALIZABLE / ROW-LOCK):                                                                 |\n|      SELECT * FROM user_sessions WHERE refresh_token_hash = incoming_hash FOR UPDATE;                                |\n|   4. If record found and `revoked_at IS NULL` and `expires_at > NOW()`:                                              |\n|      a. Generate new raw_token_next. Compute new_hash = SHA-256(raw_token_next).                                     |\n|      b. Record old token in history:                                                                                 |\n|         INSERT INTO session_token_history (session_id, family_id, token_hash, rotated_at) VALUES (...);              |\n|      c. Update session record:                                                                                       |\n|         UPDATE user_sessions SET refresh_token_hash = new_hash, last_used_at = NOW() WHERE id = session.id;          |\n|      d. Commit Transaction.                                                                                          |\n|   5. Mint new RS256 JWT Access Token (15m).                                                                          |\n|   6. Issue new raw_token_next to client.                                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 2. TOKEN REUSE / REPLAY DETECTION                                                                                    |\n|                                                                                                                      |\n|   1. Client presents raw_token_replayed. Compute hash = SHA-256(raw_token_replayed).                                  |\n|   2. Query finds NO active session with that hash in `user_sessions`.                                                |\n|   3. Query `session_token_history WHERE token_hash = hash`:                                                          |\n|      -> Record Found! This token was previously rotated in Family `F_ID`.                                            |\n|   4. REPLAY ATTACK TRIGGERED:                                                                                        |\n|      a. UPDATE user_sessions SET revoked_at = NOW() WHERE family_id = F_ID; (Revokes entire family)                  |\n|      b. INSERT INTO audit_logs { action: \'TOKEN_REPLAY_DETECTED\', family_id: F_ID, metadata: {...} };                |\n|      c. Clear cookies & return 401 Unauthorized (TOKEN_FAMILY_REVOKED).                                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 3. CONCURRENT REFRESH HANDLING (Client-Side Mutex & Backend 401 Behavior)                                            |\n|                                                                                                                      |\n|   - Frontend API client implements a Promise-based in-flight Mutex (`isRefreshing` lock):                             |\n|     * If multiple API requests encounter a 401 simultaneously, only ONE refresh request is dispatched.               |\n|     * All other pending requests await the resolution of that single refresh Promise before retrying with the new    |\n|       access token.                                                                                                  |\n|   - If an uncoordinated second request uses the already-consumed token before receiving the new one, the backend    |\n|     detects the rotated token and revokes the family. The client then redirects cleanly to login.                    |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Final Cookie & CSRF Security Model (No `COOKIE_SECRET`)\n\nBecause OAuth transaction state is held entirely in Redis and session tokens are signed JWTs or high-entropy CSPRNG values, **no `COOKIE_SECRET` is required**. It has been completely eliminated from the architecture.\n\n### Cookie Configuration:\n\n| Cookie Name | Path Scope | Attributes | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `pg_access_token` | `/` | `HttpOnly; Secure in prod; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=900` | 15-minute RS256 stateless access token. |\n| `pg_refresh_token`| `/api/v1/auth` | `HttpOnly; Secure in prod; SameSite=Strict; Max-Age=2592000` | 30-day rotated refresh token (isolated to auth path). |\n| `pg_csrf_token`   | `/` | `Secure in prod; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=2592000` *(JS Accessible)* | High-entropy CSPRNG Double-Submit CSRF token. |\n\n### CSRF Protection Standard:\n- **Double-Submit Pattern**: On state-changing requests (`POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, `PATCH`), the client must read `pg_csrf_token` and supply it in the `X-CSRF-Token` header.\n- **Server Validation**: The CSRF middleware verifies that `req.headers[\'x-csrf-token\'] === req.cookies[\'pg_csrf_token\']` using constant-time string comparison (`crypto.timingSafeEqual`).\n- Safe idempotent methods (`GET`, `HEAD`, `OPTIONS`) are exempt from CSRF checks.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Native Desktop OAuth Preparation (RFC 8252)\n\nPhase 2 prepares the exact native OAuth contract required by the future Tauri client (Phase 5) without modifying backend logic later:\n\n```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| FUTURE DESKTOP CLIENT (Tauri)                                                                      |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| 1. Starts local ephemeral TCP loopback server: `http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>/callback`           |\n| 2. Generates PKCE `code_verifier` and `code_challenge = S256(verifier)`.                           |\n| 3. Opens system default browser to Google OAuth:                                                   |\n|    https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?                                                   |\n|      client_id=GOOGLE_DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID&                                                           |\n|      response_type=code&                                                                           |\n|      scope=openid%20email%20profile&                                                               |\n|      redirect_uri=http://127.0.0.1:<random_port>/callback&                                         |\n|      code_challenge=<challenge>&code_challenge_method=S256&state=<state>                           |\n| 4. User signs in on Google; Google redirects to loopback `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback?code=..`|\n| 5. Desktop captures `code` and closes loopback socket.                                             |\n| 6. Desktop calls Backend API:                                                                      |\n|    POST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange                                                       |\n|    { \"code\": \"...\", \"code_verifier\": \"...\", \"redirect_uri\": \"http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback\" }  |\n+---------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                  |\n                                  v\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| BACKEND API (`POST /api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange`)                                          |\n|                                                                                                    |\n| 1. Validates `redirect_uri` format starts strictly with `http://127.0.0.1:` (or `http://[::1]:`).  |\n| 2. Exchanges `code` + `code_verifier` with Google via HTTPS using `GOOGLE_DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID`.       |\n| 3. Validates OIDC ID token claims (`iss`, `aud == GOOGLE_DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID`, `email_verified`).     |\n| 4. Resolves user identity via `(provider, sub)`.                                                   |\n| 5. Creates session record in PostgreSQL.                                                           |\n| 6. Returns JSON:                                                                                   |\n|    {                                                                                               |\n|      \"success\": true,                                                                              |\n|      \"data\": {                                                                                     |\n|        \"access_token\": \"ey...\",                                                                    |\n|        \"refresh_token\": \"d8f0...\",                                                                 |\n|        \"user\": { \"id\": \"...\", \"email\": \"...\", \"role\": \"...\" }                                      |\n|      }                                                                                             |\n|    }                                                                                               |\n|    (No cookies set for native desktop clients).                                                    |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. JWT RS256 Key Management & Lifecycle\n\n1. **Production Mode (`NODE_ENV=production`)**:\n   - `JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM` and `JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM` **MUST** be explicitly provided via environment variables / secret manager.\n   - If missing in production, the server crashes immediately at boot with a fatal configuration error (`FATAL: JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM is required in production`).\n2. **Development Mode (`NODE_ENV=development`)**:\n   - If keys are not supplied in `.env`, the server generates an in-memory 2048-bit RSA keypair at startup and logs a prominent warning:\n     `\342\232\240\357\270\217 [DEV ONLY] Generated ephemeral RS256 keypair (Kid: pg-dev-ephemeral). All sessions will invalidate on server restart.`\n3. **Key Rotation Support**:\n   - Every minted JWT includes a `kid` header (e.g. `pg-auth-2026-v1`).\n   - The token verification service supports a key map `{ [kid: string]: string }`, allowing zero-downtime key rotation by retaining old public keys during transition windows.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Session Revocation vs. Access Token Expiration\n\n| Token Type | Lifespan | Revocation Mechanics | Effective Window |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Refresh Token** | 30 Days | **Instantaneous**: `revoked_at = NOW()` in PostgreSQL or deleted from database. Any subsequent refresh call is immediately rejected. | Immediate (0ms) |\n| **Access Token** | 15 Minutes | **Stateless Cryptographic Validity**: Valid until `exp` timestamp is reached (max 15 minutes). Once expired, client must refresh, at which point revoked sessions are blocked. | Up to 15 minutes |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Logging & Redaction Policy (Zero Secret Leakage)\n\nThe structured request logging middleware and all error formatters enforce a strict blocklist. The following fields are **NEVER logged to stdout, stderr, files, or audit tables**:\n\n- `code` (OAuth Authorization Codes)\n- `state` (OAuth transaction state)\n- `nonce` (OIDC nonce)\n- `code_verifier` / `code_challenge` (PKCE parameters)\n- `id_token` / `access_token` (Google & App JWTs)\n- `refresh_token` (Raw refresh tokens)\n- `X-CSRF-Token` / `pg_csrf_token`\n- `JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM` / `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`\n\n---\n\n## 8. Database Migration Schema (`002_auth_token_families.sql`)\n\n```sql\n-- ============================================================================\n-- PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 MIGRATION 002: AUTH TOKEN FAMILIES & IP REMOVAL\n-- Phase 2 Foundation: Token Family Rotation, Replay Detection & IP Minimization\n-- ============================================================================\n\n-- 1. Add family_id and last_used_at to user_sessions\nALTER TABLE user_sessions \n    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS family_id UUID DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4() NOT NULL,\n    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS last_used_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL;\n\n-- 2. Drop client_ip_anonymized to uphold complete IP minimization mandate\nALTER TABLE user_sessions \n    DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS client_ip_anonymized;\n\n-- 3. Token rotation history table for detecting replayed/stolen refresh tokens\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS session_token_history (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    session_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES user_sessions(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    family_id UUID NOT NULL,\n    token_hash VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,\n    rotated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- 4. Optimized indexes\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_user_sessions_family ON user_sessions(family_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_user_sessions_active_hash ON user_sessions(refresh_token_hash) WHERE revoked_at IS NULL;\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_token_history_hash ON session_token_history(token_hash);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Final Phase 2 API Endpoints\n\n| Method | Path | Description | Authentication & Security |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/start` | Generates PKCE + Redis state; returns 302 to Google | Public; Rate limited (20 req/min) |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/google/callback` | Atomic `GETDEL` state consumption; OIDC validation; issues session cookies | Public; Single-use state verification |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/desktop/google/exchange` | Native desktop PKCE code exchange returning JSON | Public; Native loopback URI check |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/refresh` | Atomically rotates refresh token in family; issues new 15m JWT | Refresh Cookie/Bearer; Replay detection |\n| `POST` | `/api/v1/auth/logout` | Revokes current session family; clears cookies | Double-Submit CSRF Check |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/me` | Returns current user profile, role, and active session count | RS256 JWT Bearer / Cookie |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions` | Lists user\'s active sessions | RS256 JWT Bearer; Scoped to current user |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/auth/sessions/:id` | Revokes a specific session by ID | RS256 JWT Bearer + CSRF Check |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/admin/users` | Lists users (Admin-only RBAC assertion endpoint) | Strict `requireRole([\'admin\'])` middleware |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Final Environment Variables Template (`.env.example`)\n\n```env\n# ============================================================================\n# PRIVACY GATEWAY \342\200\224 ENVIRONMENT CONFIGURATION (PHASE 2)\n# ============================================================================\n\n# Service Runtime\nNODE_ENV=development\nPORT=3000\nHOST=0.0.0.0\n\n# Database & Redis Configuration\nPOSTGRES_HOST=localhost\nPOSTGRES_PORT=5432\nPOSTGRES_DB=privacy_gateway\nPOSTGRES_USER=pgadmin\nPOSTGRES_PASSWORD=pgsecret_change_in_production\n\nREDIS_HOST=localhost\nREDIS_PORT=6379\nREDIS_PASSWORD=redissecret_change_in_production\n\n# Google OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect (Development Credentials)\n# Web Client:\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_dev_web_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_dev_web_client_secret\nGOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback\n\n# Native Desktop Client (For Phase 5 Preparation):\nGOOGLE_DESKTOP_CLIENT_ID=your_dev_desktop_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com\n\n# Asymmetric RS256 JWT Configuration\n# In production, supply 2048-bit or 4096-bit RSA PEM strings.\n# In development, leave blank to use auto-generated ephemeral keypair.\nJWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=\nJWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM=\nJWT_KEY_ID=pg-auth-2026-v1\nJWT_ISSUER=https://api.privacygateway.net\nJWT_AUDIENCE=https://privacygateway.net\n\n# CORS & Security Limits\nCORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000\nRATE_LIMIT_MAX=100\nRATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Final Automated Test Plan\n\nAutomated tests will be created in `apps/api/tests/auth.test.ts` and `apps/api/tests/oidc.test.ts` using Node.js native test runner (`node:test` + `node:assert`):\n\n1. **OAuth State & Redis Invalidation**:\n   - `start` endpoint sets Redis key with 300s TTL.\n   - Callback consumes state via `GETDEL`; second attempt with identical state returns 400 Bad Request.\n   - Callback with missing or malformed state returns 400 Bad Request.\n2. **OIDC Token Validation Unit Tests**:\n   - Valid token with matching mock JWKS RS256 signature passes.\n   - Rejection of invalid signature.\n   - Rejection of mismatched `iss` (`iss !== \'https://accounts.google.com\'`).\n   - Rejection of mismatched `aud` (`aud !== GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`).\n   - Rejection of expired token (`exp < now`).\n   - Rejection of token with `email_verified === false`.\n   - Rejection of token with mismatched `nonce`.\n3. **Identity & Account Linking Protection**:\n   - First-time Google user creates record in `users` and `authentication_accounts`.\n   - Existing Google identity logins re-authenticate user without creating duplicate records.\n   - Collision test: User exists with email `victim@example.com`, but new Google login has different `sub` \342\206\222 Rejects with 409 Conflict (prevents takeover).\n4. **Token Family & Rotation Integration Tests**:\n   - Successful refresh generates new refresh token and updates `session_token_history`.\n   - Replay test: Attempting to refresh with an old, already-rotated token revokes all sessions in that family.\n   - Concurrency test: Rapid sequential refresh attempts with an old token trigger replay revocation.\n5. **RBAC & Authorization Boundary Tests**:\n   - Unauthenticated request to `/api/v1/auth/me` returns 401.\n   - User with `role: \'user\'` accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` returns 403 Forbidden.\n   - User with `role: \'admin\'` accessing `/api/v1/admin/users` returns 200 OK.\n6. **CSRF Protection Tests**:\n   - `POST /api/v1/auth/logout` without `X-CSRF-Token` returns 403 Forbidden.\n   - `POST /api/v1/auth/logout` with matching `X-CSRF-Token` succeeds and revokes session.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Exact Files to Create & Modify\n\n```\napps/api/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 env.ts                     # Add RS256 keys, Google credentials, remove COOKIE_SECRET\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 keys.ts                    # Dynamic RS256 keypair loader (with dev fallback generator)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oauth.ts                   # Google discovery document fetcher & JWKS cache manager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.controller.ts         # startGoogleAuth, handleGoogleCallback, desktopExchange, refreshSession, logout, getMe, listSessions, revokeSession\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.controller.ts        # listAllUsers (RBAC test controller)\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.middleware.ts         # requireAuth (RS256 JWT verification), requireRole, optionalAuth\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 csrf.middleware.ts         # Double-Submit CSRF validator for cookie mutations\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.routes.ts             # REST router for /api/v1/auth/*\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 admin.routes.ts            # REST router for /api/v1/admin/*\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.service.ts            # Google token exchange, sub-based identity linking, session family manager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 token.service.ts           # RS256 JWT minting/verifying & CSPRNG refresh token hashing\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oidc.service.ts            # JWKS signature verifier & claim assertions\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 app.ts                         # Register cookie-parser, auth routes, and admin routes\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n    \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 auth.test.ts                   # Integration tests: OAuth state, callback, refresh, replay, logout, RBAC\n    \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 oidc.test.ts                   # Unit tests: JWKS verification, issuer/aud/exp/nonce claim assertions\n\ninfrastructure/migrations/\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 002_auth_token_families.sql        # Migration for token family tracking and IP removal\n\npackages/shared-types/src/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 api.ts                             # Auth DTOs (UserProfileDto, AuthResponseDto, SessionItemDto)\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.ts\n\nsrc/App.tsx\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Update React Dashboard with:\n    - Real \"Sign in with Google\" button & authentication status bar\n    - Authenticated profile banner (Email, Role, User ID, Session count)\n    - Interactive Session Manager (List active sessions, revoke specific session)\n    - Role-Based Access Control tester (Execute admin probe and display 200 vs 403 outcome)\n    - Interactive Logout action\n```\n\n---\n\n### Request for Final Phase 2.2 Approval\n\nThis finalized **Phase 2.2 Specification** incorporates every required security correction:\n1. Strict atomic refresh rotation (Approach A) with client mutex and zero plaintext refresh token storage.\n2. Complete removal of `COOKIE_SECRET` in favor of pure Redis state and Double-Submit CSRF protection.\n3. Clean separation of Web vs. RFC 8252 Native Desktop OAuth without embedded client secrets.\n4. RS256 asymmetric signing with explicit development ephemeral fallback and production enforcement.\n5. Accurate documentation of immediate refresh revocation vs. 15-minute access token lifespan.\n6. Zero logging of sensitive tokens, credentials, or parameters.\n\nPlease provide your approval to begin implementing Phase 2.2."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
