    user_response: "Google Sign-In is NOT completing successfully.\n\nCurrent behavior:\n\n1. I open the Privacy Gateway dashboard.\n2. I click \"Sign in with Google\".\n3. The page becomes blank for approximately one second.\n4. It returns to the dashboard.\n5. The user remains Anonymous.\n6. No authenticated session appears.\n\nDO NOT implement another authentication system and DO NOT simulate/mock successful Google authentication.\n\nWe need to debug the ACTUAL Phase 2.2 Google OAuth flow end-to-end.\n\nFirst, inspect the existing implementation and identify exactly where the flow is failing.\n\nTrace this complete sequence:\n\nBrowser\n\342\206\222 GET /api/v1/auth/google/start\n\342\206\222 Redis OAuth state creation\n\342\206\222 302 redirect to accounts.google.com\n\342\206\222 Google authorization\n\342\206\222 GET /api/v1/auth/google/callback?code=...&state=...\n\342\206\222 Redis GETDEL\n\342\206\222 Google token endpoint\n\342\206\222 Google ID token verification\n\342\206\222 PostgreSQL identity resolution\n\342\206\222 session creation\n\342\206\222 access/refresh/CSRF cookies\n\342\206\222 redirect back to dashboard\n\342\206\222 GET /api/v1/auth/me\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo not log any secret values.\n\nNever log:\n- authorization code\n- state\n- nonce\n- PKCE verifier\n- access token\n- ID token\n- refresh token\n- client secret\n- CSRF token\n\nInstead log only safe diagnostic information such as:\n\n- OAuth step name\n- endpoint\n- HTTP status\n- error code\n- sanitized error message\n- whether Redis state existed\n- whether Redis GETDEL succeeded\n- whether Google token exchange succeeded/failed\n- OIDC validation failure category\n- database operation success/failure\n- final redirect status\n\nUse request IDs to correlate the entire OAuth transaction.\n\nAdd a safe development-only OAuth diagnostic mode if necessary, but it MUST NOT expose secrets.\n\nCheck all of the following:\n\n1. GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID\n\nVerify that the application is actually loading the configured Google client ID and is NOT using the placeholder value from .env.example.\n\n2. GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET\n\nFor the web-server OAuth flow, verify the actual development client secret is configured and loaded by the backend.\n\n3. GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI\n\nVerify the actual runtime value.\n\nFor local development it should be something like:\n\nhttp://localhost:3000/api/v1/auth/google/callback\n\nBUT do not assume this value.\n\nDetermine the actual URL being sent to Google.\n\n4. Google Cloud OAuth configuration\n\nVerify that the exact redirect URI used by the application is registered under the SAME Google OAuth Web Application client.\n\nDo not silently substitute another client ID.\n\nCheck for:\n\n- scheme mismatch\n- hostname mismatch\n- port mismatch\n- path mismatch\n- trailing slash mismatch\n\n5. Authorized JavaScript origins\n\nDetermine whether the current application origin requires configuration in Google Cloud and verify that the configured origin matches the actual development origin.\n\n6. OAuth consent screen\n\nVerify that the OAuth consent configuration is usable for the current Google account.\n\nIf the application is in testing mode, verify that the Google account being used is an authorized test user where required.\n\n7. Redis OAuth state\n\nImmediately after /google/start:\n\nverify:\n\npg:oauth:state:<state>\n\nexists with the expected TTL.\n\nAt callback:\n\nverify GETDEL consumes it exactly once.\n\nIf state is missing, expired, or already consumed, report:\n\nOAUTH_STATE_INVALID_OR_EXPIRED\n\nDo not silently redirect to the dashboard.\n\n8. Google callback errors\n\nThe callback MUST NOT silently redirect to \"/\".\n\nIf Google returns:\n\n?error=...\n\nhandle it explicitly.\n\nShow a safe user-facing authentication error page/message such as:\n\n\"Google authentication was cancelled or rejected.\"\n\nand log only the sanitized OAuth error code.\n\n9. Google token exchange\n\nVerify that the backend sends:\n\ngrant_type=authorization_code\ncode=<authorization code>\nclient_id=<web client ID>\nclient_secret=<web client secret>\nredirect_uri=<exact same redirect URI>\ncode_verifier=<original PKCE verifier>\n\nDo not log their values.\n\nIf Google returns an error such as:\n\ninvalid_grant\nredirect_uri_mismatch\ninvalid_client\nunauthorized_client\n\nsurface the sanitized error category in development diagnostics instead of silently returning to the dashboard.\n\n10. OIDC verification\n\nVerify:\n\n- JWKS retrieval\n- kid lookup\n- RS256 signature\n- issuer\n- audience\n- exp\n- iat\n- nonce\n- email_verified\n- sub\n\nIf validation fails, report the exact SAFE failure category.\n\n11. PostgreSQL\n\nVerify that the database is reachable and that:\n\nusers\nauthentication_accounts\nuser_sessions\n\ncan be written successfully.\n\n12. Cookies\n\nAfter successful authentication verify that the browser receives:\n\npg_access_token\npg_refresh_token\npg_csrf_token\n\nwith the intended attributes.\n\nIn local development, do NOT accidentally make Secure cookies unusable over HTTP if that prevents the development login from working.\n\nProduction must still require Secure cookies.\n\n13. Frontend\n\nVerify that after callback:\n\nGET /api/v1/auth/me\n\nis actually called.\n\nIf /auth/me returns 401, determine why.\n\nDo not simply reset the UI to Anonymous without showing a diagnostic state in development.\n\n14. Callback redirect\n\nOnly redirect to the dashboard AFTER:\n\n- OAuth state validation\n- Google token exchange\n- OIDC verification\n- identity resolution\n- session creation\n- cookie issuance\n\nhave all succeeded.\n\nIf any step fails, return a visible authentication error instead of silently redirecting to \"/\".\n\n15. Add an OAuth Debug panel to the development dashboard\n\nCreate a development-only panel showing:\n\nOAuth Start: PASS/FAIL\nRedis State: PASS/FAIL\nGoogle Authorization: PASS/FAIL\nCallback Received: PASS/FAIL\nToken Exchange: PASS/FAIL\nOIDC Verification: PASS/FAIL\nDatabase Identity: PASS/FAIL\nSession Creation: PASS/FAIL\nCookies Issued: PASS/FAIL\n/me Authentication: PASS/FAIL\n\nNever display secret/token values.\n\n16. Add integration tests for the actual failure cases:\n\n- missing Google credentials\n- placeholder credentials\n- invalid redirect URI\n- Redis state missing\n- Redis state expired\n- Redis state replay\n- Google callback error\n- token exchange invalid_grant\n- invalid OIDC signature\n- wrong issuer\n- wrong audience\n- nonce mismatch\n- unverified email\n- database unavailable\n- successful authentication\n\n17. Most important:\n\nDo NOT claim \"Google OAuth is working\" unless an actual browser-based Google authentication flow has been successfully completed with real Google OAuth credentials.\n\nUnit tests are NOT sufficient to claim live Google authentication works.\n\nAfter diagnosing the issue, report:\n\nA. Exact root cause\nB. Exact file(s) responsible\nC. Exact configuration required\nD. Code changes made\nE. Tests executed\nF. Whether a REAL browser Google login was successfully completed\n\nDo not proceed to Phase 3 until real Google Sign-In works end-to-end."
