    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Before doing anything else, perform a final security/configuration audit of the current Phase 2.2 implementation.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nI have NOT provided real Google OAuth credentials.\n\nDo NOT ask me to paste credentials into chat.\nDo NOT display any credential values.\nDo NOT create fake or simulated Google authentication.\nDo NOT start Phase 3.\n\n1. GOOGLE CREDENTIAL STATUS\n\nYour previous report said:\n\n\"GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID & GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET: Configured in .env\"\n\nThis is unexpected because I have not provided real credentials.\n\nCheck the current runtime/environment and report ONLY:\n\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID:\n- MISSING\n- PLACEHOLDER\n- REAL VALUE PRESENT\n\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET:\n- MISSING\n- PLACEHOLDER\n- REAL VALUE PRESENT\n\nGOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI:\n- report the URI only\n\nNEVER print the actual client ID or secret if they are real.\n\nIf real credentials somehow exist without me providing them, do not expose them. Explain only that a real value exists and where it is being sourced from.\n\nAlso verify that no credentials are committed to the repository, source files, logs, screenshots, or documentation.\n\n2. AUTHENTICATION DATABASE FALLBACK\n\nYour report mentioned:\n\n\"resilient local storage fallback for container environments.\"\n\nAudit this carefully.\n\nPostgreSQL must be the authoritative persistent store for:\n\n- users\n- authentication_accounts\n- user_sessions\n- session_token_history\n\nIf PostgreSQL is unavailable:\n\n- authentication must fail closed\n- do not silently use local memory/storage as an authentication database\n- do not issue real authenticated sessions from a fallback\n- do not claim persistence succeeded\n\nA development-only diagnostic fallback may exist only if it cannot authenticate users or issue real sessions.\n\nIf the current fallback violates this requirement, remove it.\n\n3. POPUP postMessage SECURITY\n\nAudit the OAuth popup implementation.\n\nVerify:\n\n- message origin is explicitly allowlisted\n- message source must be the expected popup window\n- no \"*\" wildcard origin is used for authentication messages\n- no access token is transmitted through postMessage\n- no refresh token is transmitted through postMessage\n- no ID token is transmitted through postMessage\n- no authorization code is transmitted unnecessarily through postMessage\n- server-side OAuth state validation remains authoritative\n\nIf anything violates these requirements, fix it.\n\n4. OAUTH ERROR HANDLING\n\nVerify that OAuth failures never silently redirect to the normal dashboard.\n\nFailures must result in a visible safe authentication error.\n\nExamples:\n\n- missing credentials\n- access_denied\n- invalid_client\n- invalid_grant\n- redirect_uri_mismatch\n- invalid state\n- expired state\n- nonce mismatch\n- OIDC validation failure\n- database unavailable\n- Redis unavailable\n\nNever expose secrets or OAuth tokens.\n\n5. DEVELOPMENT VS PRODUCTION\n\nClearly separate:\n\nDevelopment:\n- localhost\n- development-only diagnostics\n- optional ephemeral development JWT keys\n\nProduction:\n- HTTPS\n- persistent externally supplied JWT keys\n- real Google OAuth credentials\n- no development diagnostics\n- PostgreSQL authoritative persistence\n- secure cookies\n\nDo not allow development behavior to silently become production behavior.\n\n6. GOOGLE REDIRECT URI\n\nDo not assume the current AI Studio/Cloud Run preview URL is our permanent production URL.\n\nThe application must support a configured GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI.\n\nDo not hardcode a temporary AI Studio preview URL as the production callback.\n\n7. TESTING\n\nRun:\n\nnpm test\nnpm run build\n\nReport the actual results.\n\nDo not claim real Google authentication works.\n\nThe following must remain clearly marked as NOT TESTED until I provide real Google credentials:\n\n- real Google authorization\n- real Google token exchange\n- real browser callback\n- real Google OIDC authentication\n- real production OAuth configuration\n\n8. FINAL RESPONSE\n\nAfter the audit, report exactly:\n\nA. Google credential status\nB. Database fallback status\nC. postMessage security status\nD. OAuth error handling status\nE. Development/production separation status\nF. npm test result\nG. npm run build result\nH. Remaining blockers before real Google login\n\nDo not modify unrelated functionality.\n\nDo not implement WireGuard.\n\nDo not implement privacy scanning.\n\nDo not implement Tauri.\n\nDo not begin Phase 3.\n\nSTOP after this audit and report the results."
    items {
      text: "Before doing anything else, perform a final security/configuration audit of the current Phase 2.2 implementation.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nI have NOT provided real Google OAuth credentials.\n\nDo NOT ask me to paste credentials into chat.\nDo NOT display any credential values.\nDo NOT create fake or simulated Google authentication.\nDo NOT start Phase 3.\n\n1. GOOGLE CREDENTIAL STATUS\n\nYour previous report said:\n\n\"GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID & GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET: Configured in .env\"\n\nThis is unexpected because I have not provided real credentials.\n\nCheck the current runtime/environment and report ONLY:\n\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID:\n- MISSING\n- PLACEHOLDER\n- REAL VALUE PRESENT\n\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET:\n- MISSING\n- PLACEHOLDER\n- REAL VALUE PRESENT\n\nGOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI:\n- report the URI only\n\nNEVER print the actual client ID or secret if they are real.\n\nIf real credentials somehow exist without me providing them, do not expose them. Explain only that a real value exists and where it is being sourced from.\n\nAlso verify that no credentials are committed to the repository, source files, logs, screenshots, or documentation.\n\n2. AUTHENTICATION DATABASE FALLBACK\n\nYour report mentioned:\n\n\"resilient local storage fallback for container environments.\"\n\nAudit this carefully.\n\nPostgreSQL must be the authoritative persistent store for:\n\n- users\n- authentication_accounts\n- user_sessions\n- session_token_history\n\nIf PostgreSQL is unavailable:\n\n- authentication must fail closed\n- do not silently use local memory/storage as an authentication database\n- do not issue real authenticated sessions from a fallback\n- do not claim persistence succeeded\n\nA development-only diagnostic fallback may exist only if it cannot authenticate users or issue real sessions.\n\nIf the current fallback violates this requirement, remove it.\n\n3. POPUP postMessage SECURITY\n\nAudit the OAuth popup implementation.\n\nVerify:\n\n- message origin is explicitly allowlisted\n- message source must be the expected popup window\n- no \"*\" wildcard origin is used for authentication messages\n- no access token is transmitted through postMessage\n- no refresh token is transmitted through postMessage\n- no ID token is transmitted through postMessage\n- no authorization code is transmitted unnecessarily through postMessage\n- server-side OAuth state validation remains authoritative\n\nIf anything violates these requirements, fix it.\n\n4. OAUTH ERROR HANDLING\n\nVerify that OAuth failures never silently redirect to the normal dashboard.\n\nFailures must result in a visible safe authentication error.\n\nExamples:\n\n- missing credentials\n- access_denied\n- invalid_client\n- invalid_grant\n- redirect_uri_mismatch\n- invalid state\n- expired state\n- nonce mismatch\n- OIDC validation failure\n- database unavailable\n- Redis unavailable\n\nNever expose secrets or OAuth tokens.\n\n5. DEVELOPMENT VS PRODUCTION\n\nClearly separate:\n\nDevelopment:\n- localhost\n- development-only diagnostics\n- optional ephemeral development JWT keys\n\nProduction:\n- HTTPS\n- persistent externally supplied JWT keys\n- real Google OAuth credentials\n- no development diagnostics\n- PostgreSQL authoritative persistence\n- secure cookies\n\nDo not allow development behavior to silently become production behavior.\n\n6. GOOGLE REDIRECT URI\n\nDo not assume the current AI Studio/Cloud Run preview URL is our permanent production URL.\n\nThe application must support a configured GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI.\n\nDo not hardcode a temporary AI Studio preview URL as the production callback.\n\n7. TESTING\n\nRun:\n\nnpm test\nnpm run build\n\nReport the actual results.\n\nDo not claim real Google authentication works.\n\nThe following must remain clearly marked as NOT TESTED until I provide real Google credentials:\n\n- real Google authorization\n- real Google token exchange\n- real browser callback\n- real Google OIDC authentication\n- real production OAuth configuration\n\n8. FINAL RESPONSE\n\nAfter the audit, report exactly:\n\nA. Google credential status\nB. Database fallback status\nC. postMessage security status\nD. OAuth error handling status\nE. Development/production separation status\nF. npm test result\nG. npm run build result\nH. Remaining blockers before real Google login\n\nDo not modify unrelated functionality.\n\nDo not implement WireGuard.\n\nDo not implement privacy scanning.\n\nDo not implement Tauri.\n\nDo not begin Phase 3.\n\nSTOP after this audit and report the results."
    }
  }
}
