  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Database Issues**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously examining the database fallback mechanism, specifically in the `auth.service.ts` file. My focus is now zeroed in on how the system behaves in cases of PostgreSQL failure. It\'s concerning that, under these conditions, the service was defaulting to generating real JWTs, and creating in-memory users. I\'m evaluating the security implications, especially concerning potential vulnerabilities. This section is quite critical, and I\'ll keep digging.\n\n\n**Addressing Fallback Mechanisms**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed several instances where memory storage was acting as a fallback for user sessions and authentication data when PostgreSQL was unavailable, specifically within the `rotateSessionToken`, `revokeSession`, `listUserSessions`, and `getUserProfile` functions. This violates the security requirements of our system. I\'m removing all these memory storage fallbacks to ensure a fail-closed approach. The system will now throw a `DATABASE_UNAVAILABLE` error, record diagnostic failures, and not issue any tokens or sessions in the event of a database outage, aligning with the user\'s explicit mandate.\n\n\n**Securing PostMessage Channels**\n\nI\'ve just finished a thorough review of `window.postMessage` usage, finding critical security flaws. All instances used the wildcard `*` for `targetOrigin`, which is now addressed. I\'m calculating the precise target origin, likely the application\'s origin, to ensure secure communication. I\'m now auditing the event listener in `App.tsx` and verifying `event.origin` and `event.source`. I\'m also confirming no sensitive data gets through.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 168180191
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
